INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (57, 'Bucephalandra "Helena Black QQ" (Super Rare, 3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Helena Black QQ" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Helena Black QQ" Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Bucephalandra "Helena Black QQ"</strong> is an elite and highly sought-after variant within the Helena family. Known for its incredibly dark, near-black coloration and compact growth, the "QQ" designation often refers to a specific high-quality leaf texture and intense metallic sheen. When grown under optimal conditions, this plant displays deep violet and midnight-blue iridescent hues that make it a true "black hole" focal point in any premium aquascape.</p>

<p>As a slow-growing rheophyte from Borneo, it requires patience and stability to reach its full aesthetic potential.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Intensity:</strong> Thrives in <strong>medium to high lighting</strong>. While it can survive in low light, higher intensity is required to develop the signature "Black" and "Blue" iridescence.</li>
    <li><strong>Spectrum:</strong> Full-spectrum RGB LED lighting is highly recommended to bring out the dark metallic pigments and the silvery "galaxy" spots (stomata) on the leaf surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Photoperiod:</strong> Limit to 6–8 hours. Because it grows slowly, excessive light without balanced nutrients can lead to Green Spot Algae (GSA) on its dark leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stability:</strong> This is the most critical factor. Sudden shifts in GH, KH, or temperature can trigger "Buce melt."</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers cooler tropical water, ideally <strong>71°F to 78°F (22°C – 26°C)</strong>. Higher temperatures can increase the risk of rhizome rot.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.4. It prefers slightly acidic to neutral water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Strong water circulation is beneficial. It mimics its natural habitat in fast-moving streams and keeps the leaves free from organic waste and biofilm.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Highly recommended. While not strictly "required" for survival, CO2 injection significantly improves growth rates and is essential for achieving the deepest black and purple colors.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> It is a water-column feeder. Regular dosing of liquid fertilizers (NPK + micronutrients) is necessary. Ensure adequate <strong>Iron (Fe)</strong> levels to support its dark pigmentation.</li>
    <li><strong>Bio-Indicator:</strong> Bucephalandra is an excellent indicator of nutrient balance; if new leaves are pale, increase micronutrient dosing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Epiphytic Growth:</strong> Like all Bucephalandra, <strong>do not bury the rhizome</strong> in substrate. Doing so will lead to rot and the death of the plant.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Secure the plant to hardscape elements like lava rock, driftwood, or stones using cyanoacrylate super glue gel or specialized thread.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> Due to its dark color and compact size, it is perfect for the foreground or midground, especially tucked into rock crevices where it can provide high contrast against green mosses.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Texture:</strong> Features small, oval to elliptical leaves with a very dark, velvety texture. The edges are often slightly wavy.</li>
    <li><strong>Iridescence:</strong> Under submerged conditions, the leaves develop a stunning metallic "oil-slick" sheen ranging from dark purple to deep blue.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Extremely slow. It may take months to see significant new growth, making mature clumps very valuable.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> Propagate by cutting the rhizome with a sharp, sterilized blade.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Each cutting should have at least 4–5 healthy leaves and a few roots to ensure a successful transition.</li>
    <li><strong>Lateral Growth:</strong> Trimming the lead growing point often encourages the rhizome to sprout multiple new lateral heads, creating a bushier clump over time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Transition:</strong> When first introduced, it may shed a few leaves (melt). As long as the rhizome remains firm, do not discard it; new submerged-adapted leaves will eventually sprout.</li>
    <li><strong>Algae Control:</strong> Maintain a clean tank with regular water changes. Inhabitants like Amano shrimp and Otocinclus are excellent for keeping the slow-growing leaves free of algae.</li>
    <li><strong>Rarity:</strong> "Helena Black QQ" is a collector-grade plant. It is best introduced into a mature, biologically stable aquarium rather than a brand-new setup.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 650.00, 1300.00, 0.0, false, false, false, true, 5, true, '2026-03-05 11:33:13.21505+00', '2026-03-05 11:33:13.215071+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/HelenaBlackQQ%2Fhelenablackqq002.jpg?alt=media&token=66f5b174-3e3a-4a5d-a3a6-4e8c79e1e2bb', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (56, 'Bucephalandra "Crocodile" (Rare, 3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Crocodile" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Crocodile" Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Bucephalandra "Crocodile"</strong> is a distinctive and rugged variety named for its unique leaf texture and growth pattern. Unlike the smooth or velvety leaves of many other Bucephalandra species, the "Crocodile" variant features highly textured, elongated leaves with prominent midribs and wavy, crinkled margins that resemble the scaly skin of a crocodile. It is a hardy, medium-sized variety that adds a primitive, prehistoric aesthetic to any aquascape.</p>

<p>As a rheophyte from the tropical streams of Borneo, it is built to withstand water flow and provides a tough, structural element to the midground of your tank.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Intensity:</strong> Performs best under <strong>medium lighting</strong>. While it is adaptable to low light, moderate intensity helps define the deep ridges and textures of the leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Spectrum:</strong> Use full-spectrum LED lighting. This highlights the dark green to brownish hues and the subtle silvery "galaxy" spots (stomata) characteristic of the genus.</li>
    <li><strong>Algae Prevention:</strong> Because "Crocodile" is a slow grower, limit the photoperiod to 7–8 hours to prevent hair algae or Green Spot Algae (GSA) from anchoring to the textured leaf surfaces.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stability:</strong> Like all Buce, it demands stable water chemistry. Frequent or drastic changes in parameters can lead to rhizome melt.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers a range of <strong>72°F to 78°F (22°C – 26°C)</strong>. Avoid high temperatures (above 82°F) which can stress the plant and invite rot.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.5. It thrives in slightly acidic to neutral water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Enjoys moderate to strong water flow. This helps deliver nutrients to the leaves and prevents organic debris from settling in the deep "scales" of the foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Not strictly required, but <strong>CO2 injection is highly recommended</strong> to encourage faster growth and more robust leaf development.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> It is a water-column feeder. Regular dosing of liquid fertilizers (Macro and Micro nutrients) is essential. Iron (Fe) supplementation will help maintain its deep, dark coloration.</li>
    <li><strong>Nutrient Balance:</strong> Ensure consistent phosphate levels to help deter Green Spot Algae, which often targets the older, slower-growing leaves of this variety.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Epiphytic Nature:</strong> <strong>Do not bury the rhizome</strong> in the substrate. If the rhizome is covered by soil or sand, it will rot and the plant will perish.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Best grown attached to hardscape. Use super glue gel, fishing line, or cotton thread to secure it to lava rocks, driftwood, or stones.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> Its unique texture makes it an ideal "transition" plant for the midground, bridging the gap between smooth-leaved plants and rough hardscape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Shape:</strong> Narrow, lanceolate (spear-shaped) leaves with a distinct "crinkled" or puckered texture along the surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Coloration:</strong> Typically displays a deep forest green, which can transition into brownish or coppery tones under specific lighting and nutrient conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Slow to moderate. It tends to form a sturdy, creeping rhizome that eventually creates a dense, architectural colony.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Rhizome Division:</strong> Easily propagated by cutting the rhizome with sharp, sterilized scissors.</li>
    <li><strong>Cuttings:</strong> Ensure each cutting has at least 3–4 leaves and a visible portion of the rhizome with some roots attached.</li>
    <li><strong>Recovery:</strong> Once cut, the "mother" plant will often sprout new heads from the nodes near the cut, leading to a bushier appearance over time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Melt Management:</strong> If the plant experiences "melt" upon introduction, remove the decaying leaves but leave the rhizome attached. It will often sprout new, adapted leaves within a few weeks.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleanup Crew:</strong> Amano shrimp and Nerite snails are excellent companions for "Crocodile" as they can navigate the textured leaves and keep them clean of biofilm.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquatic Exotica Tip:</strong> For business displays, placing this variety against light-colored rocks (like Dragon Stone) helps the dark, scaly texture pop.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 450.00, 900.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 10, true, '2026-03-05 11:26:39.125605+00', '2026-03-05 11:33:28.508517+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/crocodile%2Fcrocodile.jpg?alt=media&token=96d41443-7efa-49e9-bfcc-08c17ce4eb63', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (52, 'Seleganella Krussiana (One Plant)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Selaginella kraussiana (Krauss'' Spikemoss) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Selaginella kraussiana (Krauss'' Spikemoss) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Selaginella kraussiana</strong>, commonly known as <strong>Krauss'' Spikemoss</strong>, African Clubmoss, or Spreading Spikemoss, is a lush, mat-forming perennial native to tropical and Southern Africa. Despite its name and moss-like appearance, it is actually a vascular plant related to ferns (a "fern ally"). It is highly valued in the hobby for its delicate, lacy texture and its ability to form a vibrant green carpet in terrariums and shaded gardens.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Prefers <strong>low to medium indirect light</strong> or partial shade. It is naturally adapted to the deep shade of forest floors.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun:</strong> Strictly avoid direct sunlight. The delicate fronds will scorch, turn brown, or become "bleached" very quickly under harsh rays.</li>
    <li><strong>Indoors:</strong> A north- or east-facing window is ideal. In terrariums, standard LED lighting works well as long as the plant isn''t placed too close to the heat of the bulbs.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the soil <strong>consistently moist</strong> at all times. This plant does not have a dormant dry phase and will shrivel rapidly if the soil dries out completely.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Always use <strong>room-temperature water</strong>. Cold water can shock the plant''s system. Like many delicate plants, it prefers distilled, RO, or rainwater to avoid mineral buildup from tap water.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> High humidity (above 60-70%) is essential. It is a perfect candidate for <strong>closed terrariums</strong>, humid bathrooms, or placement near a humidifier.</li>
    <li><strong>Warning:</strong> While it loves moisture, avoid "soggy" or anaerobic soil. Ensure your pot or terrarium has a drainage layer to prevent root rot.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>60°F to 75°F (15°C – 24°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>It prefers cooler, stable tropical conditions. Avoid placing it near heaters, air conditioning vents, or drafty doorways.</li>
    <li>It is frost-tender and will not survive freezing temperatures.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Requires a rich, well-draining soil that retains moisture. A peat-based or coco-coir-based potting mix with added perlite or vermiculite is ideal.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Not a heavy feeder. Apply a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to <strong>half-strength</strong> or quarter-strength once or twice during the growing season (spring and summer).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> A prostrate, creeping groundcover that spreads via trailing stems. It rarely grows taller than 2–4 inches but can spread significantly wide.</li>
    <li><strong>Foliage:</strong> Tiny, scale-like leaves arranged in four rows along the stems, giving it a fern-like appearance.</li>
    <li><strong>Varieties:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><em>''Aurea'' (Golden Spikemoss):</em> Features bright chartreuse or golden-green foliage.</li>
            <li><em>''Brownii'' (Cushion Moss):</em> Forms tight, mounding clumps rather than spreading mats.</li>
            <li><em>''Variegata'' (Frosty Fern):</em> Dark green foliage with distinct white tips.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stem Cuttings:</strong> Extremely easy to propagate. Simply snip a 2–3 inch section of a healthy stem and lay it on moist soil or sphagnum moss.</li>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> Larger mats can be gently pulled apart or cut into sections during repotting. Ensure each division has a portion of the shallow root system attached.</li>
    <li>In high-humidity environments, stems often sprout "rhizophores" (aerial roots) that will anchor themselves as soon as they touch the ground.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Pet Safety:</strong> Non-toxic to dogs, cats, and horses according to the ASPCA. It is safe for bioactive vivariums containing small reptiles or amphibians.</li>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> If it becomes too leggy or outgrows its space, it can be safely trimmed back. This actually encourages denser, bushier growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Troubleshooting:</strong> If the leaves are shriveled and brown, humidity is too low. If the plant turns yellow and mushy, it is likely being overwatered or has poor drainage.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 70.00, 200.00, 5.0, false, false, true, false, 0, true, '2025-12-29 12:25:59.381605+00', '2026-01-18 08:33:25.83874+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/SeleganellaKrussiana%2FSelaginellaKraussiana.jpg?alt=media&token=c09b1dd3-1c83-470e-9304-686a6095879e', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (1, 'Eye Lash Fern', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Eyelash Fern Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Eyelash Fern Care Guide</h1>

  <p>Eyelash ferns are a delicate and visually appealing choice for indoor plant enthusiasts, known for their unique fronds. Here''s a guide to help you keep your Eyelash Fern happy and healthy:</p>

  <h2>1. Lighting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bright, Indirect Light:</strong> Eyelash Ferns thrive in bright, indirect light or dappled shade. Avoid direct sunlight, which can burn their delicate fronds. A location near a window with filtered light or a north-facing windowsill is generally a good option.</li>
    <li><strong>Moderate Indirect Light Indoors:</strong> While ferns are often thought of as shade plants, indoors they need brighter light. Consider placing the plant less than 3 feet from a south-facing window to maximize potential for growth, while avoiding direct sun.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Watering</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consistent Moisture:</strong> Eyelash Ferns are thirsty plants and prefer consistently moist soil, but not waterlogged.</li>
    <li><strong>Check Soil Moisture:</strong> Water regularly, checking the soil every other day or so, and allow the top layer to dry slightly between waterings. Use room temperature water to avoid shocking the plant. Ensure the pot has drainage holes to prevent root rot. If using a terrarium, create a false bottom or drainage layer.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Terracotta Pots:</strong> Terracotta pots wick away moisture too quickly, making it difficult to keep the soil consistently moist.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Humidity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>High Humidity is Key:</strong> Eyelash Ferns need high humidity to flourish, ideally between 50-80%.</li>
    <li><strong>Increase Humidity:</strong> Mist the leaves regularly, especially in dry conditions. Place the pot on a tray of pebbles filled with water. Use a humidifier.</li>
    <li><strong>Browning/Crisping Fronds:</strong> Low humidity can cause the fronds to brown or crisp.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Temperature</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Warm Temperatures:</strong> These ferns prefer warm temperatures between 60-85°F (15-30°C).</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Cold:</strong> They are sensitive to cold temperatures and should be protected from temperatures below 50°F (10°C).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Soil</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Well-Draining & Moisture-Retentive:</strong> Eyelash Ferns thrive in a well-draining, loose, richly organic soil that retains moisture but doesn''t stay soggy.</li>
    <li><strong>Recommended Mix:</strong> A blend of peat moss, perlite, and loam or pine bark works well. Some sources suggest a mix including sphagnum peat moss, compost, vermiculite or perlite, and bark or coconut coir.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Fertilization</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Diluted Liquid Fertilizer:</strong> During the growing season (spring and summer), fertilize monthly with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to half strength.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Over-fertilizing:</strong> Over-fertilizing can damage the roots and foliage.</li>
    <li><strong>Repotting:</strong> Repot every 1-2 years or when the plant outgrows its pot, using fresh potting mix.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Other Considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> Remove any dead or yellowing fronds to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Propagation:</strong> Eyelash Ferns can be propagated by division during the spring.</li>
    <li><strong>Pests:</strong> Watch for mealybugs, spider mites, or aphids.</li>
    <li><strong>Toxicity:</strong> Eyelash Fern (Actiniopteris australis) is non-toxic to pets and humans. However, some sources suggest <em>Actiniopteris semiflabellata</em> might be toxic. It''s always best to prevent ingestion of any plant material by pets or children.</li>
    <li><strong>Terrariums:</strong> Eyelash Ferns are excellent choices for closed terrariums due to their high humidity needs.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>By providing the right conditions, you can enjoy the unique beauty of your Eyelash Fern.</p>

</body>
</html>', 250.00, 500.00, 5.0, true, true, false, true, 0, true, '2025-06-25 15:27:42.191679+00', '2026-01-30 05:07:25.113241+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/EyelashFernPlant.jpeg?alt=media&token=d14f585b-a671-48b2-9ddf-1c64b7f0f942', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/EyelashFernPlant.jpeg?alt=media&token=d14f585b-a671-48b2-9ddf-1c64b7f0f942');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (46, 'Sheet Moss (4 x 4 Inch)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Sheet Moss (Hypnum curvifolium) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Sheet Moss (Hypnum curvifolium) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Sheet Moss</strong>, often belonging to the genus <em>Hypnum</em>, is the quintessential "carpeting" moss. It is a pleurocarpous moss, meaning it grows horizontally in wide, flat sheets rather than upright clumps. In nature, it blankets logs, rocks, and forest floors, creating a seamless emerald carpet. It is the most versatile moss for terrariums, kokedama, and even temporary floral displays.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Sheet Moss is highly adaptable but prefers <strong>low to medium indirect light</strong>.</li>
    <li>It can tolerate slightly more light than Mood Moss, but direct midday sun will bleach it yellow and make it brittle.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium, place it in a spot that mimics a "shaded forest floor." If using artificial lights, 6–8 hours of moderate LED light is sufficient.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> It thrives in 60-80% humidity. It is very happy in closed terrariums but is also one of the few mosses that can survive in open containers if misted daily.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the "sheet" damp to the touch. It acts like a sponge, holding a significant amount of water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> As with all mosses, <strong>distilled, RO, or rainwater</strong> is best. Tap water minerals will eventually build up in the moss fibers and turn it brown from the bottom up.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Substrate and Placement</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> It isn''t picky about soil but prefers something slightly acidic. It grows well on peat moss, bark, or even directly on rocks if they stay moist.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation:</strong> This is the easiest moss to install. Simply take a "sheet" and press it firmly onto a damp surface. Ensure there are no air pockets underneath so the rhizoids can anchor properly.</li>
    <li><strong>Hardscape:</strong> Because it grows in mats, it is the best choice for covering <strong>terrarium backgrounds</strong> or wrapping around <strong>driftwood</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> It spreads outward in all directions, overlapping itself to create a thick, springy carpet.</li>
    <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> It has a classic "mossy" look with small, densely packed green leaves. It stays relatively flat (usually under 1 inch in height).</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Moderate. It is faster than Mood Moss and will eventually fill in gaps between other plants if given enough moisture.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Maintenance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> If your sheet moss gets too large, you can simply tear it into smaller pieces and move them to other areas; they will continue to grow independently.</li>
    <li><strong>Browning:</strong> If the moss turns brown but feels "crispy," it needs more water. If it turns brown/black and feels "mushy," it is likely overwatered or lacks airflow.</li>
    <li><strong>Dormancy:</strong> Sheet moss is incredibly resilient. If it dries out and turns golden-brown, it usually isn''t dead—it''s dormant. A heavy misting will often "wake it up" within hours.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Sheet Moss is the preferred choice for <strong>reptile and amphibian enclosures</strong> because it can withstand a bit of foot traffic (trampling) better than delicate fern moss.</li>
    <li>It is frequently used in <strong>Bonsai</strong> to cover the soil surface, helping to retain moisture for the tree.</li>
    <li>If you are using "preserved" sheet moss from a craft store, keep in mind that it is no longer living and will not grow; living sheet moss should be vibrant green and slightly damp when purchased.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 250.00, 700.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 10, true, '2025-12-21 18:26:04.911511+00', '2026-02-15 05:04:38.174059+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/sheetmoss%2Fsheetmoss03.jpg?alt=media&token=a5f426ac-3ea4-4e20-806d-f5717acebea1', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (58, 'Bucephalandra "Dark Catherine" (Rare, 3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Dark Catherine" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Dark Catherine" Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Bucephalandra "Dark Catherine"</strong> (also commonly spelled <em>Catherinae</em>) is a sophisticated and highly elegant variety from the island of Borneo. It is easily distinguished by its long, very narrow, and needle-like leaves that feature intense ruffling along the margins. As its name suggests, "Dark Catherine" displays a moody, deep forest-green to near-black coloration, often shimmering with a metallic purple or copper iridescence under high-quality lighting.</p>

<p>This variety is a favorite at <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong> for its refined texture and its ability to create a "willow-like" effect when flowing in the current of a professional aquascape.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Intensity:</strong> Thrives in <strong>medium to high lighting</strong>. While it is more tolerant of lower light than some "Black" varieties, high intensity is required to bring out the dark purple metallic sheen.</li>
    <li><strong>Spectrum:</strong> Full-spectrum RGB LEDs are recommended to highlight the "galaxy" spots (silvery stomata) and the deep, dark pigments of the narrow leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Photoperiod:</strong> Maintain a consistent 7–8 hour light cycle. Because it grows slowly, avoid excessive light without balanced CO2, as the thin leaves are susceptible to Green Spot Algae (GSA).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stability:</strong> Like all Buce, "Dark Catherine" hates sudden shifts in water chemistry. Regular, small water changes are better than infrequent large ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers <strong>71°F to 78°F (22°C – 26°C)</strong>. In warmer water, ensure high oxygenation to prevent rhizome rot.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.4. It thrives in slightly acidic to neutral water with low to medium hardness.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Enjoys <strong>moderate to strong flow</strong>. The long, narrow leaves look beautiful waving in the current, and the flow helps keep the ruffled edges free of organic debris.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Highly recommended. CO2 injection significantly improves the density of the ruffles and the speed of rhizome branching.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> It is a water-column feeder. Regular dosing of liquid macros and micros (NPK + Trace) is essential. Supplement with <strong>Iron (Fe)</strong> to deepen the dark coloration.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Very slow. It is a slow-motion grower that rewards patience with a dense, "spiky" architectural look.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Epiphytic Nature:</strong> <strong>Do not bury the rhizome</strong> in the substrate. The rhizome must be exposed to the water column, or it will rot.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Secure it to <strong>Lava Rock, Driftwood, or Seiryu Stone</strong> using super glue gel (cyanoacrylate) or dark cotton thread. Its fine roots will eventually anchor it firmly to the hardscape.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> Due to its narrow, "grass-like" leaves, it is perfect for the <strong>foreground to midground</strong> or for accentuating the cracks between large rocks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Appearance and Texture</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Shape:</strong> Extremely narrow, linear, and elongated with a pointed tip. The edges are notably "crispy" or wavy.</li>
    <li><strong>Coloration:</strong> Deep, dark olive-green to charcoal. Under optimal conditions, the new growth can appear reddish-brown before maturing into a dark, metallic purple.</li>
    <li><strong>Form:</strong> Tends to grow in low, creeping mats that eventually form a thick, bushy clump of needle-like foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> Simply snip the rhizome with a sharp, clean blade.</li>
    <li><strong>Cuttings:</strong> Ensure each piece has at least 4–6 leaves. Because the leaves are so narrow, a slightly longer rhizome section is often safer for propagation.</li>
    <li><strong>Multi-Heading:</strong> Trimming the lead tip will often force the plant to sprout multiple new heads from the sides of the rhizome, creating a much fuller appearance.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Cleanliness:</strong> Amano shrimp and Otocinclus are essential "clean-up crews" to keep the intricate, ruffled margins free from algae and biofilm.</li>
    <li><strong>Melt Management:</strong> If the plant "melts" when first added to your tank, do not remove the rhizome. Clean off the dead leaves and keep the water stable; new, adapted growth will usually appear within 2–4 weeks.</li>
    <li><strong>Design Tip:</strong> For <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong> displays, pair "Dark Catherine" with bright green plants like <strong>Micranthemum ''Monte Carlo''</strong> to make the dark, needle-like leaves stand out.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 600.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 1, true, '2026-03-05 13:29:40.207938+00', '2026-03-05 13:29:40.207959+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/DarkCatherine%2Fdarkcatherine001.jpg?alt=media&token=dce5923f-90ac-46d0-ac28-7923fbf196ad', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (59, 'Bucephalandra "Blue Curly" (Super rare, 1 Rhizome)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Blue Curly" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Blue Curly" Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Bucephalandra "Blue Curly"</strong> is a premium, high-contrast variety highly prized by collectors and professional aquascapers at <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong>. As the name suggests, this variety is defined by its intensely wavy (curly) leaf margins and its remarkable ability to produce a deep, metallic blue iridescence. It is a compact to medium-sized rheophyte that adds significant texture and a "cool" color palette to any high-tech or low-tech aquarium setup.</p>

<p>Native to the shaded, fast-flowing jungle streams of Borneo, it is a robust survivor that rewards stable conditions with some of the most beautiful foliage in the aquatic world.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Intensity:</strong> Thrives in <strong>medium to high lighting</strong>. While it can survive in low light, the signature "blue" metallic sheen will only fully develop under stronger illumination.</li>
    <li><strong>Spectrum:</strong> High-quality RGB LED lighting is essential to reflect the blue and violet pigments. Without the proper spectrum, the leaves may appear as a simple dark green.</li>
    <li><strong>Photoperiod:</strong> 7–8 hours is ideal. Be cautious of high light without CO2, as the "curls" in the leaves can easily trap Green Spot Algae (GSA).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stability:</strong> Like all Bucephalandra, "Blue Curly" requires consistent water parameters. Avoid large, sudden water chemistry swings to prevent leaf melt.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers <strong>71°F to 79°F (22°C – 26°C)</strong>. Ensure good oxygenation, especially if your tank runs on the warmer side of this range.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.2. It prefers slightly acidic to neutral water with low to moderate carbonate hardness (KH).</li>
    <li><strong>Flow:</strong> It loves <strong>moderate to strong water flow</strong>. The movement helps the "curly" leaves shimmer and prevents debris from settling in the wavy margins.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Highly recommended. Injecting CO2 will lead to much tighter "curls," faster rhizome branching, and a more pronounced blue metallic luster.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> It is a water-column feeder. Regular dosing of liquid Macro (NPK) and Micro (Trace) nutrients is vital. <strong>Iron (Fe)</strong> is particularly important for enhancing the blue and purple tones.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Slow. Like most premium Buce, it grows at a steady but leisurely pace.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Epiphytic Growth:</strong> <strong>Do not bury the rhizome</strong> in the substrate. The rhizome must remain above the soil/sand to breathe, or it will rot and the plant will die.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Best secured to <strong>Lava Rock, Driftwood, or specialized Ceramic Rings</strong> using cyanoacrylate super glue gel or dark thread.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> Due to its striking texture and color, it is best placed in the <strong>foreground or midground</strong> focal points where it can be easily admired.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Appearance and Texture</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Shape:</strong> Oval to slightly elongated leaves with <strong>heavily ruffled or "curly" edges</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Coloration:</strong> Deep forest green to dark olive base color. Under optimal light and CO2, the leaves develop an "oil-slick" iridescence that reflects bright blue and violet.</li>
    <li><strong>Stomata:</strong> The leaves are covered in distinct, tiny silver dots (stomata) that look like a "galaxy" against the dark blue background.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> Simply snip the rhizome into sections using sharp, sterilized aquatic scissors.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Each cutting should have at least 3–4 healthy leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Bushy Growth:</strong> Trimming the lead growing tip encourages the plant to sprout multiple new heads along the rhizome, eventually forming a thick, "curly" cushion.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Algae Crew:</strong> Keep Amano shrimp or Otocinclus catfish to help keep the textured leaf surfaces free of algae and biofilm.</li>
    <li><strong>Melt Management:</strong> If the plant loses leaves after transition, leave the rhizome alone as long as it is firm. It will likely sprout new, water-adapted leaves within a few weeks.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquatic Exotica Design:</strong> Combine "Blue Curly" with lighter green plants like <strong>Anubias ''Nana Petite''</strong> or <strong>Staurogyne repens</strong> to make the blue metallic hues pop.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 350.00, 700.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 5, true, '2026-03-05 13:32:47.956623+00', '2026-03-05 13:32:47.956639+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/BlueCurly%2Fbluecurly001.jpg?alt=media&token=f9cce0e1-294c-4e6e-bb0c-3d4922ff5c0a', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (15, 'Bucephalandra Boyan (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Boyan" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Boyan" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Boyan" is a slow-growing variety of Bucephalandra that is relatively easy to care for, making it suitable for both beginner and experienced aquascapers. Native to Borneo, these plants are known for their ability to thrive in flowing water and attach themselves to hardscape.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Boyan" can grow well in low to moderate lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While low light is sufficient for survival, moderate lighting can promote faster growth and enhance coloration.</li>
    <li>If using higher light levels, be mindful of potential algae growth, as Bucephalandra grows slowly.</li>
    <li>Some aquascapers place Bucephalandra in shaded areas to help prevent algae issues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Maintain stable water parameters for optimal health.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 71-79°F (21-26°C), although they can tolerate a wider range of 70-82°F (21-28°C).</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, mimicking its natural habitat in fast-moving streams.</li>
    <li>Clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes is essential.</li>
    <li>Some water hardness (GH) is helpful, preferably 5 dGH or above.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly required but can promote faster growth and more vibrant coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer can be beneficial.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer is recommended, particularly if using CO2 injection.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Boyan" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can cause it to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape, such as rocks or driftwood, using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If placing it in the substrate, ensure the roots are covered but the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Boyan" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It typically grows up to 3-6 cm (1.5-2.5 inches) tall.</li>
    <li>Some varieties, like "Mini Narrow," are known for their compact size.</li>
    <li>The leaves can have a distinctive shape, with round or elongated forms and potentially wavy edges.</li>
    <li> Its final size, coloration, and growth rate depend on the aquarium conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by cutting the rhizome into sections using clean, sharp scissors.</li>
    <li>Ensure each section has healthy roots and a few leaves or a growing tip for best results.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape or plant them (with the rhizome exposed) in the substrate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Boyan" is considered easy to moderate in care level.</li>
    <li>It is sensitive to sudden changes in water parameters, so aim for stability.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when the plant adjusts to a new environment, but it will usually recover and produce new growth from the rhizome if it remains healthy.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any dead, yellowing, or decaying leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra can be grown emersed (out of water) in humid environments like paludariums or terrariums.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 250.00, 550.00, 5.0, false, true, true, false, 0, true, '2025-07-09 07:40:57.712204+00', '2026-03-01 19:24:20.335192+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/boyan%2Fboyan01.jpeg?alt=media&token=fc126447-296b-4ba5-8990-9a5b45e3f86e', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/boyan%2Fboyan01.jpeg?alt=media&token=fc126447-296b-4ba5-8990-9a5b45e3f86e');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (13, 'Bucephalandra Red Velvet Mini (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" is a captivating and highly sought-after variety of Bucephalandra, known for its deep red or maroon coloration and velvety leaf texture. Native to Borneo, these slow-growing plants add a vibrant and unique element to planted aquariums.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" can thrive in low to medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>Moderate lighting is recommended to bring out its best colors and encourage healthy growth.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate higher lighting, be mindful of algae growth, as Bucephalandra are slow growers.</li>
    <li>Excessive light can cause leaves to burn and stress the plant, especially without adequate CO2 and nutrients.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" prefers stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 70-82°F (21-28°C) or {Link: 71-82.5°F (21.5-28°C) according to Shrimp and Snail Breeder https://aquariumbreeder.com/bucephalandra-care-guide-planting-growing-and-propagation/}.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, mimicking its natural habitat in streams with moderate to high flow.</li>
    <li>Maintain clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes to prevent the buildup of waste products.</li>
    <li>Some hardness (GH/KH) is helpful, with a dGH of 5 or above being recommended.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not essential for survival, but it will significantly enhance growth and coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer is beneficial.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer containing N, P, K, and micronutrients like iron and magnesium, will support healthy growth and vibrant colors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot and melting.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape elements like rocks or driftwood using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If planting in the substrate, ensure only the roots are covered and the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It features deep red or maroon leaves with a velvety texture.</li>
    <li>The leaves are generally 4-6 cm long and 2-4 cm wide, often with wavy edges.</li>
    <li>Young leaves may be a lighter color, deepening to a rich red or maroon as they mature.</li>
    <li>It can reach a height of 10-15 cm (4-6 inches).</li>
    <li> Some varieties may also show iridescent qualities, reflecting blue or purple hues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by cutting the rhizome into sections, each with a few leaves and roots.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape or plant them (with the rhizome exposed) in the substrate.</li>
    <li>Bare rhizomes can also produce new shoots if healthy and provided with good conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Velvet" is relatively hardy and considered easy to care for, even for beginners.</li>
    <li>However, it is sensitive to drastic changes in water parameters, so maintaining stability is key.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when introduced to a new environment or with unstable parameters, but new growth will typically emerge from a healthy rhizome.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, decaying, or melting leaves.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is crucial for distributing nutrients and preventing detritus buildup.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 250.00, 550.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 20, true, '2025-07-09 07:40:57.712204+00', '2026-03-05 13:39:31.343093+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/red-velvet%2Fredvelvet01.jpeg?alt=media&token=4d372056-6bfc-4e58-af73-d4b188b06364', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/red-velvet%2Fredvelvet01.jpeg?alt=media&token=4d372056-6bfc-4e58-af73-d4b188b06364');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (26, 'Bucephalandra Black Angel (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Black Angel" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Black Angel" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Black Angel" is a striking and relatively rare variety of Bucephalandra native to the streams of Borneo. As the name suggests, it is prized for its exceptionally dark foliage, which can appear almost black or deep indigo under aquarium lighting, providing a dramatic contrast against lighter green plants.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Black Angel":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Black Angel" does best in low to medium lighting environments.</li>
    <li>Because the leaves are so dark, they can easily mask the early signs of algae; therefore, lighting should be kept moderate (around 30-50 PAR) to prevent algae issues.</li>
    <li>While the plant can survive in low light, slightly higher intensity is required to bring out the metallic blue or purple sheen often hidden within the dark leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like its cousins, "Black Angel" requires stable water parameters to prevent melting.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (21-26°', 250.00, 600.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 10, true, '2025-12-21 17:36:44.253732+00', '2026-03-05 03:46:01.184532+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/BlackAngel%2Fblackangel01.jpeg?alt=media&token=74c1ae6d-a730-448d-adca-d2d2f617190b', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (60, 'Anubias nana ( 1 Pot)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Anubias barteri var. nana Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Anubias nana Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Anubias barteri var. nana</strong>, or simply <strong>Anubias nana</strong>, is perhaps the most iconic and essential plant in the freshwater aquarium hobby. Native to the shaded riverbanks of West Africa, it is legendary for its "bulletproof" nature. It features thick, dark green, heart-shaped leaves that are tough enough to withstand even the most herbivorous fish, such as Cichlids or Goldfish. For a business like <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong>, it is a staple "bread and butter" plant that appeals to both beginners and high-end scapers.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Prefers <strong>low to medium lighting</strong>. It is one of the few plants that can thrive in very dim setups.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun/High Light:</strong> In high-tech tanks with intense light, Anubias nana is highly prone to <strong>Green Spot Algae (GSA)</strong> because its leaves are very slow-growing and long-lived. </li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> It is best placed in shaded areas under larger plants or hardscape to keep the leaves clean and algae-free.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Hardiness:</strong> Extremely adaptable to a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers <strong>72°F to 82°F (22°C – 28°C)</strong>. It can tolerate warmer water better than many Bucephalandra species.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.5. It is comfortable in both soft and moderately hard water.</li>
    <li><strong>Flow:</strong> Appreciates moderate water movement to prevent organic debris from settling on its broad leaves, which can suffocate the plant or encourage algae.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Not required. It is a true "low-tech" plant. However, CO2 injection will result in faster leaf production and more robust growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> As an epiphyte, it takes its nutrients from the water column. Regular dosing of a comprehensive liquid fertilizer is beneficial. </li>
    <li><strong>Nutrient Warning:</strong> If the leaves turn yellow (chlorosis), it is often a sign of iron or potassium deficiency.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The Golden Rule:</strong> <strong>Do not bury the rhizome.</strong> If the thick, horizontal stem (rhizome) is buried in sand or gravel, it will rot, and the plant will die.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Secure the plant to <strong>Driftwood, Bogwood, or Rocks</strong> using super glue gel, fishing line, or cotton thread. Over time, its strong roots will anchor itself firmly to the hardscape.</li>
    <li><strong>Substrate Option:</strong> You can bury the fine roots in the substrate, but ensure the rhizome itself remains entirely above the surface.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> A compact variety that reaches about 2–4 inches (5–10 cm) in height.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Very slow. It typically produces one new leaf every few weeks.</li>
    <li><strong>Foliage:</strong> Thick, leathery, and dark green. The leaves are remarkably durable and can last for years in the right conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Rhizome Division:</strong> Easily propagated by cutting the rhizome with a sharp, clean pair of scissors or a blade.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Each division should have at least 3–4 healthy leaves and a few roots to ensure survival.</li>
    <li><strong>Bushy Growth:</strong> To encourage the plant to branch out, you can make a small nick in the rhizome; this often stimulates a new growing point.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Algae Prevention:</strong> To combat Green Spot Algae, maintain a healthy level of Phosphates in the water and keep the plant in lower-light areas of the tank.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean-up Crew:</strong> Nerite snails and Otocinclus are excellent for keeping the broad, flat leaves of Anubias nana polished and free of biofilm.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquatic Exotica Design:</strong> This plant is perfect for the <strong>midground</strong> or for covering the "joints" where driftwood meets rocks. Its dark green color provides a beautiful contrast to lighter stones like Dragon Stone.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 400.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 8, true, '2026-03-05 17:42:00.613912+00', '2026-03-05 17:42:00.613931+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/anubias%2Fnanapetite%2FnanPetite.png?alt=media&token=f515ad55-354e-46b5-b691-a11a56b64da7', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (61, 'Anubias nana ''Gold'' (1 Pot)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Anubias barteri var. nana ''Gold'' Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Anubias nana ''Gold'' Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Anubias nana ''Gold''</strong> (also known as Anubias ''Golden'') is a vibrant color variant of the classic Anubias nana. It is highly prized in the aquascaping community for its striking lime-green to golden-yellow foliage. Unlike the standard dark green Anubias, the ''Gold'' variety provides a brilliant "pop" of color that can brighten up dark corners of an aquarium. It is just as hardy as the original species, making it an excellent choice for <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong> customers who want a high-contrast look without the difficulty of demanding stem plants.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Prefers <strong>low to medium lighting</strong>. While its golden color can become more intense under medium light, it should be monitored closely.</li>
    <li><strong>Algae Risk:</strong> Because it is a slow grower, high intensity light can quickly lead to <strong>Green Spot Algae (GSA)</strong> covering the leaves. If the leaves start turning green rather than gold, it is often a sign of too little light; if they turn white or transparent, the light is too intense.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> Ideally placed in the midground or foreground where it is not overshadowed but is protected from the most intense "hot spots" of your aquarium LED.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Adaptability:</strong> Extremely hardy and tolerant of a wide variety of water conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers <strong>72°F to 82°F (22°C – 28°C)</strong>. Like most Anubias, it can handle occasional temperature fluctuations well.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.8. It thrives in both soft and moderately hard water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Appreciates moderate water flow to keep the leaves free of detritus and waste buildup.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Not required for survival, but CO2 injection will encourage faster growth and more vibrant golden hues in new leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> It draws nutrients from the water column through its leaves and roots. Regular dosing of a comprehensive liquid fertilizer is recommended.</li>
    <li><strong>Nutrient Tip:</strong> Maintaining proper phosphate levels can help keep the slow-growing leaves free from Green Spot Algae.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The Golden Rule:</strong> <strong>Do not bury the rhizome.</strong> The thick horizontal stem must remain above the substrate. If buried in sand or gravel, the rhizome will rot and the plant will eventually die.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Best used as an epiphyte. Secure it to <strong>Lava Rocks, Driftwood, or Seiryu Stones</strong> using cyanoacrylate super glue gel or dark thread. Its roots will naturally grow and anchor themselves to the surface over time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> A compact variety, typically reaching 2–4 inches (5–10 cm) in height.</li>
    <li><strong>Coloration:</strong> New leaves appear as a bright, neon yellow-gold, gradually maturing into a lime-green. This creates a beautiful gradient of color within a single clump.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Very slow. It is a patient plant that requires very little maintenance once established.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Rhizome Division:</strong> Simply cut the rhizome into pieces with a sharp, clean blade.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Ensure each section has at least 3–4 healthy leaves and a few root filaments.</li>
    <li><strong>Bushy Growth:</strong> Trimming the lead tip can help stimulate the rhizome to branch out, creating a fuller, more compact golden bush.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Occasionally "dust" the leaves during water changes by gently rubbing them between your fingers to remove biofilm or early algae growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean-up Crew:</strong> Amano shrimp, Nerite snails, and Otocinclus are perfect for keeping the bright golden leaves polished and clean.</li>
    <li><strong>Design Tip:</strong> For <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong> setups, pair ''Gold'' Anubias next to dark-colored plants like <strong>Bucephalandra ''Godzilla''</strong> or <strong>Dark Catherine</strong>. The color contrast is spectacular and makes both plants stand out.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 400.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 8, true, '2026-03-05 17:43:17.995258+00', '2026-03-05 17:43:17.995278+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/anubias%2Fnanagold%2FnanGold.png?alt=media&token=a90fa5d7-6560-4685-ac30-a6b076b203cf', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (62, 'Anubias nana ''Petite'' ( 1 Pot)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Anubias barteri var. nana ''Petite'' Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Anubias nana ''Petite'' Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Anubias barteri var. nana ''Petite''</strong> is the miniature version of the already compact Anubias nana. It is one of the most desirable plants for nano-aquascaping and detailing larger layouts. Known for its tiny, deep green, teardrop-shaped leaves, it remains the "gold standard" for creating a sense of scale in miniature environments. For <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong>, this is a high-demand "detailing" plant that is essential for professional-grade hardscape work.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Thrives in <strong>low to medium lighting</strong>. It is highly adaptable but does not require high-intensity light.</li>
    <li><strong>Algae Risk:</strong> Because the leaves are tiny and grow extremely slowly, they are highly susceptible to <strong>Green Spot Algae (GSA)</strong> if placed directly under intense light.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> It is best used to detail the "cracks" and crevices of your hardscape. Placing it in slightly shaded areas or tucked between rocks helps keep the foliage clean.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Hardiness:</strong> One of the hardiest plants in the hobby. It can withstand significant fluctuations in water chemistry.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers <strong>72°F to 82°F (22°C – 28°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.5. It is comfortable in both soft and moderately hard water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Moderate flow is beneficial to prevent debris from settling on the small leaves, which can lead to localized rot or algae growth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Not required, making it perfect for low-tech "Walstad" or simple shrimp tanks. However, CO2 will produce a much denser, more compact growth habit.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> It is an epiphytic feeder. It primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column. Weekly dosing of a balanced liquid fertilizer is recommended.</li>
    <li><strong>Nutrient Tip:</strong> If the tiny leaves start to turn yellow or show holes, it usually indicates a <strong>Potassium (K)</strong> or <strong>Iron (Fe)</strong> deficiency.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The Critical Rule:</strong> <strong>Never bury the rhizome.</strong> If the green horizontal stem is buried under substrate, the plant will rot and die.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Its small size makes it perfect for "gluing." Use a tiny drop of cyanoacrylate super glue gel to attach it to <strong>Lava Rock, Driftwood, or Seiryu Stone</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Detailing:</strong> Many scapers use ''Petite'' to hide visible glue lines or to transition between large hardscape elements and the foreground substrate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> It is truly "petite," typically reaching only 1–2 inches (2.5–5 cm) in total height. The leaves are often no larger than a fingernail.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Extremely slow. It may only produce a few leaves per month.</li>
    <li><strong>Durability:</strong> The leaves are thick and leathery, making them unappealing to most herbivorous fish and snails.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Rhizome Division:</strong> Propagate by carefully cutting the thin rhizome with a sharp blade or specialized aquascaping scissors.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Each cutting should have at least 3 healthy leaves and a few root strands.</li>
    <li><strong>Bushy Effect:</strong> Because it is so small, ''Petite'' looks best when several small rhizomes are grouped together to form a dense "bush."</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Shrimp Safety:</strong> This plant is a favorite for shrimp breeders as it provides excellent surface area for biofilm to grow without taking up much swimming space.</li>
    <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Use a soft toothbrush to very gently brush the leaves if you notice any algae buildup.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquatic Exotica Design:</strong> Use ''Petite'' in the <strong>foreground</strong> or to create "miniature forests" on driftwood branches. It pairs exceptionally well with <strong>Bucephalandra ''Mini Coin''</strong> for a high-end, textured look.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 450.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 8, true, '2026-03-05 17:44:50.458642+00', '2026-03-05 17:44:50.458658+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/anubias%2Fnanapetite%2FnanPetite.png?alt=media&token=f515ad55-354e-46b5-b691-a11a56b64da7', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (63, 'Anubias ''Dragon Claw''', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Anubias barteri ''Dragon Claw'' Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Anubias ''Dragon Claw'' Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Anubias ''Dragon Claw''</strong> is a unique and rare cultivar of the Anubias family, highly prized for its distinct leaf shape. Unlike the rounded or heart-shaped leaves of the standard Nana, ''Dragon Claw'' features elongated, narrow, and slightly curved leaves that taper to a sharp point—resembling the talons of a dragon. Its dark green, leathery foliage and compact growth habit make it an exotic addition to any collection at <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong>.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Prefers <strong>low to medium lighting</strong>. Like most Anubias, it is highly efficient at photosynthesizing in shaded areas.</li>
    <li><strong>High Light Warning:</strong> Avoid placing it directly under high-intensity LED "hot spots." The slow growth rate of ''Dragon Claw'' makes it a prime target for Green Spot Algae (GSA) if the lighting is too intense.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> It looks best when nestled into the midground, particularly in the shadows of driftwood or larger plants where its unique leaf silhouette can be highlighted.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Hardiness:</strong> Extremely robust and adaptable to various water conditions, making it perfect for both beginners and experts.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers <strong>72°F to 82°F (22°C – 28°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 6.0 – 7.8. It is very comfortable in the slightly acidic to neutral water common in most aquascapes.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Enjoys moderate water circulation. This helps keep the "claw-like" leaves clean and ensures nutrient delivery to the rhizome.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Not required for survival. However, CO2 injection will promote slightly faster growth and a more robust root system.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> It is an epiphytic feeder that draws nutrients primarily from the water column. Regular dosing of a comprehensive liquid fertilizer (NPK + Trace) is recommended.</li>
    <li><strong>Indicator:</strong> If the sharp tips of the "claws" start to turn yellow, check for a potassium deficiency.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The Critical Rule:</strong> <strong>Do not bury the rhizome.</strong> The horizontal stem must remain above the substrate. If it is buried under gravel or soil, the plant will suffer from rhizome rot.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Secure the plant to <strong>hardscape (Rocks, Wood, or Ceramic)</strong> using cyanoacrylate super glue gel or fishing line. Its roots are exceptionally strong and will eventually "grip" the surface like a claw.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquascaping Tip:</strong> Its narrow leaves make it an excellent choice for "vertical" detailing on upright pieces of driftwood.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Shape:</strong> Distinctly narrow, lanceolate, and often slightly curved or "hooked" at the tip.</li>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> Remains relatively compact, typically reaching 3–5 inches (7–12 cm) in height.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Very slow. It is a low-maintenance plant that stays in its designated spot without overtaking the tank.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Rhizome Division:</strong> Propagate by cutting the rhizome into smaller sections with a sharp, sterile blade.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Each piece should have at least 3 healthy leaves to ensure it has enough energy to start new growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Stimulation:</strong> Trimming the roots slightly when propagating can sometimes encourage the plant to anchor more quickly to new hardscape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Algae Control:</strong> Keep your phosphate levels balanced to prevent Green Spot Algae. Inhabitants like Amano shrimp or Nerite snails are great for keeping the "claws" polished.</li>
    <li><strong>Durability:</strong> Its tough, leathery leaves are generally safe from herbivorous fish like Goldfish or Cichlids.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquatic Exotica Design:</strong> Pair ''Dragon Claw'' with <strong>Anubias Nana Gold</strong> or <strong>Bucephalandra Blue Curly</strong> to create a high-contrast texture and color map in your midground.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 450.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 12, true, '2026-03-05 17:45:55.819032+00', '2026-03-05 17:45:55.819048+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/anubias%2Fdragonclaw%2Fdragonclaw.png?alt=media&token=5b0072dd-f931-4dab-a955-3013b7c57f97', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (64, 'Anubias glabra', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Anubias barteri var. glabra Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Anubias glabra Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Anubias barteri var. glabra</strong> (often sold as <em>Anubias minima</em> or <em>Anubias lanceolata</em>) is a graceful and distinctive variant within the Anubias family. Unlike the common rounded leaves of most Anubias, the ''Glabra'' variety is defined by its long, slender, lance-shaped (sword-like) leaves. Native to the rainforest streams of West Africa, it offers a refined verticality to aquascapes and is a favorite at <strong>Aquatic Exotica</strong> for customers looking for a hardy alternative to traditional bushy plants.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Prefers <strong>low to medium lighting</strong>. It is highly efficient in shaded conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>High Light Warning:</strong> Avoid intense direct light. Its slow growth rate makes the long, narrow leaves prime targets for <strong>Green Spot Algae (GSA)</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> It is exceptionally versatile. Due to its upright growth, it works well in the midground or background, especially when tucked behind rocks to let the long leaves flow upward.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Hardiness:</strong> Extremely robust; a true "beginner-friendly" plant that can adapt to almost any freshwater environment.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Prefers <strong>72°F to 82°F (22°C – 28°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>pH Range:</strong> 5.5 – 7.5. It thrives in slightly acidic to neutral water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Enjoys gentle to moderate water circulation. This mimics its natural habitat along riverbanks and keeps its glossy leaves free of debris.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> Not required. It will grow steadily in low-tech setups. However, CO2 injection will result in more robust foliage and a faster-creeping rhizome.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> Primarily an epiphytic feeder. It absorbs nutrients through its leaves and roots from the water column. Regular dosing with a liquid fertilizer is recommended to maintain its deep green luster.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The Critical Rule:</strong> <strong>Never bury the rhizome.</strong> If the green horizontal stem is buried in soil or sand, it will rot, leading to the death of the plant.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Secure the plant to <strong>Driftwood, Lava Rock, or Slate</strong> using super glue gel or thread. Its roots are vigorous and will quickly anchor the plant to any porous surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Vertical Interest:</strong> Because the leaves grow more vertically than other Anubias, it is an excellent choice for "green wall" effects on aquarium backgrounds.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Shape:</strong> Narrow, lanceolate leaves with a smooth texture and slightly wavy edges.</li>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> Can grow taller than ''Nana'' varieties, typically reaching <strong>6 to 12 inches (15–30 cm)</strong> in height.</li>
    <li><strong>Coloration:</strong> Displays a rich, dark green color that provides a cool, forest-like feel to the aquarium.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Slow to moderate. It is a stable plant that maintains its shape for long periods without needing frequent pruning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Rhizome Division:</strong> Easily propagated by cutting the rhizome with a sharp, clean blade.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Ensure each cutting has at least 3–4 healthy leaves and a few root filaments.</li>
    <li><strong>Recovery:</strong> The mother plant will often sprout multiple new heads from the cut site, eventually creating a denser, more impressive colony.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Cichlid and Perch Proof:</strong> Its tough, leathery leaves are generally ignored by herbivorous fish, making it a top choice for African Cichlid tanks.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean-up Crew:</strong> Inhabitants like Amano shrimp or Nerite snails are great for keeping the long leaf blades free of biofilm.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquatic Exotica Design:</strong> Use <strong>Anubias glabra</strong> alongside <strong>Anubias nana ''Petite''</strong> at the base to create a sense of depth and varied texture in your hardscape.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 400.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 9, true, '2026-03-05 17:47:04.642448+00', '2026-03-05 17:47:04.642467+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/anubias%2Fgalbra%2Fgalbra.png?alt=media&token=b737d8bb-f8d6-41d6-b1b0-273ef7ee9d59', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (24, 'Christmas Moss', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Christmas Moss Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Christmas Moss Care Guide</h1>

<p>Christmas Moss (Vesicularia montagnei) is a popular aquatic moss native to Southeast Asia, commonly found in tropical regions of India, Thailand, and the Philippines. This attractive moss gets its name from its distinctive triangular, Christmas tree-like growth pattern. It''s highly valued in aquascaping for creating natural-looking underwater landscapes and provides excellent shelter for small fish and invertebrates. Christmas moss is considered relatively easy to grow and maintain, making it suitable for both beginners and experienced aquarists.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss can thrive in low to medium lighting conditions, making it very versatile.</li>
    <li>Low light (1-2 watts per gallon) is sufficient for healthy growth and maintenance.</li>
    <li>Medium lighting enhances growth rate and creates denser, more vibrant moss coverage.</li>
    <li>High lighting can promote algae growth on the moss, which can be difficult to remove.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss is adaptable to a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 68-78°F (20-25°C) for optimal growth.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5, with slightly acidic to neutral water preferred.</li>
    <li>Water hardness: 5-15 dGH, tolerant of both soft and moderately hard water.</li>
    <li>Good water circulation helps prevent debris accumulation and promotes healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Regular water changes help maintain water quality and prevent algae issues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not required but will significantly enhance growth rate and density.</li>
    <li>Can grow well without CO2 supplementation in low-tech aquarium setups.</li>
    <li>Liquid fertilizers containing micronutrients benefit overall health and coloration.</li>
    <li>Iron supplementation helps maintain the rich green coloration of the moss.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss does not require substrate as it attaches to surfaces naturally.</li>
    <li>Attach to driftwood, rocks, or aquarium decorations using fishing line or cotton thread.</li>
    <li>Can be secured to mesh or plastic grids to create moss walls or carpets.</li>
    <li>Allow several weeks for the moss to naturally attach before removing tying materials.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss is a slow to moderate-growing aquatic moss.</li>
    <li>Features distinctive triangular, Christmas tree-like branching patterns.</li>
    <li>Individual fronds can grow 2-4 inches long with proper conditions.</li>
    <li>Creates dense, carpet-like coverage when allowed to spread naturally.</li>
    <li>Rich green coloration that can vary from bright to dark green depending on conditions.</li>
    <li>Forms thick mats over time, providing excellent hiding places for fish and invertebrates.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by dividing existing moss colonies into smaller portions.</li>
    <li>Simply cut or tear healthy sections and attach to new locations.</li>
    <li>Each small piece can develop into a full colony given time and proper conditions.</li>
    <li>Propagation is most successful during periods of active growth in spring and summer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss is hardy and forgiving, making it excellent for beginners.</li>
    <li>Regular pruning helps maintain shape and prevents excessive thickness that blocks light.</li>
    <li>Remove any dead or brown sections promptly to prevent decay and water quality issues.</li>
    <li>Gentle water flow helps keep the moss clean and free from debris accumulation.</li>
    <li>Compatible with most community fish and is especially beneficial for breeding tanks.</li>
    <li>Provides natural filtration by absorbing nutrients and producing oxygen during photosynthesis.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 100.00, 250.00, 5.0, false, false, true, false, 0, true, '2025-09-18 06:42:29.89409+00', '2026-02-16 10:23:12.752458+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/ChristmasMoss%2FChristmasMoss.jpeg?alt=media&token=4e436c96-7efa-4fae-a874-888757724cb8', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (32, 'Bucephalandra Super Catherine (One Rhizome)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" is an elegant and highly distinctive variety of Bucephalandra native to Borneo. It is easily recognized by its exceptionally long, narrow, and needle-like leaves that feature a deep green to dark reddish-brown coloration. This "Super" variant is essentially a more robust or elongated version of the standard Catherineae, making it a perfect choice for creating a flowing, grassy texture on aquarium hardscape.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Super Catherine":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" is quite versatile and can be kept in low to medium lighting.</li>
    <li>Under lower light, the leaves tend to stay a dark, forest green. Under medium light, you may see more bronze or reddish tints on the new growth.</li>
    <li>Because the leaves are very thin and narrow, they are particularly sensitive to hair algae; avoid high-intensity lighting unless the tank is very well-balanced with CO2.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like most Buce species, "Super Catherine" thrives in stable, consistent water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (22-26°C). {Link: According to www.pudr.ca https://www.pudr.ca/product-page/bucephalandra-super-catherine}, maintaining a temperature within the low to mid-70s is ideal for preventing rhizome rot.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers slightly acidic water but can adapt to neutral conditions.</li>
    <li>High water oxygenation and good flow are beneficial, as this variety often grows near waterfalls and fast-moving streams in the wild.</li>
    <li>A GH of 4-8 is recommended to provide the necessary minerals for its unique leaf structure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>While CO2 is not strictly necessary for "Super Catherine," it will help the plant grow a more dense and "bushy" rhizome system.</li>
    <li>Liquid fertilization is highly recommended since it is an epiphyte. A balanced NPK fertilizer with added micronutrients will keep the narrow leaves from becoming brittle.</li>
    <li>Occasional iron supplementation can help enhance the darker, darker-red tones in the foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" is an epiphyte and should never be planted with its rhizome in the substrate.</li>
    <li>The thin rhizome is especially prone to rot if buried. It should be attached to driftwood, rocks, or mesh using super glue gel or thin fishing line.</li>
    <li>Because of its long, flowing leaves, it looks spectacular when attached to higher points of driftwood where the water flow can move the foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Super Catherine" is a slow-growing plant with a creeping growth habit.</li>
    <li>The leaves are its most notable feature: extremely narrow, linear, and slightly wavy along the margins.</li>
    <li>The color is typically a very dark green, often appearing almost black or dark brown in certain lighting.</li>
    <li>It produces a very fine root system that eventually creates a dense mat over whatever surface it is attached to.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is simple: use sharp aquascaping scissors to snip a portion of the rhizome.</li>
    <li>Ensure the cutting has at least 5-6 of the narrow leaves to give it enough energy to establish itself.</li>
    <li>Simply reattach the cutting to a new piece of hardscape and it will eventually begin to sprout new lateral buds.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is excellent for "detail work" in an aquascape, such as filling small gaps between rocks or adding texture to the ends of thin branches.</li>
    <li>"Super Catherine" is known to be relatively hardy once established, but it can melt if moved into a brand-new, uncycled tank.</li>
    <li>Keep the leaves clean of debris; because they are so thin, detritus buildup can quickly lead to localized rot or algae growth.</li>
    <li>Shrimp, especially Amano and Cherry shrimp, love to pick through the narrow leaves of "Super Catherine," keeping them pristine.</li>
    <li>It can be grown emersed in high-humidity setups, where it takes on a slightly more upright growth habit.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 1200.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 0, true, '2025-12-21 17:47:53.833454+00', '2026-03-05 03:44:49.244037+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/SuperCatherine%2Fsupercathrine.jpeg?alt=media&token=0f15be31-b747-40a5-aef8-e3e02d556b48', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (28, 'Bucephalandra PinkLady (One rhizome)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" is a rare and highly attractive variety of Bucephalandra, cherished for its unique pinkish-green leaves and vibrant pink-tinted stems. This endemic species from the island of Borneo provides a subtle but beautiful pop of color to any aquascape, making it a favorite among collectors and enthusiasts.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Pink Lady":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" thrives under low to moderate lighting levels.</li>
    <li>While the plant can tolerate low light, medium lighting is recommended to intensify the pink and red hues in the leaves and stems.</li>
    <li>As a slow grower, it is susceptible to algae; keep lighting levels balanced (typically below 50 umols) to maintain healthy, clean foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stable water conditions are essential for "Pink Lady" to thrive and avoid the dreaded "Buce melt."</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 72-82°F (22-28°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/pink-lady}, this variety adapts well to standard tropical temperatures within this stable range.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0. It prefers slightly acidic to neutral water (pH 6.0-7.0) for optimal health.</li>
    <li>Clean, well-filtered water with good flow is important, as these plants naturally grow in fast-moving streams.</li>
    <li>Moderate water hardness (dGH 4-12) is beneficial for long-term leaf health.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly required but is highly recommended to improve growth rates and coloration.</li>
    <li>Regular dosing of a liquid fertilizer containing iron and micronutrients is helpful for maintaining the distinctive pink tints.</li>
    <li>It primarily draws nutrients from the water column, so maintaining a consistent fertilization schedule will lead to better results.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" is an epiphytic plant and must not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, or it will rot.</li>
    <li>Attach the plant to hardscape like driftwood or rocks using super glue gel or thread.</li>
    <li>If you wish to place it in the substrate, ensure only the roots are buried while the rhizome remains completely exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is very slow-growing, with new leaves sprouting from thin, long rhizomes.</li>
    <li>It is characterized by olive-green leaves with pronounced midribs and stems that display a clear pink or reddish hue.</li>
    <li>Mature leaves can grow up to 1.5 to 3 inches long, making it a larger variant compared to many "mini" types.</li>
    <li>The foliage often features tiny white dots (stomata) and can exhibit an iridescent sheen under the right lighting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by carefully cutting the rhizome into sections with sharp scissors.</li>
    <li>Ensure each section has at least a few leaves and healthy roots before reattaching it to a new surface.</li>
    <li>It is best to allow the plant to establish a healthy clump before attempting to divide the rhizome.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Be patient during the acclimation period; "Pink Lady" may experience some leaf melt when first introduced to a new environment.</li>
    <li>Regular maintenance, such as removing decaying leaves and cleaning organic waste, helps prevent algae issues.</li>
    <li>The plant can be grown emersed in high-humidity setups like paludariums, though its submerged form usually shows the best color.</li>
    <li>Avoid making drastic changes to water parameters, as stability is the key success factor for all Bucephalandra species.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 650.00, 1300.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 1, true, '2025-12-21 17:40:54.622881+00', '2026-03-05 03:45:41.792904+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/PinkLady%2Fpinklady1.jpeg?alt=media&token=e9a03678-1e93-4800-b5fd-b5e0f145bc40', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (20, 'Syngonium Pink Arrowhead', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Syngonium Pink Arrowhead Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Syngonium Pink Arrowhead Care Guide</h1>

<p>The Syngonium Pink Arrowhead (Syngonium podophyllum ''Pink'') is a stunning tropical houseplant prized for its heart-shaped leaves that display beautiful pink, green, and cream variegation. Also known as Pink Splash or Pink Allusion, this climbing vine starts with compact arrow-shaped juvenile leaves that gradually develop into larger, lobed mature leaves as the plant climbs. Native to Central and South America, this plant is beloved for its vibrant coloration and relatively easy care requirements.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bright, indirect light is ideal for maintaining the pink coloration and healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Place near an east or north-facing window, or 2-3 feet from a south/west window with a sheer curtain.</li>
    <li>Direct sunlight will scorch the delicate pink leaves and cause them to fade or turn brown.</li>
    <li>Insufficient light causes the pink coloration to fade, leaves become more green, and growth slows significantly.</li>
    <li>LED grow lights can supplement natural light, especially in winter. Keep 12-18 inches away for 10-12 hours daily.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Requirements</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water when the top 1-2 inches of soil feel dry, typically every 7-10 days in growing season.</li>
    <li>Water thoroughly until excess drains from drainage holes, then empty saucers after 30 minutes.</li>
    <li>Use filtered, distilled, or rainwater when possible for best results.</li>
    <li>Tap water should sit 24 hours to allow chlorine to evaporate before use.</li>
    <li>Yellow leaves indicate overwatering, while droopy leaves with crispy edges suggest underwatering.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 65-80°F (18-27°C) with day temperatures of 70-80°F (21-27°C).</li>
    <li>Night temperatures should remain between 65-70°F (18-21°C).</li>
    <li>Avoid temperatures below 60°F (15°C) as this can damage the plant.</li>
    <li>Prefers 50-60% humidity for optimal pink coloration and leaf health.</li>
    <li>Increase humidity using humidifiers, pebble trays, grouping plants together, or misting around the plant.</li>
    <li>Ensure gentle air movement to prevent fungal issues, but avoid cold drafts from AC/heating vents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Potting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requires well-draining, airy potting mix to prevent root rot.</li>
    <li>Ideal soil blend: 40% quality potting soil, 20% perlite, 20% orchid bark, 20% coconut coir.</li>
    <li>Choose pots with drainage holes; terracotta is excellent for moisture regulation.</li>
    <li>Start with 4-6 inch pots for young plants, repotting annually in spring.</li>
    <li>Repot when roots grow from drainage holes or water drains too quickly.</li>
    <li>Slightly acidic to neutral soil (pH 5.5-7.0) is ideal for optimal nutrient uptake.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Feed every 2-4 weeks during spring and summer with balanced liquid fertilizer (10-10-10 or 20-20-20).</li>
    <li>Dilute fertilizer to half strength to prevent burning the roots.</li>
    <li>Reduce feeding to monthly or stop completely in fall and winter when growth slows.</li>
    <li>Resume regular fertilization in spring when new growth appears.</li>
    <li>Over-fertilizing signs include brown leaf tips, excessive green growth, or white salt buildup on soil.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pruning and Support</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Regular pruning maintains compact growth and encourages pink juvenile leaves.</li>
    <li>Best pruning time is spring/early summer for optimal recovery.</li>
    <li>Remove yellowing leaves, leggy vines, and trim for desired shape using clean, sharp tools.</li>
    <li>Provide moss poles, trellises, or stakes to support climbing growth habit.</li>
    <li>Supporting the vine encourages larger, more mature leaf development.</li>
    <li>Sterilize pruning tools with rubbing alcohol between cuts to prevent disease spread.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water propagation is the easiest method for beginners.</li>
    <li>Cut 4-6 inch stems with at least 2-3 nodes (small bumps where roots emerge).</li>
    <li>Remove lower leaves, keeping 2-3 leaves at the top of the cutting.</li>
    <li>Place cutting in clean water with nodes submerged, changing water every 3-5 days.</li>
    <li>Roots typically develop in 2-4 weeks in bright, indirect light.</li>
    <li>Transplant to soil once roots are 2+ inches long for best success rate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common Problems</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Pink color fading: Usually caused by insufficient light, overwatering, or excessive fertilization.</li>
    <li>Yellow leaves: Typically indicates overwatering, root rot, or natural leaf aging.</li>
    <li>Brown leaf edges: Results from low humidity, fluoride in water, or over-fertilizing.</li>
    <li>Drooping leaves: Usually caused by underwatering, being root bound, or temperature stress.</li>
    <li>Leggy growth: Indicates insufficient light or natural climbing behavior requiring support.</li>
    <li>Spider mites, aphids, and mealybugs can be treated with increased humidity and insecticidal soap.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Pink Arrowhead is relatively easy to care for and suitable for beginners.</li>
    <li>Rotate the plant weekly for even growth and coloration throughout the foliage.</li>
    <li>Clean leaves monthly with a damp cloth to improve light absorption and plant health.</li>
    <li>Group with other tropical plants to create a humid microclimate naturally.</li>
    <li>Pinch growing tips regularly to encourage bushier, more compact growth habit.</li>
    <li>Watch for seasonal changes as growth naturally slows during winter months.</li>
    <li>Keep away from pets and children as the plant contains toxic calcium oxalate crystals.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 50.00, 80.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 0, true, '2025-09-08 17:12:23.499313+00', '2026-03-05 03:46:54.303725+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/SyngoniumPinkArrowhead%2FSyngoniumPinkArrowhead1.jpeg?alt=media&token=edff1725-5818-4e39-bb88-7370df9f4b91', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (22, 'Syngonium Tiffany', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Syngonium Tiffany Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Syngonium Tiffany Care Guide</h1>

<p>Syngonium Tiffany (Syngonium podophyllum ''Tiffany'') is a stunning variegated climbing vine native to Central and South America. This captivating variety is prized for its arrow-shaped leaves that display beautiful cream, white, and green variegation with subtle pink undertones. As the plant matures, it transitions from compact juvenile leaves to larger, lobed adult foliage when provided with climbing support. The high variegation makes this cultivar particularly sought after among houseplant enthusiasts.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Tiffany requires bright, indirect light to maintain its striking variegation.</li>
    <li>Higher light levels enhance the cream and white markings while preventing reversion to green.</li>
    <li>Can tolerate medium light but variegation may fade and growth may slow significantly.</li>
    <li>Avoid direct sunlight as it can scorch the delicate variegated leaves and cause browning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water when the top 1-2 inches of soil feel dry, typically every 7-10 days in growing season.</li>
    <li>Maintain consistent moisture but avoid waterlogged conditions that can cause root rot.</li>
    <li>Use filtered or distilled water when possible as variegated plants can be sensitive to chemicals.</li>
    <li>Reduce watering frequency in winter when growth naturally slows down.</li>
    <li>Water thoroughly until excess drains from drainage holes, then empty saucers.</li>
    <li>Yellow leaves typically indicate overwatering while crispy edges suggest underwatering.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 supplementation is not necessary for this houseplant.</li>
    <li>Feed every 2-4 weeks during spring and summer with balanced liquid fertilizer.</li>
    <li>Use diluted fertilizer (half strength) to prevent burning the sensitive variegated foliage.</li>
    <li>Reduce feeding to monthly or stop completely during fall and winter dormancy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requires well-draining, airy potting mix to prevent root rot and promote healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Ideal soil blend: 40% potting soil, 20% perlite, 20% orchid bark, 20% coconut coir.</li>
    <li>Choose pots with adequate drainage holes to prevent water accumulation.</li>
    <li>Repot annually in spring, increasing pot size by 1-2 inches in diameter.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Tiffany is a fast-growing climbing vine when provided with support.</li>
    <li>Features arrow-shaped juvenile leaves with cream, white, and green variegation.</li>
    <li>Mature leaves become larger and more lobed when the plant is allowed to climb.</li>
    <li>Can reach 3-6 feet indoors with proper support and care.</li>
    <li>High variegation makes this cultivar more light-sensitive than solid green varieties.</li>
    <li>Pink undertones may appear in optimal lighting conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stem cuttings root easily in water or moist potting mix.</li>
    <li>Cut 4-6 inch stems with at least 2-3 nodes for best success rates.</li>
    <li>Remove lower leaves and place cuttings in water or directly into moist soil.</li>
    <li>Water propagation typically shows roots in 2-4 weeks under bright, indirect light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Tiffany requires more attention than solid green varieties due to high variegation.</li>
    <li>Provide moss poles or trellises to encourage climbing and larger leaf development.</li>
    <li>Maintain humidity levels of 50-60% to prevent brown leaf edges and promote health.</li>
    <li>Pinch growing tips regularly to encourage bushier, more compact growth.</li>
    <li>The plant is toxic to pets and children due to calcium oxalate crystals.</li>
    <li>Temperature range of 65-80°F (18-27°C) is ideal for optimal growth and variegation.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 50.00, 80.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 10, true, '2025-09-08 17:17:10.721789+00', '2025-12-25 07:21:43.435781+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/SyngoniumTiffany%2FSyngoniumTiffany3.jpeg?alt=media&token=3aa67011-fd9a-4c64-aea5-6f8d24fb3933', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (37, 'Duck Weed', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Duckweed Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Duckweed (Lemna minor) Care Guide</h1>

<p>Duckweed is one of the smallest and fastest-growing flowering plants in the world. Often appearing as a vibrant green carpet on the surface of ponds and slow-moving rivers, it is a floating aquatic plant that is both loved for its nitrate-sucking abilities and notorious for how quickly it can take over an aquarium. It serves as an excellent natural water purifier and a nutritious snack for many types of fish.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for (or manage) Duckweed:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is highly adaptable and can grow in almost any lighting condition, from low to high intensity.</li>
    <li>Under high-intensity lighting, the growth rate becomes explosive, and the plant will double its biomass in a matter of days.</li>
    <li>It does best with 8-10 hours of light; however, if the light is too dim, the leaves may become smaller and pale yellow.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is incredibly hardy and can survive in a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 50-86°F (10-30°C). {Link: According to www.aquariumcoop.com https://www.aquariumcoop.com/blogs/aquarium/duckweed}, it can tolerate temperatures near freezing but thrives in warm, tropical water.</li>
    <li>pH range: 5.0-9.0. It is extremely tolerant of various pH levels.</li>
    <li>It prefers still or very slow-moving surface water. Strong surface agitation or heavy bubbles from an air stone can push the plants underwater, causing them to die and rot.</li>
    <li>It is an excellent bioindicator; if the duckweed starts to die, it is often a sign of a significant issue with water quality or nutrient depletion.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not necessary for duckweed because it has direct access to atmospheric CO2 as a floating plant.</li>
    <li>Duckweed is a "nutrient sponge," meaning it is exceptionally efficient at absorbing nitrates, phosphates, and potassium from the water column.</li>
    <li>In tanks with low fish stock, you may need to add a liquid fertilizer to keep it green, as it can quickly strip the water of all available nutrients.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is a strictly floating plant and does not require any substrate.</li>
    <li>To "plant" it, simply place the individual fronds on the surface of the water.</li>
    <li>It is recommended to use a floating "airline tubing ring" if you wish to contain it in one area of the tank and prevent it from blocking light to plants below.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>The plant consists of tiny, oval-shaped green fronds, usually no larger than 5mm in length.</li>
    <li>Each frond has a single, thin root hanging down into the water to stabilize the plant and absorb nutrients.</li>
    <li>It reproduces rapidly through vegetative budding, where new fronds grow out of the sides of the parent plant and then break off.</li>
    <li>It can form a thick mat that blocks out all light to the bottom of the tank, which can be detrimental to submerged plants if not thinned out regularly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation requires no effort from the hobbyist. As long as there are nutrients and light, the plant will propagate itself indefinitely.</li>
    <li>If you wish to move it to another tank, simply scoop a handful out and drop it onto the new water surface.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is an excellent way to control algae because it outcompetes algae for nutrients and shades the water.</li>
    <li>Many fish, such as Goldfish, Tilapia, and some Cichlids, love to eat duckweed; it is a great source of protein and fiber.</li>
    <li>Be warned: once introduced, duckweed is very difficult to completely remove from a tank, as a single missed frond can restart the entire colony.</li>
    <li>Regular harvesting is necessary. Use a net to scoop out excess duckweed weekly to ensure that light can still reach your midground and background plants.</li>
    <li>It can be dried and processed into a high-quality homemade fish food flake or pellet.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10.00, 10.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 5, true, '2025-12-21 18:01:05.157463+00', '2025-12-21 18:01:05.15748+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/DuckWeed%2FDuckWeed.jpg?alt=media&token=31b046f8-1481-41ab-9b88-30a025e1d17a', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (8, 'Bucephalandra Mini Melawi (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Mini Melawi" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Mini Melawi" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Mini Melawi" is a beautiful and relatively small variety of Bucephalandra, native to the island of Borneo. It''s known for its compact size, making it a good choice for smaller aquariums or as a foreground or midground plant in larger setups. </p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Mini Melawi":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Mini Melawi" can thrive in low light conditions, but it will display better coloration and growth with medium lighting.</li>
    <li>It''s important to be mindful of algae growth on the leaves, especially if using higher light levels, as Bucephalandra are slow growers.</li>
    <li>Target a PAR (photosynthetically active radiation) level below 50 umols for easy management, though they can tolerate higher levels if your tank is very clean and the plants are healthy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Similar to other Bucephalandra varieties, "Mini Melawi" prefers stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (21-26°C). {Link: According to www.pudr.ca https://www.pudr.ca/product-page/bucephalandra-melawi-mini}, a temperature range of 20-24°C (68-75°F) is recommended for Bucephalandra Melawi Mini.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0. Some sources recommend a narrower range of 6-7.5.</li>
    <li>They appreciate good water flow, similar to their natural habitat in fast-moving streams in Borneo.</li>
    <li>Clean, well-filtered water is crucial for healthy growth and preventing melting.</li>
    <li>Some hardness (GH/KH) is beneficial, with a dGH of 5 or above being helpful.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly necessary but will significantly enhance growth rate and coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer like Easy Green can be beneficial.</li>
    <li>Adequate fertilization, especially with a balanced NPK + Fe (nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium + iron) fertilizer, will help the plant thrive and showcase its vibrant colors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Mini Melawi" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape, such as rocks or driftwood, using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If you choose to place it in the substrate, ensure the roots are covered but the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Mini Melawi" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It features smaller, more compact leaves compared to its cousin Bucephalandra "Melawi".</li>
    <li>The leaves are often described as dense, narrow, and short-stalked, with a slightly wavy margin and a rounded tip that has a small point.</li>
    <li>Younger leaves may display a coppery color with iridescent green hues, while older leaves are typically a dark green.</li>
    <li>The underside of the leaf is often silvery white with a brownish-red central vein.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Similar to other Bucephalandra varieties, "Mini Melawi" can be propagated by carefully cutting the rhizome into sections, ensuring each piece has a healthy root and a few leaves.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape as you would with a new plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra is generally considered easy to care for, making "Mini Melawi" a good choice for beginners.</li>
    <li>However, they are sensitive to drastic changes in water parameters, so aim for stability.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when introduced to a new environment, especially in uncycled tanks, but the plant usually recovers and produces new growth from the rhizome.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, decaying, or melting leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>They are naturally found in areas with good water flow, so consider incorporating a pump or filter with strong outflow to mimic their natural habitat.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra can also be grown emersed (out of water) in paludariums or terrariums with high humidity.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 250.00, 550.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 0, true, '2025-07-09 07:40:57.712204+00', '2026-03-05 03:59:39.188385+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/mini-melawi%2Fminimelawi01.jpeg?alt=media&token=607a37e0-cb24-4938-9bf4-609656bdc935', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/mini-melawi%2Fminimelawi01.jpeg?alt=media&token=607a37e0-cb24-4938-9bf4-609656bdc935');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (31, 'Bucephalandra Splendor Purple (One rhizome)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" is a breathtaking and relatively rare variety of Bucephalandra native to the rainforests of Borneo. It is celebrated by aquascapers for its intense, dark violet tones and narrow, elegant leaves. This variety is particularly prized for its ability to maintain a deep purple hue even under moderate conditions, making it a "splendid" centerpiece for high-end aquascapes.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" thrives in medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate low light, the "purple" brilliance of the leaves is most pronounced under medium to high-intensity full-spectrum lighting.</li>
    <li>Be cautious of algae; because this plant grows slowly, high light without adequate CO2 can lead to Green Spot Algae on the leaf surfaces.</li>
    <li>A photoperiod of 6-8 hours is usually sufficient to maintain color and health.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Consistency is vital; "Splendor Purple" is sensitive to large fluctuations in water chemistry.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (22-26°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/bucephalandra-splendor-purple}, keeping the water on the cooler side of the tropical range helps prevent rhizome rot.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers soft, slightly acidic water which mimics its natural forest stream habitat.</li>
    <li>Strong water movement is recommended to ensure oxygen and nutrients reach the plant and to prevent debris from settling on the foliage.</li>
    <li>Maintain a GH of 5 or above to provide necessary minerals for leaf development.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly recommended to bring out the deepest purple and blue iridescence.</li>
    <li>Without CO2, the plant may lean more toward a dark olive green with only subtle purple highlights.</li>
    <li>Regular liquid fertilization is essential, as this plant primarily feeds from the water column.</li>
    <li>A fertilizer rich in iron (Fe) and micronutrients will help sustain the vibrant, dark pigmentation of the leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" is an epiphyte and must never be buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>Burying the rhizome prevents the plant from "breathing" and will cause it to rot and die.</li>
    <li>Secure the plant to hardscape like driftwood, lava rock, or stones using super glue gel or dark cotton thread.</li>
    <li>It can be tucked into small cracks in rocks where its roots will eventually grip the surface.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is a slow-growing plant with a compact and bushy growth habit.</li>
    <li>The leaves are typically narrow and elongated with slightly wavy edges and a pointed tip.</li>
    <li>The coloration is its most famous trait: deep, velvety purple and dark indigo hues that shimmer with a metallic sheen under light.</li>
    <li>The leaf surface is often peppered with tiny white spots (stomata) that give it a "starry" appearance when submerged.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is achieved by carefully cutting the rhizome into smaller sections.</li>
    <li>Ensure each cutting has at least 3-4 leaves and a few healthy roots attached.</li>
    <li>Reattach the new sections to your hardscape. Over time, these will grow into new, dense clumps.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Splendor Purple" is prone to melting if introduced to an uncycled or unstable aquarium; wait until your tank is established before adding it.</li>
    <li>If melting occurs, remove the decaying leaves but leave the rhizome in place, as it often produces new shoots once it adapts.</li>
    <li>Using Amano shrimp or Nerite snails is highly effective for keeping the slow-growing leaves free of algae and biofilm.</li>
    <li>Regularly trim dead or yellowing leaves near the base of the rhizome to encourage the plant to put energy into new, colorful growth.</li>
    <li>It can also be grown emersed in high-humidity terrariums, though the purple color is far more intense when grown submerged.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 300.00, 1400.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 5, true, '2025-12-21 17:46:12.630458+00', '2026-03-05 13:40:39.788637+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/SplendorPurple%2Fsplendorpurple1.jpeg?alt=media&token=14ec8367-0f6c-44d6-844b-251de0c5ae06', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (5, 'Dwarf Mondo Grass', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Mondo Grass (Ophiopogon japonicus ''Kyoto Dwarf'') Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Mondo Grass (Ophiopogon japonicus ''Kyoto Dwarf'') Care Guide</h1>

  <p>Ophiopogon japonicus ''Kyoto Dwarf'', also known as Dwarf Mondo Grass or Dwarf Monkey Grass, is a compact variety of Mondo Grass highly valued for its dense, dark green foliage and low-growing habit. It''s a popular choice for ground cover, edging, and adding texture to gardens and terrariums.</p>

  <h2>1. Lighting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Low to Moderate Indirect Light:</strong> ''Kyoto Dwarf'' mondo grass prefers low to moderate indirect light.  It can tolerate lower light conditions, but brighter light can lead to more vigorous growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Direct Sunlight:</strong>  Protect the plant from direct sunlight, which can cause leaf burn.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Watering</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Moderate Moisture:</strong> Maintain moderate moisture levels in the soil. Water when the top inch of the soil feels slightly dry.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Overwatering:</strong> Ensure proper drainage to prevent waterlogging, which can lead to root rot.  Dwarf Mondo Grass is sensitive to wet soil, so overwatering is a common issue.</li>
    <li><strong>Check Soil Moisture:</strong> Some sources recommend watering when the soil feels dry to the touch.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Humidity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Moderate to High Humidity:</strong> Dwarf Mondo Grass appreciates moderate to high humidity levels.</li>
    <li><strong>Increase Humidity:</strong> Regular misting can help maintain adequate moisture, especially in dry environments like indoor terrariums.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Temperature</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Moderate Temperatures:</strong>  Keep the plant in a moderate temperature range, ideally between 15°C to 24°C (60°F to 75°F).</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature Tolerance:</strong>  It''s very temperature tolerant and can even be partially hardy in Central Europe, but overwinters more safely in frost-free conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Drafts:</strong> Protect the plant from drafts, as sudden temperature changes can stress it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Soil</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Well-Draining and Lightweight:</strong>  Plant in well-draining, lightweight soil to prevent waterlogging.</li>
    <li><strong>Well-Drained Loamy Soil:</strong>  Some recommend well-draining loamy soil that retains moisture without becoming waterlogged.</li>
    <li><strong>Improve Drainage:</strong> Adding organic matter like coco coir and perlite or vermiculite can improve drainage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Fertilization</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light Feeding:</strong> Fertilize sparingly with a balanced, water-soluble fertilizer during the growing season (spring and summer).</li>
    <li><strong>Dilute Fertilizer:</strong> Dilute the fertilizer to half or quarter strength to prevent over-fertilization.</li>
    <li><strong>Minimal Fertilization:</strong> Some sources indicate it grows slowly and doesn''t require added fertilizer, suggesting that replacing the potting soil once a year provides enough nutrition.  </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Other Care Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> Minimal pruning is typically required. Trim any yellow or damaged leaves as needed. Some sources suggest trimming in late winter or early spring to promote fresh growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Propagation:</strong> Propagate by dividing the clumps, which can be done in the spring.  You can also cut off the underground runners for propagation.</li>
    <li><strong>Pests and Diseases:</strong>  Watch for scale insects, which can cause unsightly spots.  Root rot caused by *Pythium splendens* is the most serious disease, especially in heavy soils with poor drainage.</li>
    <li><strong>Toxicity:</strong> Dwarf Mondo Grass is considered non-toxic to dogs and humans.</li>
    <li><strong>Terrarium Use:</strong> It''s an ideal choice for terrariums due to its low-growing habit and humidity tolerance.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>By providing the right care, you can enjoy the beautiful, low-maintenance foliage of your Dwarf Mondo Grass (Ophiopogon japonicus ''Kyoto Dwarf'').</p>

</body>
</html>', 30.00, 50.00, 5.0, true, false, false, false, 50, true, '2025-07-08 22:20:47.273981+00', '2025-09-08 16:57:17.502219+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/Dwarf%20Mondo%20Grass.jpeg?alt=media', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/Dwarf%20Mondo%20Grass.jpeg?alt=media');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (6, 'Goliath Silver', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Goliath Silver Aquatic Plant Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Goliath Silver Aquatic Plant Care Guide</h1>

  <p>The term "Goliath Silver aquatic plant" could refer to a few different species that are sometimes used in aquariums or aquatic setups.  Here''s a breakdown of possible candidates and their care requirements:</p>

  <h2>1. Bucephalandra ''Goliath''</h2>

  <p>This is likely the most common aquatic plant referred to as "Goliath" in the aquarium hobby. It''s known for its robust appearance and is a variety of Bucephalandra.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lighting:</strong> Low to moderate.  Excessive light can encourage algae growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong>  Prefers good water flow.</li>
    <li><strong>Planting:</strong>  Attach to hardscape (wood, rocks), as burying the rhizome can cause rot.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong>  Slow.</li>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong>  Not necessary but recommended for better growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Parameters:</strong> Adaptable to various water parameters, but stable conditions are important.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Other Potential "Goliath Silver" Aquatic Plants</h2>

  <p>While the name "Goliath Silver" isn''t a standard botanical name for a commonly known aquatic plant, it could potentially be used for other species or cultivated varieties with "silver" or "goliath" characteristics.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consider appearance:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Does the plant have silver-colored or marked leaves?</li>
        <li>Is it a larger or more robust form of a known aquatic species?</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Identify the actual species:</strong>  If you know the actual species name, you can find specific care instructions.
      <ul>
        <li>You can use resources like online plant databases or aquarium forums to help with identification. </li>
        <li>Plant identification apps like Greg or PictureThis may also be helpful, although they might not be as accurate for aquatic plants as dedicated aquarium plant resources.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>General Aquatic Plant Care Tips</h2>

  <p>If you''re unsure of your plant''s exact species, you can still follow these general guidelines for caring for most aquatic plants:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lighting:</strong> Provide appropriate lighting, generally 10 to 12 hours of full spectrum light with a Kelvin rating between 6,500K and 8,000K.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Parameters:</strong>  Maintain stable water parameters. Most aquatic plants prefer a pH between 6.5 and 7.8, general hardness of 50 ppm to 100 ppm, alkalinity between 3° and 8° dKH, and nitrates below 10 ppm and phosphates below 0.5 ppm.</li>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong>  If planting in substrate, choose fine to medium grade gravel or coarse sand. Avoid compacting fine sand and prevent accumulation of organic debris in coarse gravel.</li>
    <li><strong>Nutrients:</strong>  Aquatic plants need essential nutrients like nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, iron, magnesium, and manganese.
      <ul>
        <li>Fish food and waste provide nitrogen and phosphorus, but you may need to add supplemental minerals.</li>
        <li>Liquid fertilizers designed for aquatic plants can be used.</li>
        <li>Root tabs can be added near the roots of plants that absorb nutrients through their roots.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong>  Supplemental CO2 can enhance plant growth, but it''s not always necessary.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Changes:</strong>  Perform regular water changes to maintain water quality.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Good water flow helps distribute nutrients and prevents debris from settling on leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong>  Prune as needed to manage size and shape and remove dead leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Compatibility:</strong>  Consider compatibility with your fish, especially if you have large herbivorous species.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>By providing the right conditions and observing your plant closely, you can help it thrive in your aquarium or aquatic setup.</p>

</body>
</html>', 700.00, 950.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 0, true, '2025-07-08 22:28:17.72771+00', '2025-10-15 18:24:16.616002+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/Goliath%20Silver.webp?alt=media', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/Goliath%20Silver.webp?alt=media');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (10, 'Peperomia Green Creeper', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Peperomia Green Creeper Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Peperomia "Green Creeper" Care Guide</h1>

<p>The Peperomia "Green Creeper" is a popular and relatively easy-to-care-for houseplant known for its attractive green foliage and vining habit.  It’s a delightful addition to any indoor space, whether as a centerpiece on a table or hanging from a basket.</p>

<h2>Light</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia "Green Creeper" thrives in bright, indirect light.</li>
    <li>Place it near an east or west-facing window, ensuring it''s protected from the harsh afternoon sun, which can scorch the leaves.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate lower light, reduce watering frequency in shadier spots.</li>
    <li>Morning sun is beneficial, but avoid direct afternoon sun.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering</h2>
<ul>
    <li>As a semi-succulent, this plant doesn''t need frequent watering.</li>
    <li>Water only when the soil has dried out after the previous watering.</li>
    <li>Overwatering is detrimental and can lead to root rot and leaf drop, {Link: according to Ugaoo https://www.ugaoo.com/products/peperomia-green-creeper-plant} and {Link: YouTube https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RgCFaXlRxPw}.</li>
    <li>Ensure the pot has good drainage holes to allow excess water to escape.</li>
    <li>Allow the soil to dry slightly between waterings, but don''t let it stay dry for too long, as underwatering can also cause leaves to drop.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia "Green Creeper" prefers moderate to high humidity levels.</li>
    <li>Mist the plant occasionally or place it near a humidifier, {Link: says Ugaoo https://www.ugaoo.com/products/peperomia-green-creeper-plant}.</li>
    <li>A humidity level of 50% is generally good, but misting can help keep the leaves healthy and dust-free.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>The ideal temperature range is between 65-75°F (18-24°C).</li>
    <li>Protect the plant from cold drafts and sudden temperature fluctuations, as these can stress the plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Use a well-draining, loose soil mix.</li>
    <li>A mix containing potting soil, perlite, and potentially some cow manure or peat moss is recommended for optimal drainage and moisture retention.</li>
    <li>Avoid soil mixes that are excessively heavy with coco peat or manure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Feed your plant with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to half strength every 4-6 weeks during the growing season (spring and summer).</li>
    <li>Alternatively, apply a well-balanced liquid fertilizer once every three months, {Link: according to Plan A Plant https://planaplant.com/products/peperomia-green-creeper-set-of-5}.</li>
    <li>You can also use vermicompost weekly and seaweed monthly for greener leaves, along with Epsom salt monthly for better blooming.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pruning and Maintenance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Pruning can encourage the plant to become bushier.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing or decaying leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia "Green Creeper" is easy to propagate from stem or leaf cuttings.</li>
    <li>Simply snip a healthy piece, let it dry for a few hours (optional), and then place it in water or moist soil.</li>
    <li>Roots should develop within a few weeks.</li>
    <li>When propagating in soil, ensure it''s a good quality mix, potentially with added perlite, {Link: says a YouTube video https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=RKY5L6_dDrY&pp=ygULI3Byb3BvZ2F0aW4%3D}.</li>
    <li>Covering the pot with cling film or placing it in a sealed bag can help retain humidity and speed up the process.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common Problems</h2>
<ul>
    <li>**Yellow and floppy leaves:** Often a sign of overwatering. Water only when the soil is dry to the touch.</li>
    <li>**Brownish and dehydrated leaves:** May indicate excessive heat and lack of humidity.</li>
    <li>**Limp leaves:** Can be caused by either overwatering or underwatering.</li>
    <li>**Leggy growth or root rot:** Common problems due to poor light or overwatering. Requires proper watering, light optimization, and regular pruning.</li>
    <li>**Droopy leaves, mushy stems, and moldy soil:** Strong indicators of overwatering.</li>
    <li>**Pests:** Like many houseplants, Peperomia can be susceptible to mealybugs, spider mites, and aphids.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 50.00, 100.00, 5.0, true, false, false, true, 10, true, '2025-07-08 22:20:47.273981+00', '2025-10-01 04:48:28.515432+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/PeperomiaGreenCreeper%2FPeperomia%20Green%20Creeper.jpeg?alt=media&token=d757ddf6-7c96-4448-9756-ae20eb6e8fee', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/PeperomiaGreenCreeper%2FPeperomia%20Green%20Creeper.jpeg?alt=media&token=d757ddf6-7c96-4448-9756-ae20eb6e8fee');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (38, 'Java Fern', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Java Fern Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Java Fern (Microsorum pteropus) Care Guide</h1>

<p>Java Fern is one of the most iconic, beginner-friendly aquarium plants in the hobby. Known for its hardy nature, attractive leaf structure, and ability to thrive in low-tech setups, it is a staple in planted tanks, aquascapes, and low-maintenance aquariums. It naturally grows attached to rocks, wood, and other hard surfaces rather than in substrate, making it incredibly versatile for design.</p>

<p>Here is a complete guide to help you care for Java Fern:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Java Fern grows well in low to medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>High light is not necessary and may cause algae to grow on its leaves.</li>
    <li>Provide 6–10 hours of light daily for steady, healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Under too intense light, leaves may burn or develop black patches.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Very hardy and adaptable to a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li>Temperature: 68–82°F (20–28°C). Ideal range is around 72–78°F.</li>
    <li>pH: 6.0–7.8. Performs well in both slightly acidic and slightly alkaline water.</li>
    <li>GH/KH: Soft to moderately hard water is suitable.</li>
    <li>Tolerates low flow and medium flow, but avoid strong currents directly hitting the leaves long-term.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not required; the plant grows steadily even in non-CO2 tanks.</li>
    <li>Liquid fertilizers improve growth and coloration but are optional.</li>
    <li>Java Fern absorbs nutrients directly from the water column, so root tabs are unnecessary.</li>
    <li>If leaves appear pale or develop transparent spots, consider supplementing potassium and micronutrients.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Java Fern must NOT be buried in substrate. Burying the rhizome will cause it to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach the rhizome to hardscape such as driftwood, lava rock, or stones using super glue gel, cotton thread, or fishing line.</li>
    <li>The roots will naturally anchor over time as the plant attaches itself.</li>
    <li>Can be used as a midground or background plant depending on variety (regular, narrow leaf, trident, windelov, etc.).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Java Fern features long, textured, green leaves emerging from a horizontal rhizome.</li>
    <li>Growth rate is slow to moderate depending on lighting and nutrient availability.</li>
    <li>Dark spots on the underside of leaves are often reproductive spores, not a health issue.</li>
    <li>Leaves may grow up to 12–14 inches depending on the variety.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagates through rhizome division—simply cut the rhizome into sections, each with several leaves.</li>
    <li>Also produces plantlets on older leaves; these can be gently removed and attached to new hardscape.</li>
    <li>Baby Java Ferns grow slowly at first but become hardy once established.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Excellent plant for beginners due to its tolerance of low tech conditions.</li>
    <li>Great for aquariums with fish that uproot plants, such as cichlids or goldfish.</li>
    <li>If algae accumulates on older leaves, trim them off to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Works beautifully in river-style, jungle-style, and nature aquascapes.</li>
    <li>Do not move or disturb the plant often—Java Fern grows best when left undisturbed.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 120.00, 150.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 7, true, '2025-12-21 18:03:54.987771+00', '2025-12-21 18:03:54.987792+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/JavaFern%2FJavaFern.jpeg?alt=media&token=d630d903-be55-40c9-bf81-483a489bc66a', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (39, 'Pilea nummulariifolia (Creeping Charlie 3 Cuttings)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Pilea nummulariifolia (Creeping Charlie) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Pilea nummulariifolia (Creeping Charlie) Care Guide</h1>

<p>Pilea nummulariifolia, commonly known as "Creeping Charlie," is a lush, perennial evergreen creeper native to the Caribbean and northern South America. It is beloved for its bright green, crinkled leaves that grow along trailing reddish stems, making it a popular choice for hanging baskets, terrariums, and as a ground cover in tropical vivariums.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Pilea nummulariifolia:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Pilea nummulariifolia thrives in bright, indirect light to partial shade.</li>
    <li>Direct sunlight should be avoided as it can easily scorch the delicate, textured leaves, leading to brown patches.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium or vivarium setting, it does well under standard full-spectrum LED lighting. If the light is too low, the plant will become leggy with smaller leaves and longer stems.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This plant loves moisture and should be kept consistently damp but not soggy.</li>
    <li>Ideal humidity: 50-80%. {Link: According to www.glass-box-tropicals.com https://www.glassboxtropicals.com/pilea-nummulariifolia-creeping-charlie/}, it is an excellent candidate for humid enclosures and thrives when the air is moist.</li>
    <li>Water when the surface of the soil just begins to feel dry. It does not tolerate prolonged drought, which causes the leaves to wilt and drop.</li>
    <li>In household environments, regular misting or a pebble tray can help maintain the necessary humidity levels.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 65-80°F (18-27°C).</li>
    <li>As a tropical plant, it is not cold-hardy and should be kept away from drafts or temperatures below 55°F (13°C).</li>
    <li>It enjoys the stable, warm temperatures typically found inside homes or enclosed glass tanks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Use a well-draining potting mix rich in organic matter. A blend of peat moss, perlite, and standard potting soil works well.</li>
    <li>In terrariums, it grows easily in ABG (Atlanta Botanical Gardens) mix or on damp sphagnum moss.</li>
    <li>Feed every 4-6 weeks during the spring and summer growing season with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to half strength.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Pilea nummulariifolia is a fast-growing, trailing vine that can quickly form a dense mat.</li>
    <li>The leaves are round (nummular), deeply veined, and have a puckered or "quilted" texture that adds significant visual interest.</li>
    <li>The stems are slightly fleshy and can root wherever a node touches moist soil, allowing it to "creep" across surfaces effectively.</li>
    <li>It can be used as a cascading plant in hanging pots or as a lush green background/carpet in a vivarium.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is incredibly simple through stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Take a 3-4 inch cutting that includes at least two nodes and place it in water or directly into moist soil or moss.</li>
    <li>Roots usually form within a few days to a week. Once a root system is established, the plant will begin to produce new foliage rapidly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Regular pruning is often necessary to keep the plant contained, especially in smaller terrariums where it can outcompete other species.</li>
    <li>Ensure good air circulation if humidity is very high to prevent fungal issues or leaf rot.</li>
    <li>Pilea nummulariifolia is generally non-toxic to cats and dogs, making it a safe choice for pet-friendly homes.</li>
    <li>If the plant becomes too thin or leggy at the top, pinch back the growing tips to encourage a bushier growth habit.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 30.00, 70.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 5, true, '2025-12-21 18:05:31.180292+00', '2025-12-21 19:56:33.30011+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/PileaNummulariifolia%2FPileaNummulariifolia.jpeg?alt=media&token=5cb9b25c-d6cb-41d3-819c-7594987402ae', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (21, 'Peperomia caperata', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Peperomia caperata Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Peperomia caperata Care Guide</h1>

<p>Peperomia caperata, commonly known as Emerald Ripple Peperomia or Radiator Plant, is a charming compact houseplant native to Brazil. This popular species is prized for its distinctive heart-shaped leaves with deeply ridged, quilted texture that creates beautiful ripple patterns. The leaves are typically dark green with silvery veining, though many cultivars exist with variations in color including red, burgundy, and variegated forms.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia caperata thrives in bright, indirect light conditions.</li>
    <li>Can tolerate medium light but may lose some vibrant coloration and compact growth.</li>
    <li>Avoid direct sunlight as it can scorch the delicate leaves and fade their patterns.</li>
    <li>Insufficient light causes elongated stems and loss of the characteristic compact form.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water when the top 1-2 inches of soil are dry, typically every 7-10 days.</li>
    <li>Peperomia caperata stores water in its thick leaves making it drought-tolerant.</li>
    <li>Water at soil level to avoid getting water on the textured leaves which can cause fungal issues.</li>
    <li>Ensure thorough drainage as waterlogged soil quickly leads to root rot.</li>
    <li>Reduce watering frequency in winter when growth naturally slows down.</li>
    <li>Overwatering is the most common cause of plant failure with this species.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 supplementation is not necessary for this houseplant.</li>
    <li>Feed monthly during spring and summer with diluted liquid fertilizer.</li>
    <li>Use balanced fertilizer (10-10-10) diluted to half strength to prevent burning.</li>
    <li>Reduce feeding to every 2-3 months during fall and winter dormancy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requires extremely well-draining, lightweight potting mix to prevent root rot.</li>
    <li>Ideal soil blend: 50% quality potting soil, 30% perlite, 20% orchid bark.</li>
    <li>Choose pots with drainage holes; terracotta pots help prevent overwatering.</li>
    <li>Shallow pots are preferred as it has a small, fibrous root system.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia caperata is a slow-growing, compact plant.</li>
    <li>Features heart-shaped leaves with deeply ridged, quilted texture.</li>
    <li>Leaves are typically 2-4 inches long with distinctive ripple patterns.</li>
    <li>Can reach a height and spread of 8-10 inches when mature.</li>
    <li>Produces small, cream-colored flower spikes on healthy plants.</li>
    <li>Many cultivars available with varying leaf colors from green to burgundy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Leaf cuttings are the most successful propagation method.</li>
    <li>Cut healthy leaves with petioles attached for best results.</li>
    <li>Allow cut surfaces to dry for 24 hours before planting.</li>
    <li>Root in well-draining mix under bright, indirect light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia caperata is relatively easy to care for and beginner-friendly.</li>
    <li>Sensitive to overwatering, so err on the side of underwatering.</li>
    <li>Remove yellowing or damaged leaves promptly to prevent disease spread.</li>
    <li>Clean leaves gently with a soft brush to remove dust from ridged surfaces.</li>
    <li>The plant is non-toxic to pets and children, making it household safe.</li>
    <li>Temperature range of 65-75°F (18-24°C) is ideal for growth.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 100.00, 200.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 4, true, '2025-09-08 17:13:52.580373+00', '2026-03-05 03:46:46.758464+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/PeperomiaCaperata%2FPeperomia%20caperata.jpeg?alt=media&token=d4c88e3a-fcb5-4ac7-8976-6e8995aa0953', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (16, 'Bucephalandra Star Glitz (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz", sometimes referred to as Bucephalandra Starglitz, is a beautiful and captivating variety of Bucephalandra native to Borneo. It''s known for its dark green, wavy-edged leaves, often exhibiting speckles and sometimes brown tints on new leaves, adding sparkle to your aquascape,. Like other Bucephalandra, it is a slow-growing epiphyte that is relatively easy to care for.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Star Glitz" thrives in low to moderate lighting conditions,.</li>
    <li>It can tolerate low light, but moderate to high lighting may enhance its coloration and growth rate,,.</li>
    <li>Be cautious with high lighting levels, as this slow-growing plant is susceptible to algae growth on its leaves,. You might consider placing it in shaded areas to help prevent algae issues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz" prefers stable water conditions,,.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (21-26°C), although a wider range of 70–82°F (21–28°C) is generally tolerated,,. Some varieties may do better in cooler water temperatures below 80°F (27°C), {Link: according to The 2Hr Aquarist https://www.2hraquarist.com/blogs/freshwater-aquarium-plants-guide/how-to-grow-bucephalandra}.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0,,.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, mimicking its natural habitat in fast-moving streams,.</li>
    <li>Maintain clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes to prevent the buildup of waste products.
    <li>Some hardness (GH/KH) is helpful, with a dGH of 5 or above being recommended.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly necessary for survival, but it will significantly enhance growth and coloration,,.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer is beneficial,.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer, such as APT Complete, will support healthy growth and vibrant colors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot,,.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape elements like rocks or driftwood using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread,,.</li>
    <li>It can also be grown on a coarse gravel substrate, provided the rhizome remains exposed and only the roots are covered.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz" is a very slow-growing plant,.</li>
    <li>It features dark green leaves, 2-4 cm wide and 4-6 cm long, often with wavy edges and speckles. New leaves may have a brown tint.
    <li>Like many Bucephalandra species, it can be grown submerged or emersed.</li>
    <li>The final size, coloration, and growth rate depend on the aquarium conditions and care provided.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz" can be propagated by cutting the rhizome into sections,.</li>
    <li>Each section should have healthy roots and at least one growing tip or a few leaves for best results,.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape as you would with a new plant. Bare rhizomes can also produce new shoots if healthy and provided with good conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Star Glitz" is considered an easy plant to care for, even for beginners,.</li>
    <li>Maintain stable water parameters to prevent melting and rotting,. Melting can occur when the plant adjusts to a new environment, but it will usually recover and produce new growth from a healthy rhizome,.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any dead, yellowing, or decaying leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial for distributing nutrients and preventing detritus buildup,.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra species can be grown emersed (out of water) in humid environments like paludariums or terrariums,.</li>
    <li>Algae growth on the leaves can be a problem due to the slow growth rate and large surface area of the leaves, especially in tanks with strong lighting and unbalanced parameters,.</li>
    <li>Grazing tank mates like Otocinclus catfish, Stiphodon gobies, Siamese algae eaters, Amano shrimp, and Nerite snails can help keep the leaves clean.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 250.00, 350.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 0, true, '2025-07-09 07:40:57.712204+00', '2026-03-05 04:00:19.015568+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/star-glitz%2Fstargliz01.jpeg?alt=media&token=c135db46-373e-4c31-9c88-398b99207ebf', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/star-glitz%2Fstargliz01.jpeg?alt=media&token=c135db46-373e-4c31-9c88-398b99207ebf');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (12, 'Bucephalandra Pygmea (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" (also often spelled "Pygmaea" or "Pygmy") is a small, slow-growing species of Bucephalandra native to Borneo. It''s valued for its diminutive size, often only 2-4 inches (5-10 cm) tall, and its wavy green leaves that grow in a rosette formation, making it a perfect addition to nano tanks and intricate aquascapes.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" thrives in low to moderate lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While low light is sufficient for its survival, moderate lighting can enhance its coloration and growth.</li>
    <li>However, be cautious with high lighting levels as it is a slow grower and susceptible to algae growth on its leaves.</li>
    <li> Some varieties, like ''Wavy green'', require moderate to high lighting, but it''s important to balance light intensity and duration to avoid stressing the plants or encouraging algae growth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" prefers stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 71-82.5°F (21.5-28°C) or 72-82°F (22-28°C).</li>
    <li>pH range: 5.5-7.5 or 6.0-7.5.</li>
    <li>Maintain clean, well-filtered water with proper flow. Bucephalandra species do better in stable tanks with matured biological systems.</li>
    <li>Some hardness (GH) is beneficial; preferably 5 GH and above.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not mandatory for survival but can significantly enhance growth and coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer can be beneficial.</li>
    <li>Regular dosing of a comprehensive liquid fertilizer will support healthy development.</li>
    <li>Adequate fertilization is important, especially with a balanced NPK + Fe (nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium + iron) fertilizer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape, such as rocks or driftwood, using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If you choose to place it in the substrate, ensure only the roots are covered and the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It typically reaches a size of 2-4 inches (5-10 cm) tall.</li>
    <li>Features wavy, green leaves that grow in a rosette formation.</li>
    <li>Can exhibit rich, dark green hues, and some varieties may have subtle red or brown undertones.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by dividing the rhizome into sections.</li>
    <li>Each section should have healthy roots and at least one growing tip.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape as you would with a new plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra is generally considered easy to care for.</li>
    <li>Maintain stable water parameters to prevent melting.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when introduced to a new environment, especially in uncycled tanks, but the plant usually recovers and produces new growth from the rhizome if the rhizome remains healthy.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, decaying, or melting leaves.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 250.00, 550.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 20, true, '2025-07-09 07:40:57.712204+00', '2026-03-05 13:39:18.109948+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/pygmea%2Fpygmea01.jpeg?alt=media&token=2906af07-bdce-4df8-b044-a47c90dd51b0', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/pygmea%2Fpygmea01.jpeg?alt=media&token=2906af07-bdce-4df8-b044-a47c90dd51b0');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (3, 'Aridarum Narrow', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Aridarum Narrow Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Aridarum Narrow Care Guide</h1>

  <p>Aridarum Narrow is a rare and unique plant that thrives in fast-flowing currents of waterfalls and streams in its natural habitat, which is the island of Borneo. It can be grown submerged in an aquarium or emersed (above water) in a terrarium or paludarium.</p>

  <h2>General Care Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lighting:</strong> Low to medium lighting is preferred.  Excessive light can encourage algae growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Flow:</strong> Aridarum Narrow does well with good water flow, similar to its natural habitat in fast-moving streams.</li>
    <li><strong>Planting:</strong> This is an epiphytic plant, meaning it grows attached to surfaces like driftwood or rocks, not planted in the substrate.  Burying the rhizome can cause it to rot and die. You can attach it using thread, super glue, or by wedging it into crevices.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Aridarum Narrow is known for its slow growth rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Propagation:</strong>  Propagate by cutting the rhizome and attaching the sections to hardscape.</li>
    <li><strong>CO2:</strong> CO2 injection is recommended for better growth, especially in aquarium settings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Specific Care for Aquariums</h2>
  <ul>
      <li><strong>Water Parameters:</strong> Stable water parameters are essential for healthy growth.  Avoid drastic changes, which can lead to melting or rotting.</li>
      <li><strong>Rhizome Placement:</strong> Never bury the rhizome in the substrate; it should be exposed and attached to hardscape.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Specific Care for Terrariums/Paludariums</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> High humidity (80% or more) is crucial for this plant.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> It grows beautifully when attached to a rock or the back wall of a terrarium.</li>
    <li><strong>Moisture:</strong>  Keep the plant permanently moist, but it can tolerate short periods of drought.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Fertilize sparingly with a diluted orchid fertilizer during the growing season.</li>
    <li><strong>Light:</strong>  Aridarum Narrow prefers a semi-shaded location in emersed setups.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Important Notes</h2>
  <ul>
      <li>Aridarum Narrow is similar in care to Bucephalandra and Java Fern species.</li>
      <li>Ensure you remove the plant from its pot and any cotton surrounding the roots before placing it in your setup.</li>
      <li>Do not treat it like a traditional rooted aquatic plant; the rhizome needs to be above the substrate.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>By following these guidelines, you can successfully cultivate this unique and visually appealing plant in your aquarium or terrarium setup.</p>

</body>
</html>', 1500.00, 3000.00, 5.0, true, true, false, true, 5, true, '2025-07-08 19:17:44.162277+00', '2026-03-05 13:40:04.716459+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/aridarumNarrow_1.jpeg?alt=media&token=dac76d4c-fef7-4aa4-90de-1bcd122f5598', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/aridarumNarrow_1.jpeg?alt=media&token=dac76d4c-fef7-4aa4-90de-1bcd122f5598');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (4, 'Asparagus Fern', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Asparagus Fern (Asparagus Plumosus) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Asparagus Fern (Asparagus Plumosus) Care Guide</h1>

  <p>The Asparagus Fern, scientifically known as <em>Asparagus setaceus</em> (and often referred to as <em>Asparagus plumosus</em> or Lace Fern), is a popular houseplant known for its delicate, feathery foliage. Despite its common name, it''s not a true fern but rather a member of the asparagus family.  Here''s a guide to help you care for this attractive plant:</p>

  <h2>1. Lighting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bright, Indirect Light:</strong> Asparagus ferns thrive in bright, indirect light or dappled shade. Direct sunlight can scorch the delicate foliage.</li>
    <li><strong>Morning Sun:</strong> A few hours of gentle morning sun can be beneficial.</li>
    <li><strong>Low Light Tolerance:</strong> They can tolerate lower light conditions, but growth may slow down, and the plant might become sparse or yellow over time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Watering</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consistent Moisture:</strong>  Keep the soil consistently moist, but avoid overwatering, which can lead to root rot.</li>
    <li><strong>Check Soil Moisture:</strong> Water when the top 2-3 cm (1 inch) of soil feels dry.</li>
    <li><strong>Thorough Watering:</strong> Water thoroughly until excess water drains from the pot''s drainage holes.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduce in Winter:</strong> Reduce watering frequency in winter when growth slows.</li>
    <li><strong>Tepid Water:</strong> Use tepid (room temperature) water to avoid shocking the roots.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Humidity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>High Humidity Preferred:</strong> Asparagus ferns appreciate higher humidity levels.</li>
    <li><strong>Increase Humidity:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Mist the leaves regularly, especially in dry conditions.</li>
        <li>Use a room humidifier.</li>
        <li>Place the pot on a tray filled with pebbles and water.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Prevent Browning:</strong> Higher humidity helps prevent the frond tips from turning brown.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Temperature</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Optimal Range:</strong>  Asparagus ferns grow best in temperatures between 18°C to 27°C (65°F to 80°F).</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Cold:</strong>  Never expose the plant to temperatures below 10°C (50°F). Avoid cold drafts and sudden temperature fluctuations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Soil</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Well-Draining and Rich:</strong>  Use a well-draining, organically rich soil. A mixture of peat moss, perlite, and potting soil works well.</li>
    <li><strong>Slightly Acidic to Neutral:</strong>  A slightly acidic to neutral soil pH (6.5-6.8) is ideal.</li>
    <li><strong>Ensure Drainage:</strong>  Good drainage is important to prevent waterlogged roots.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Fertilization</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Diluted Liquid Fertilizer:</strong> During the growing season (spring and summer), fertilize monthly with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to half strength.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduce in Winter:</strong> Reduce or stop fertilization during the winter months when the plant''s growth slows down.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid Over-fertilizing:</strong> Over-fertilizing can damage the plant.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Other Care Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong>  Regularly prune to maintain shape and remove dead or yellowing fronds. Use sharp, sterile shears. Wear gloves, as the plant has small, sharp spikes.</li>
    <li><strong>Propagation:</strong>  Propagate by dividing the tuberous roots in spring.  Alternatively, you can grow them from seeds found in the red berries, but this is a slower process.</li>
    <li><strong>Repotting:</strong> Repot every 1-2 years or when the plant becomes root-bound. Use a pot with drainage holes.</li>
    <li><strong>Pests:</strong> Asparagus ferns can be susceptible to pests like spider mites and mealybugs. Inspect regularly and treat infestations promptly with insecticidal soap or neem oil.</li>
    <li><strong>Toxicity:</strong>  Asparagus ferns are toxic to both dogs and cats. Ingestion of the berries can cause vomiting, diarrhea, and/or abdominal pain, while repeated dermal exposure to the sap can lead to allergic dermatitis.</li>
    <li><strong>Overwintering:</strong>  If growing outdoors in a cooler climate, bring potted asparagus ferns inside when temperatures drop below 55°F (13°C).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>With proper care, your Asparagus Fern can thrive and bring its beautiful, feathery foliage to your home or garden.</p>

</body>
</html>', 150.00, 250.00, 5.0, false, true, true, false, 3, true, '2025-07-08 19:35:16.001345+00', '2026-03-05 03:48:48.274905+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/Asparagus%20Fern-%20Asparagus%20Plumosus.jpeg?alt=media', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/Asparagus%20Fern-%20Asparagus%20Plumosus.jpeg?alt=media');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (35, 'Bucephalandra Red Chilli ( 3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" is one of the most vibrant and iconic varieties within the Bucephalandra genus. Native to the riverbanks of Borneo, it is famous for its narrow, wavy leaves and its ability to develop a stunning deep red or maroon coloration when grown under optimal conditions. Its fiery appearance makes it a standout choice for adding warm tones to an otherwise green aquascape.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Red Chilli":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" can be grown in low light, but its namesake red coloration is only achieved with medium to high-intensity lighting.</li>
    <li>Full-spectrum lighting is essential for bringing out the deep reds and purples; high-quality RGB LEDs are particularly effective for this variety.</li>
    <li>Maintain a consistent photoperiod of 7-9 hours. Be cautious with high light levels, as the slow growth rate makes the leaves a target for algae like Green Spot Algae (GSA).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like most Bucephalandra, "Red Chilli" thrives in very stable and clean water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 70-78°F (21-26°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/bucephalandra-red-chilli}, keeping the water within this range prevents stress and potential melting of the rhizome.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers slightly acidic water but can adapt to neutral levels if the parameters remain steady.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is vital to prevent debris from accumulating on the small, textured leaves.</li>
    <li>A General Hardness (dGH) between 4-10 is recommended for healthy leaf development.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly recommended to accelerate the slow growth rate and enhance the "Red Chilli" pigmentation.</li>
    <li>Regular liquid fertilization is crucial. It is a water-column feeder that responds well to consistent doses of iron (Fe) and micronutrients.</li>
    <li>A balanced fertilization routine helps the plant maintain its vibrant colors and prevents the older leaves from becoming pale or yellowing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" is an epiphyte and should never have its rhizome buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>If the rhizome is covered by soil or sand, it will rot, leading to the death of the plant.</li>
    <li>Attach the plant to hardscape elements like lava rock, bogwood, or stones using cyanoacrylate super glue gel or fishing line.</li>
    <li>It is an excellent choice for being placed in the foreground or tucked into the midground crevices of an aquascape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is a slow to medium grower with a compact growth habit.</li>
    <li>The leaves are narrow and lanceolate with significantly wavy (ruffled) margins.</li>
    <li>New leaves typically emerge as a bright red or bronze, eventually maturing into a deep, dark red or purplish-green.</li>
    <li>When submerged and healthy, the leaves display numerous tiny white "galaxy" spots (stomata) that catch the light beautifully.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is straightforward: divide the rhizome using sharp, clean scissors or a scalpel.</li>
    <li>Each piece of divided rhizome should have at least 3-4 leaves and some healthy root growth to ensure survival.</li>
    <li>Secure the new cuttings to hardscape and allow them to establish themselves in a high-flow area.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Red Chilli" is prone to melting if there are sudden shifts in water chemistry or temperature. Avoid introducing it to newly set-up, uncycled tanks.</li>
    <li>If you notice the leaves melting, trim them back but leave the rhizome attached to the hardscape; it will often recover and grow new, adapted leaves.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any decaying matter from the tank to keep the water quality high, which is essential for "Red Chilli."</li>
    <li>Adding Amano shrimp, Otocinclus, or Nerite snails is highly beneficial for keeping the slow-growing leaves free of algae.</li>
    <li>It can also be grown emersed in high-humidity terrariums, though the leaves will generally stay more green and less vibrant than the submerged form.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 250.00, 500.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 10, true, '2025-12-21 17:53:57.17496+00', '2026-03-05 03:44:24.771392+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/RedChilli%2Fredchilli1.jpeg?alt=media&token=ad024d88-eaa9-4b43-ad15-7ac8fc9c8c11', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (11, 'Creeping fig (Ficus pumila)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Creeping Fig Plant (Ficus pumila) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Creeping Fig Plant (Ficus pumila) Care Guide</h1>

<p>The Creeping Fig, also known as Ficus pumila, climbing fig, or fig ivy, is a versatile and fast-growing evergreen vine native to East Asia. It''s renowned for its ability to cling to surfaces like walls, fences, and trellises, creating a lush green backdrop, and is also popular as a houseplant or groundcover. Its small, heart-shaped leaves and vigorous growth make it a captivating addition to various settings.</p>

<h2>Light</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Creeping Fig prefers bright, indirect light when grown indoors. </li>
    <li>Place it near an east or north-facing window to receive morning light, avoiding the intense afternoon sun which can scorch the leaves.</li>
    <li>Outdoors, it thrives in partial sun or dappled shade.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate full sun once established, protect it from the most intense afternoon sun, especially in hot climates.</li>
    <li>Signs of too much sun include scorched or burnt leaf edges, increased leaf drop, and pale leaves.</li>
    <li>Signs of too little light include leggy growth, smaller leaves, and a loss of vibrant green color.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Creeping Fig prefers consistent moisture but doesn''t like to sit in soggy soil.</li>
    <li>Water when the top inch (2.5 cm) of soil feels dry to the touch.</li>
    <li>Water more frequently during the growing season (spring and summer) and reduce watering in fall and winter when the plant''s growth slows down.</li>
    <li>Overwatering can lead to root rot and leaf drop.</li>
    <li>Underwatering can cause leaves to wilt, droop, and become dry and crispy.</li>
    <li>Good drainage is crucial, so ensure the pot has drainage holes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Creeping Fig prefers moderate to high humidity levels, ideally between 50-70%.</li>
    <li>Misting the plant occasionally or placing it near a humidifier can help increase humidity, especially in dry environments or during winter when indoor heating can dry out the air.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Creeping Fig thrives in average room temperatures, ideally between 65-75°F (18-24°C).</li>
    <li>It can tolerate temperatures as low as 55°F (13°C), but protect it from sudden temperature fluctuations and cold drafts.</li>
    <li> Its temperature tolerance ranges from -5°C (23°F) to 43°C (109°F).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Use a well-draining, fertile soil rich in organic matter.</li>
    <li>A mix of potting soil, perlite, and potentially some peat moss or compost works well.</li>
    <li>Good drainage is crucial to prevent root rot, so ensure the pot has drainage holes.</li>
    <li>The ideal soil pH is between 6.0 and 7.5.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Creeping Fig benefits from regular fertilization during its active growing season (spring and summer).</li>
    <li>Use a balanced, water-soluble fertilizer diluted to half strength every 2-4 weeks, or once a month, during the warmer months.</li>
    <li>Reduce or stop fertilizing during the cooler winter months when growth slows down.</li>
    <li>Over-fertilization can lead to leaf burn and other problems.</li>
    <li>Organic alternatives like compost tea or fish emulsion can also be used.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pruning and Maintenance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Regular pruning is essential to control its vigorous growth, maintain the desired shape, and encourage bushier foliage.</li>
    <li>Major pruning is best done in spring, but you can trim it at any time of the year to shape it.</li>
    <li>Remove any dead, yellowing, or decaying leaves regularly.</li>
    <li>Be aware that creeping fig''s suckering tendrils can damage walls and structures; consider training it on a trellis or other support to prevent this, {Link: according to Gardening Know How https://www.gardeningknowhow.com/ornamental/foliage/creeping-fig/how-to-get-creeping-fig-to-climb.htm}.</li>
    <li>Pruning can also help manage its size when grown in containers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Creeping Fig is easily propagated from stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Take 4-6 inch (10-15 cm) tip cuttings in early spring when the plant is actively growing.</li>
    <li>Remove the leaves from the lower part of the cutting and place it in water or a moist, sterile potting mix.</li>
    <li>Provide warmth, high humidity (you can use a plastic bag or propagation dome), and bright, indirect light.</li>
    <li>Roots should develop within a few weeks, and new growth will emerge.</li>
    <li>You can also propagate through layering.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Toxicity</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Creeping Fig is considered toxic to pets, including dogs, cats, and horses.</li>
    <li>The plant''s sap contains toxic elements that can cause skin irritation in humans and pets, and gastrointestinal upset (vomiting, diarrhea) if ingested.</li>
    <li>The FDA lists this species in its Database of Poisonous Plants due to the plant''s toxic sap.</li>
    <li>It''s best to wear gloves when handling the plant and keep it out of reach of pets and young children.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common problems</h2>
<ul>
    <li>**Yellowing leaves:** Often caused by overwatering or insufficient light.</li>
    <li>**Leaf drop:** Can be caused by underwatering, overwatering, sudden environmental changes, or root rot.</li>
    <li>**Brown or crispy leaves:** May indicate underwatering, low humidity, or too much direct sunlight.</li>
    <li>**Pests:** Creeping Fig can be susceptible to spider mites, mealybugs, scale insects, aphids, and whiteflies. Treat infestations with horticultural oils (like neem oil) or insecticidal soap.</li>
    <li>**Diseases:** Root rot, leaf spot diseases, and powdery mildew are the most common. Ensure good drainage, avoid overwatering, and improve air circulation to prevent these issues.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 30.00, 50.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 0, true, '2025-07-08 22:20:47.273981+00', '2026-02-16 13:35:12.005689+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/CreepingFigPlant%2FCreepingfigPlant1.jpeg?alt=media&token=4e76cedf-9c7e-4227-b1df-ae604abc4128', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/CreepingFigPlant%2FCreepingfigPlant1.jpeg?alt=media&token=4e76cedf-9c7e-4227-b1df-ae604abc4128');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (7, 'Bucephalandra Brownie (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Brownie" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Brownie" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Brownie" is a slow-growing, rhizomatous plant known for its interesting texture and coloring, making it a popular choice for planted aquariums.</p>

<p>Here''s a guide to help you care for it:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Brownie" thrives in low to medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>Higher lighting can be used to enhance colors and promote faster growth, but should be managed carefully to avoid excessive algae growth on the slow-growing leaves.</li>
    <li>Some sources recommend light intensity of around 40 PAR (micromols).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Brownie" is adaptable to a range of water parameters, but prefers stable conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (21-26°C).</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0.</li>
    <li>It appreciates good water flow, mimicking its natural river habitat in Borneo, Southeast Asia.</li>
    <li>Maintain clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes to prevent the buildup of waste products and keep the plants healthy.</li>
    <li>Note that Bucephalandra species do better in stable tanks with mature biological systems.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>While not strictly necessary, CO2 injection can significantly improve growth rate and coloration.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive plant fertilizer will ensure the plant receives the necessary nutrients.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer like Easy Green can be beneficial.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Brownie" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>You can attach it to hardscape elements like rocks or driftwood using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If you choose to plant it, ensure the roots are in the substrate but the rhizome is exposed to prevent rotting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pruning and Maintenance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, melting, or decaying leaves to encourage new growth and maintain the plant''s health.</li>
    <li>Monitor for algae growth on the leaves, especially if using higher lighting, and gently brush it off with a soft brush if necessary.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Brownie" can be propagated by carefully cutting the rhizome into sections, each with a healthy root and a few leaves for best results.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape as you would with a new plant.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> When introducing new Bucephalandra to your tank, be aware that some melting is common as the plant adjusts to the new environment and converts from its emersed form (if grown that way) to submerged growth. As long as the rhizome remains healthy, new growth should eventually appear.</p>

</body>
</html>', 250.00, 550.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 0, true, '2025-07-09 07:25:13.189286+00', '2026-03-05 04:00:29.690636+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/brownie%2Fbrownie01.jpeg?alt=media&token=7a237373-7062-4b11-8e56-432b7d64869f', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/brownie%2Fbrownie01.jpeg?alt=media&token=7a237373-7062-4b11-8e56-432b7d64869f');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (29, 'Bucephalandra Skeleton King (One rhizome)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" (also frequently known as Bucephalandra Kishii) is one of the most impressive and largest varieties of Bucephalandra. Native to the Kalimantan region of Borneo, it is famous for its dark, leathery leaves and deeply prominent veins that resemble a ribcage, giving it its "Skeleton" name.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Skeleton King":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" is adaptable but prefers low to medium lighting.</li>
    <li>High intensity lighting is not necessary and can lead to issues with Black Brush Algae (BBA) on its slow-growing, textured leaves.</li>
    <li>A PAR level between 30-50 umols is sufficient to maintain its dark, dramatic coloration and healthy leaf structure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like most large-leaf Bucephalandra, "Skeleton King" requires very stable water conditions and clean environments.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-78°F (21-26°C). {Link: According to www.aquasabi.com https://www.aquasabi.com/Bucephalandra-sp-Skeleton-King}, it is particularly sensitive to high temperatures, so keeping the water on the cooler side is recommended.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It thrives in slightly acidic water with low to moderate carbonate hardness.</li>
    <li>Good water circulation is vital to prevent detritus from settling in the deep grooves of the leaf veins.</li>
    <li>It is more sensitive to poor water quality than smaller varieties, so regular water changes are essential.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly beneficial for this variety; because it is so slow-growing, CO2 helps the plant recover from any leaf damage or melt.</li>
    <li>It responds well to regular water column fertilization, particularly with a focus on iron and potassium.</li>
    <li>Adequate nutrients help maintain the "black" or dark forest green color of the leaves and prevent the veins from yellowing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Skeleton King" is a robust epiphyte with a thick rhizome that must never be buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>Due to its larger size and weight, it should be firmly attached to large rocks or heavy driftwood using super glue gel or thick fishing line.</li>
    <li>Over time, it develops an extensive and powerful root system that will anchor itself deeply into the crevices of your hardscape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This is a very slow-growing plant, often producing only one leaf every few weeks or even months.</li>
    <li>The leaves are large, oval, and have a distinctively "rugged" or matte texture compared to the shiny leaves of other Buce varieties.</li>
    <li>The most striking feature is the deeply indented venation on the upper surface of the leaf.</li>
    <li>Colors range from a deep olive green to a nearly metallic black-purple, with a pale or reddish underside.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by dividing the thick rhizome, but should only be attempted on mature specimens.</li>
    <li>Use a very sharp, sterilized blade to ensure a clean cut through the woody rhizome.</li>
    <li>Ensure each section has at least two or three healthy leaves and a portion of the root system to support new growth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" is often considered a "collector''s plant" due to its unique look and slower growth rate.</li>
    <li>It is particularly susceptible to melting if moved between vastly different water parameters, so acclimate it slowly.</li>
    <li>Because of the deep ridges on the leaves, keeping a crew of algae-eating shrimp is highly recommended to keep the leaf surfaces clean.</li>
    <li>If the plant produces a flower (a small white spathe), it is a sign that the plant is healthy and well-established.</li>
    <li>It can be grown emersed in very high humidity, where it often grows slightly faster than its submerged counterpart.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 750.00, 1400.00, 5.0, false, false, false, true, 5, true, '2025-12-21 17:42:47.141251+00', '2026-03-05 03:45:27.277383+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/SkeletonKing%2Fskeletonking1.jpeg?alt=media&token=2b7dc598-ba85-4cdf-b0b2-0a23c61f6f25', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (17, 'Bucephalandra Catherine (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Catherine" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Catherine" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Catherine" (also known as Catherinae) is a diverse group of Bucephalandra varieties, including "Red" and "Green" forms, originating from Borneo. These plants are highly regarded in the aquarium hobby for their unique appearance, which often includes varying coloration and ruffled leaves. Like other Bucephalandra, "Catherine" varieties are slow-growing epiphytes that are relatively easy to care for.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Catherine" varieties thrive in low to moderate lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While low light is sufficient, providing moderate light can help bring out more vibrant coloration and encourage faster growth.</li>
    <li>Be mindful of high lighting levels, as Bucephalandra are slow growers and can be prone to algae buildup on their leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" prefers stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 70-82°F (21-28°C). Some sources suggest slightly cooler water (71-79°F / 21-26°C) for optimal health and to reduce stress and melting.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, as it mimics their natural habitat in streams with moderate to high flow.</li>
    <li>Maintain clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes to prevent the buildup of waste products.</li>
    <li>Some hardness (GH/KH) is helpful, with a dGH of 5 or above being recommended.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly necessary for survival, but it will significantly enhance growth and coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer is beneficial.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer will support healthy growth and vibrant colors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape elements like rocks or driftwood using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If planting in the substrate, ensure only the roots are covered and the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>The leaves can vary in size and shape depending on the specific variety (e.g., "Mini", "Red", "Green").</li>
    <li>"Catherine Red" is known for its deep green to reddish leaves, often with a ruffled appearance, and may show tiny white dots and sometimes a faint metallic blue color on submerged leaves. </li>
    <li>"Catherine Green" features narrow, wavy, emerald green leaves.</li>
    <li>"Mini Catherine" has wavy leaves and short stalks, with a dark, olive green coloration when submerged.</li>
    <li>The final size, coloration, and growth rate are dependent on the aquarium conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" can be propagated by cutting the rhizome into sections.</li>
    <li>Each section should have healthy roots and a few leaves or a growing tip for best results.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape or plant them (with the rhizome exposed) in the substrate. Bare rhizomes can also produce new shoots if healthy and provided with good conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" is generally considered easy to care for, making it suitable for beginners.</li>
    <li>However, it is sensitive to drastic changes in water parameters, so maintaining stability is key.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when introduced to a new environment or with unstable parameters, but new growth will typically emerge from a healthy rhizome.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, decaying, or melting leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is crucial for distributing nutrients and preventing detritus buildup.</li>
    <li>Algae growth on the leaves can be a problem due to the slow growth rate and large surface area of the leaves, especially in tanks with strong lighting and unbalanced parameters.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 250.00, 550.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 10, true, '2025-07-09 07:40:57.712204+00', '2026-03-05 13:39:41.771344+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/catherine%2Fcatherine01.jpg?alt=media&token=96b7e2c3-d9b7-4965-8d5d-5ece6074daba', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/catherine%2Fcatherine01.jpeg?alt=media&token=9a51efc4-d5d7-49d5-8819-75b41ccb01dc');
INSERT INTO public.core_product (id, name, description, price, compare_at_price, rating, is_new, is_sale, is_featured, is_trending, stock, is_active, created_at, updated_at, image_url, thumbnail_url) VALUES (9, 'Bucephalandra Black Cobra (3 Rhizomes)', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra" is a sought-after variety of Bucephalandra known for its distinctive dark, sometimes almost black, coloration and unique leaf shape. It is a slow-growing plant that adds a unique touch to planted aquariums.</p>

<p>Here''s a guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Black Cobra":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra" can thrive in low to medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While low light is sufficient for survival, moderate to higher lighting can help bring out its best coloration and encourage faster growth.</li>
    <li>If using higher light levels, be cautious about excessive algae growth, as Bucephalandra is a slow-growing plant and can be prone to it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra", like other Bucephalandra species, prefers stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (21-26°C).</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0. Some sources suggest a slightly narrower range of 6.0-7.5.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, mimicking its natural habitat in streams with moderate to high flow.</li>
    <li>Clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes will help prevent melting and ensure healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Maintain some water hardness (GH/KH), with a dGH of 5 or above being helpful.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly necessary but can promote faster growth and enhance coloration.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer will provide essential nutrients.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape, such as rocks or driftwood, using super glue (gel type is recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If you choose to plant it in the substrate, ensure the roots are covered but the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It typically features medium-sized leaves (4-6cm) with a rounded ovate shape.</li>
    <li>In its submerged form, the leaves can exhibit a metallic dark purple color, although achieving the best coloration may require optimal conditions, including high-power RGB lighting and CO2 injection.</li>
    <li>Younger leaves may initially appear green and gradually darken over time, potentially developing an almost black color with bluish iridescence under the right conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by cutting the rhizome into sections, each with a few leaves and roots.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape as you would with a new plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Black Cobra" is considered an easy plant to care for, even for beginners.</li>
    <li>Maintain stable water parameters to prevent melting and rotting.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when the plant adjusts to a new environment, but it will usually recover and produce new growth from the rhizome if the rhizome remains healthy.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any unhealthy leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial for promoting healthy growth.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 250.00, 550.00, 5.0, false, true, false, false, 0, true, '2025-07-09 07:40:57.712204+00', '2026-03-05 03:59:48.304849+00', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/black-cobra%2Fblackcobra01.jpeg?alt=media&token=6c9de34e-779a-4d5a-b1d4-cb3cd96fb93f', 'https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/aqua-india-61437.firebasestorage.app/o/black-cobra%2Fblackcobra01.jpeg?alt=media&token=6c9de34e-779a-4d5a-b1d4-cb3cd96fb93f');
