<title>Bucephalandra "Mini Coin" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Mini Coin" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Mini Coin" is a miniature variety of Bucephalandra native to Borneo, where it grows on rocks in rainforest streams with strong currents. It is a popular choice for nano aquariums and aquascapes due to its small size, with foliage typically not exceeding 0.5 inches (1 cm). </p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Mini Coin" prefers low to medium lighting but can also grow in shaded areas or even full light.</li>
    <li>Moderate lighting can enhance growth and color, but high light levels should be approached with caution as the slow-growing plant can develop algae on its leaves.</li>
    <li>Placing it in shaded areas may help prevent algae issues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This plant thrives in stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>The ideal temperature range is generally between 70–82°F (21–28°C), with some sources suggesting slightly cooler water (71-79°F / 21-26°C) for optimal health.</li>
    <li>A pH range of 6.0-7.5 is suitable, though a wider range of 6-8 is tolerated.</li>
    <li>Good water flow and clean, filtered water with regular changes are important.</li>
    <li>Some water hardness is beneficial, preferably 5 dGH and above.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra does best in stable tanks with mature biological systems.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not required for survival but can significantly improve growth and coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra absorbs nutrients mainly from the water column, making liquid fertilizers beneficial, such as Easy Green.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>As an epiphytic plant, the rhizome of Bucephalandra "Mini Coin" should not be buried in the substrate to prevent rot.</li>
    <li>It should be attached to hardscape like rocks or driftwood using glue or thread.</li>
    <li>If placed in substrate, ensure only the roots are covered and the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This is a slow-growing plant that requires patience.</li>
    <li>Its small leaves have wavy edges and can vary in shape, often being obovate to elongate.</li>    <li>It has a flat, creeping growth habit.</li>
    <li>The plant can display various colors, including deep red, purple, and blue hues, depending on aquarium conditions and appropriate LED lighting.</li>
    <li>Many varieties also have tiny white dots on their submerged leaves, and sometimes a metallic blue color may appear.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by cutting the rhizome with clean scissors.</li>
    <li>Each cut section should have roots and at least one growing tip or a few leaves.</li>
    <li>These sections can then be reattached to hardscape or planted with the rhizome exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Mini Coin" is considered easy to care for but is less tolerant of sudden parameter changes compared to some other aquatic plants.</li>
    <li>Maintaining stable water conditions helps prevent melting. Melting can occur when introduced to a new tank, but the plant often recovers if the rhizome is healthy.</li>
    <li>Regularly removing old leaves encourages new growth.</li>
    <li>Good water flow prevents detritus buildup. Algae can be an issue due to slow growth and leaf surface area.</li>
    <li>Certain tank inhabitants can help control algae.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra can also grow emersed in humid environments like paludariums or terrariums.</li>
</ul>

