INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (1, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Peperomia caperata "Luna Red" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Peperomia "Red Variants" Care Guide (Luna Red, Schumi Red, Quito)</h1>

<p>While the <strong>''Rosso''</strong> is known for its bicolor leaves, the true "Red" variants of <em>Peperomia caperata</em> feature deep crimson, burgundy, or metallic copper tones across the entire leaf. These plants are the ultimate "jewel" species for terrariums, offering a dark, moody contrast to green mosses. The most popular include <strong>''Luna Red''</strong> (deep purple-red), <strong>''Schumi Red''</strong> (vibrant metallic red), and <strong>''Quito''</strong> (burnt orange to copper-red).</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Color Maintenance:</strong> These variants require <strong>bright, indirect light</strong> to maintain their deep red pigments. In low light, they will often fade to a muddy brown or dark green.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun:</strong> Strictly avoid direct sunlight. The dark pigments absorb heat more quickly than green leaves, making them even more susceptible to scorching.</li>
    <li><strong>Terrarium Placement:</strong> They thrive under full-spectrum LED lights, which highlight the "sparkle" or metallic sheen often found on ''Schumi Red'' leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Like the ''Rosso'', these are semi-succulent. Water when the <strong>top 50-75% of the soil</strong> is dry. They are better off slightly underwatered than overwatered.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> They prefer <strong>high humidity (50-70%)</strong>. While they can adapt to home air, they stay much "plumpier" and more vibrant in the humid environment of a closed terrarium.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical Rule:</strong> Do not get water on the leaves or in the center of the clump. The crinkled (ripple) texture of the leaves traps water easily, which can lead to rapid rot or "melting" in high-humidity setups.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>''Luna Red'':</strong> Features deeply rippled, heart-shaped leaves that are a dark, almost black-purple/red. It is a recipient of the RHS Award of Garden Merit.</li>
    <li><strong>''Schumi Red'':</strong> Known for having a more metallic, shimmery red surface with very deep "valleys" in the leaf ripples.</li>
    <li><strong>''Quito'':</strong> A unique variant that displays more of a glowing copper or orange-red tone, especially on new growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> All these variants stay very compact, usually topping out at 6–8 inches, making them perfect midground "pops" of color.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Cuttings:</strong> These are famous for being able to grow an entire new plant from just <strong>half a leaf</strong>.
        <ol>
            <li>Cut a healthy leaf in half horizontally.</li>
            <li>Place the cut edge into moist soil or sphagnum moss.</li>
            <li>Keep in a high-humidity prop-box; tiny "pups" will emerge from the leaf veins in 4–6 weeks.</li>
        </ol>
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Air Purification:</strong> These red variants are stars at filtering indoor air pollutants like formaldehyde and benzene.</li>
    <li><strong>Pet Safety:</strong> All <em>Peperomia caperata</em> variants are <strong>non-toxic</strong> to cats and dogs.</li>
    <li><strong>Stability:</strong> These variants are slightly more sensitive to "root shock" than the ''Rosso''. When repotting, try to keep the original root ball intact and only move up one pot size.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 120.00, NULL, 55);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (2, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Peperomia caperata ''Rosso'' Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Peperomia ''Rosso'' (Emerald Ripple Peperomia) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Peperomia ''Rosso''</strong> is a stunning, compact cultivar of <em>Peperomia caperata</em>. It is a sport of the popular ''Luna Red'' and is highly prized for its dramatic color contrast: deep, glossy green on the top of the leaf and a vibrant, earthy red on the underside. Its pointed, lance-shaped leaves grow in a tight, symmetrical rosette, making it a perfect "jewel" plant for tabletops, desks, and small terrariums.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Prefers <strong>bright, indirect light</strong>. This is essential for maintaining the deep red color on the leaf undersides.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun:</strong> Avoid harsh, direct afternoon sun, which can cause the colors to fade or burn the fleshy leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Low Light:</strong> While it can tolerate medium light, it may become "leggy" as the stems stretch toward the light source, and the foliage will lose its compact rosette shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Artificial Light:</strong> It performs exceptionally well under fluorescent or LED office lights.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Watering Technique:</strong> Like a semi-succulent, it stores water in its thick leaves. Water only when the <strong>top 50-75% of the soil</strong> is dry.</li>
    <li><strong>Bottom Watering:</strong> It is highly recommended to water from the bottom or avoid the crown. Getting water trapped in the center of the rosette can lead to rot.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> Prefers moderate to high humidity (40-60%). While it handles average home air better than most tropicals, it will thrive in a terrarium or on a pebble tray.</li>
    <li><strong>Warning:</strong> Overwatering is the most common cause of death for Peperomia ''Rosso''. If the leaves feel soft or "mushy," stop watering immediately.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>65°F to 75°F (18°C – 24°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>It is sensitive to sudden temperature drops. Keep it away from cold drafts, air conditioning vents, and heaters.</li>
    <li>It is not frost-tolerant and should never be exposed to temperatures below 50°F (10°C).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Requires a very well-draining, "chunky" mix. A blend of high-quality potting soil with plenty of perlite, orchid bark, or pumice is ideal.</li>
    <li><strong>Potting:</strong> Peperomias have small root systems and prefer being slightly root-bound. Use a pot with excellent drainage holes.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Feed sparingly with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to <strong>half-strength</strong> once a month during the spring and summer. Do not fertilize in winter.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> A compact, mounding plant that rarely exceeds 8 inches in height and width.</li>
    <li><strong>Foliage:</strong> Features deeply corrugated, "quilted" leaves that are dark green above and maroon-red below. The leaves are arranged in a dense radial pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Flowers:</strong> Produces unique, upright, greenish-white flower spikes (spadices) that resemble mouse tails. Many growers snip these off to focus the plant''s energy on the foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Leaf Cuttings:</strong> You can propagate by cutting a healthy leaf with a bit of stem and placing it in moist soil or water.</li>
    <li><strong>Stem Cuttings:</strong> Take a cutting with at least one or two leaves and a node, and root it in a high-humidity environment.</li>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> Mature clumps can be gently divided into two or more plants during repotting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Air Purification:</strong> Like many houseplants, it helps remove toxins from the air and improves indoor air quality.</li>
    <li><strong>Pet Safety:</strong> <strong>Non-toxic</strong> to cats and dogs according to the ASPCA, making it one of the safest colorful plants for pet owners.</li>
    <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Occasionally wipe the leaves with a soft, dry cloth to remove dust. Avoid using leaf shine products, as they can clog the leaf pores.</li>
    <li><strong>Terrarium Use:</strong> It is a favorite for midground placement in terrariums due to its slow growth and striking vertical color contrast.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 100.00, NULL, 54);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (3, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Pothos scandens Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Pothos scandens Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Pothos scandens</strong>, often called the "Climbing Aroid," is the <em>true</em> Pothos species. While most common houseplants sold as "Pothos" are actually <em>Epipremnum aureum</em>, <em>Pothos scandens</em> is a distinct species native to the tropical forests of the Indian Subcontinent and Southeast Asia. It is famous for its unique leaf structure, where the petiole (leaf stem) is flattened and winged, making the foliage look like two leaves joined together—a look similar to Kaffir lime leaves.</p>

<p>This resilient climber is highly valued in Ayurvedic medicine for its cooling properties and is a fantastic choice for collectors looking for a "shingling" or climbing vine that is both rare and easy to care for.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Thrives in <strong>bright, indirect light</strong>. It can tolerate medium to low light conditions, but its growth will slow down significantly.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun:</strong> Strictly avoid intense afternoon sun, which will scorch and brown the delicate foliage.</li>
    <li><strong>Indoor Placement:</strong> An east- or west-facing window is ideal. It also performs exceptionally well under artificial grow lights in office or terrarium settings.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Watering Frequency:</strong> Water when the <strong>top 50% of the soil</strong> is dry. It generally enjoys weekly watering sessions during the spring and summer.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering Technique:</strong> Ensure the soil stays moist but never soggy. Overwatering can quickly lead to root decay and yellowing leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> Prefers high humidity between <strong>60-70%</strong>. While it can adapt to normal room humidity, it will grow much faster and produce larger leaves if misted every couple of days or kept near a humidifier.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Using distilled or rainwater is best to prevent mineral buildup on its unique leaf surfaces.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>68°F to 95°F (20°C – 35°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>It is native to wet tropical biomes and prefers warm, stable conditions. </li>
    <li><strong>Cold Protection:</strong> Protect it from temperatures below 50°F (10°C) and avoid cold drafts from air conditioning units.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Needs a well-draining, airy soil mix with high organic matter. A quality indoor potting mix blended with peat moss, leaf mold, or orchid bark works perfectly.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Apply a well-diluted balanced liquid feed every <strong>3 to 4 weeks</strong> during the growing season.</li>
    <li><strong>Winter Care:</strong> Reduce fertilization and watering in the winter when the plant enters a slower growth phase.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> A vigorous climber that uses aerial roots to "stick" to tree trunks or walls. It can reach lengths of 10-12 feet indoors if given a support to climb.</li>
    <li><strong>Leaf Structure:</strong> Features dark green, glossy leaves with a distinctive "winged" petiole. In its native habitat, it can grow in a shingling pattern (leaves flat against a surface).</li>
    <li><strong>Flowers:</strong> Occasionally produces small, globular, off-white flowers, though these are rare in an indoor environment.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stem Cuttings:</strong> Propagation is straightforward via stem cuttings. Take a 5-inch cutting with at least two or three nodes.</li>
    <li><strong>Method:</strong> You can root the cutting in water or directly in moist soil. If using soil, keeping the cutting in a high-humidity environment (like a plastic bag) will speed up root development.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Air Purification:</strong> Like its cousins, <em>Pothos scandens</em> is excellent at removing toxic chemicals from the air and increasing oxygen levels.</li>
    <li><strong>Pet Safety:</strong> <strong>Toxic to cats and dogs.</strong> It contains calcium oxalate crystals which cause irritation and vomiting if ingested. Keep out of reach of pets and children.</li>
    <li><strong>Mounting:</strong> To see this plant’s true potential, mount it on a moss pole or a cork bark slab. This encourages the leaves to maintain their unique shingling habit.</li>
    <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Clean the leaves regularly with a damp cloth to enhance their appearance and prevent pests like spider mites or mealybugs.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 80.00, NULL, 53);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (4, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Selaginella kraussiana (Krauss'' Spikemoss) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Selaginella kraussiana (Krauss'' Spikemoss) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Selaginella kraussiana</strong>, commonly known as <strong>Krauss'' Spikemoss</strong>, African Clubmoss, or Spreading Spikemoss, is a lush, mat-forming perennial native to tropical and Southern Africa. Despite its name and moss-like appearance, it is actually a vascular plant related to ferns (a "fern ally"). It is highly valued in the hobby for its delicate, lacy texture and its ability to form a vibrant green carpet in terrariums and shaded gardens.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Prefers <strong>low to medium indirect light</strong> or partial shade. It is naturally adapted to the deep shade of forest floors.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun:</strong> Strictly avoid direct sunlight. The delicate fronds will scorch, turn brown, or become "bleached" very quickly under harsh rays.</li>
    <li><strong>Indoors:</strong> A north- or east-facing window is ideal. In terrariums, standard LED lighting works well as long as the plant isn''t placed too close to the heat of the bulbs.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the soil <strong>consistently moist</strong> at all times. This plant does not have a dormant dry phase and will shrivel rapidly if the soil dries out completely.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Always use <strong>room-temperature water</strong>. Cold water can shock the plant''s system. Like many delicate plants, it prefers distilled, RO, or rainwater to avoid mineral buildup from tap water.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> High humidity (above 60-70%) is essential. It is a perfect candidate for <strong>closed terrariums</strong>, humid bathrooms, or placement near a humidifier.</li>
    <li><strong>Warning:</strong> While it loves moisture, avoid "soggy" or anaerobic soil. Ensure your pot or terrarium has a drainage layer to prevent root rot.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>60°F to 75°F (15°C – 24°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>It prefers cooler, stable tropical conditions. Avoid placing it near heaters, air conditioning vents, or drafty doorways.</li>
    <li>It is frost-tender and will not survive freezing temperatures.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Requires a rich, well-draining soil that retains moisture. A peat-based or coco-coir-based potting mix with added perlite or vermiculite is ideal.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Not a heavy feeder. Apply a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to <strong>half-strength</strong> or quarter-strength once or twice during the growing season (spring and summer).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> A prostrate, creeping groundcover that spreads via trailing stems. It rarely grows taller than 2–4 inches but can spread significantly wide.</li>
    <li><strong>Foliage:</strong> Tiny, scale-like leaves arranged in four rows along the stems, giving it a fern-like appearance.</li>
    <li><strong>Varieties:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><em>''Aurea'' (Golden Spikemoss):</em> Features bright chartreuse or golden-green foliage.</li>
            <li><em>''Brownii'' (Cushion Moss):</em> Forms tight, mounding clumps rather than spreading mats.</li>
            <li><em>''Variegata'' (Frosty Fern):</em> Dark green foliage with distinct white tips.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stem Cuttings:</strong> Extremely easy to propagate. Simply snip a 2–3 inch section of a healthy stem and lay it on moist soil or sphagnum moss.</li>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> Larger mats can be gently pulled apart or cut into sections during repotting. Ensure each division has a portion of the shallow root system attached.</li>
    <li>In high-humidity environments, stems often sprout "rhizophores" (aerial roots) that will anchor themselves as soon as they touch the ground.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Pet Safety:</strong> Non-toxic to dogs, cats, and horses according to the ASPCA. It is safe for bioactive vivariums containing small reptiles or amphibians.</li>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> If it becomes too leggy or outgrows its space, it can be safely trimmed back. This actually encourages denser, bushier growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Troubleshooting:</strong> If the leaves are shriveled and brown, humidity is too low. If the plant turns yellow and mushy, it is likely being overwatered or has poor drainage.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 0, 70.00, NULL, 52);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (5, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Cryptanthus bivittatus "Pink Starlite" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Cryptanthus bivittatus "Pink Starlite" Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Cryptanthus bivittatus "Pink Starlite"</strong> (often spelled "Pink Starlight") is a stunning terrestrial bromeliad native to the rainforest floors of Brazil. Unlike many bromeliads that grow on trees (epiphytes), the Earth Star grows directly in the soil. It is famous for its stemless, star-shaped rosette of wavy leaves that feature vibrant bubblegum-pink margins and deep forest-green centers. This "Drama Queen" of the bromeliad world is a functional powerhouse, known for its ability to purify indoor air by removing toxic chemicals and increasing oxygen levels at night.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Light Intensity:</strong> Requires <strong>bright, indirect light</strong> to maintain its intense pink coloration. The brighter the light (without direct sun), the more the foliage will "sparkle" like starlight.</li>
    <li><strong>Low Light:</strong> In low light, the vibrant pink and red tones will fade to a dull, light green, and the plant may lose its compact star shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Warning:</strong> Avoid direct, hot sun. Harsh sunlight will scorch the leaves or cause "bleaching," where the pink turns a pale, leathery white.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Watering Technique:</strong> Unlike other bromeliads, do <strong>not</strong> pour water into the central rosette (the cup). Water trapped there can cause mildew, fungal growth, or crown rot. Always water the soil directly.</li>
    <li><strong>Frequency:</strong> Keep the soil <strong>consistently moist</strong> but not soggy. Water approximately once a week when the top inch of soil feels dry. In winter, reduce watering to every 10–20 days as growth slows.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Use <strong>distilled water, RO water, or rainwater</strong> at room temperature. Earth Stars are sensitive to chlorine and fluoride in tap water, which can cause brown leaf tips.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> Thrives in high humidity (50-70%). Mist the plant every couple of days or place it on a pebble tray. It is an ideal candidate for closed terrariums and humid bathrooms.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>60°F to 85°F (15°C – 29°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>The "Pink Starlite" is not cold-tolerant. Keep it away from air conditioning vents, cold drafts, or heating units that dry out the air.</li>
    <li>Protect it from freezing temperatures; exposure to frost is usually fatal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Prefers a rich, acidic, and well-draining medium. A commercial peat-based potting soil or an "African Violet" mix is ideal.</li>
    <li><strong>DIY Mix:</strong> Combine equal parts potting soil, peat moss, and sand or perlite for optimal drainage and moisture retention.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Feed sparingly. Apply a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to <strong>1/4 strength</strong> once a month during spring and summer. </li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Spray the diluted fertilizer onto the leaves (foliar feeding) rather than the soil to enhance color, but avoid the central cup.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> A small, slow-growing perennial. It typically reaches 3–5 inches in height with a spread of 6–10 inches.</li>
    <li><strong>Foliage:</strong> Stiff, lance-shaped leaves with finely serrated (spiny) edges. handle with care as the tiny "teeth" can be sharp.</li>
    <li><strong>Flowers:</strong> Produces small, inconspicuous white flowers in the center of the rosette. Like all bromeliads, the mother plant will slowly die after flowering, directing its energy into "pups."</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is easily done via <strong>offsets or "pups"</strong> that appear at the base or between the leaves after blooming.</li>
    <li>Wait until the pup is at least <strong>1/3 the size</strong> of the mother plant.</li>
    <li>Gently tug or snip the pup off and press it into moist soil. Covering the new pot with a plastic bag can help lock in the high humidity needed for the new roots to establish.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Pet Safety:</strong> Non-toxic to cats and dogs according to the ASPCA. However, keep it out of reach to avoid pets being poked by the serrated leaf margins.</li>
    <li><strong>Air Purification:</strong> A "star" at cleaning the air, this plant filters out pollutants like formaldehyde and benzene.</li>
    <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Prune dead leaves at the base with clean scissors to maintain a tidy appearance and prevent pests like mealybugs from hiding in debris.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 150.00, NULL, 51);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (6, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Boston Fern (Nephrolepis exaltata) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Boston Fern (Nephrolepis exaltata) Care Guide</h1>

<p>The <strong>Boston Fern</strong> (<em>Nephrolepis exaltata</em>) is a classic tropical plant known for its lush, arching, sword-shaped fronds. While it is one of the most popular air-purifying houseplants, its high demand for moisture and humidity makes it a staple for large terrariums and humid vivariums. In a home environment, it acts as a natural humidity regulator, though it can be a bit of a "shedder" if its needs aren''t met.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Boston Ferns thrive in <strong>bright, indirect sunlight</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun:</strong> Harsh, direct afternoon sun will quickly scorch the delicate leaflets, leading to yellowing or "crispy" brown fronds.</li>
    <li><strong>Low Light:</strong> While they can survive in lower light, they will become "leggy" and sparse, losing their signature bushy density. Filtered morning light near an East-facing window is usually the "sweet spot."</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> This is the most critical factor. They prefer <strong>60-80% humidity</strong>. In dry indoor air, the tips of the leaves will turn brown and fall off, creating a mess.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> The soil should be kept <strong>consistently moist</strong> but never waterlogged. They do not tolerate drying out; if the soil feels dry to the touch, it is time to water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> They are sensitive to heavily chlorinated tap water. Using <strong>distilled water or rainwater</strong> is recommended to keep the foliage vibrant green.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>65-75°F (18-24°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>They are tropical plants and should be protected from cold drafts, air conditioning vents, and temperatures below 50°F (10°C). Sudden temperature fluctuations can cause significant leaf drop.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Substrate and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> A loose, well-draining potting mix rich in organic matter is best. A blend of <strong>peat moss or coco coir</strong> with a bit of perlite and compost works well.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> During the growing season (spring/summer), feed every 4-6 weeks with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to <strong>half-strength</strong>. Avoid feeding during the winter when growth naturally slows.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Terrarium Use and Varieties</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> Standard Boston Ferns can grow quite large (up to 3 feet wide), making them better suited for <strong>large vivariums</strong> or as room plants.</li>
    <li><strong>Compact Varieties:</strong> For smaller terrariums, look for:
        <ul>
            <li><em>''Duffy'' (Lemon Button Fern):</em> A much smaller relative with round, button-like leaves.</li>
            <li><em>''Fluffy Ruffles'':</em> A smaller, more upright variety with stiff, multi-divided leaves.</li>
            <li><em>''Dallas'':</em> Specifically bred to stay compact and tolerate lower light and lower humidity.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>Maintenance and Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> Regularly trim away brown or dead fronds at the base to encourage fresh, green growth and improve airflow.</li>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> The easiest way to propagate is by <strong>division</strong>. During repotting, you can gently pull the root ball apart into smaller sections (ensuring each has roots and fronds) and plant them separately.</li>
    <li><strong>Runners:</strong> They often produce long, leafless "runners" (stolons) that can be pinned to the soil to grow new plantlets.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The "Shedding":</strong> Don''t panic if your fern drops some leaves when you first bring it home; it is likely adjusting to the change in humidity.</li>
    <li><strong>Pet Safety:</strong> Boston Ferns are <strong>non-toxic</strong> to cats and dogs, making them a safe choice for pet owners.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaning:</strong> Use a gentle spray bottle to mist the leaves; this both increases humidity and keeps the fronds free of dust.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 70.00, NULL, 50);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (7, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Heart Leaf Fern (Hemionitis arifolia) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Heart Leaf Fern (Hemionitis arifolia) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Heart Leaf Fern</strong>, scientifically known as <em>Hemionitis arifolia</em>, is a delicate and enchanting dwarf fern native to Southeast Asia. It is famous for its thick, leathery, dark green fronds that are shaped like perfect hearts. While it is a stunning addition to any plant collection, it is notoriously finicky as a houseplant because it demands high humidity—making it an elite choice for <strong>terrariums and vivariums</strong>.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Heart Leaf Ferns prefer <strong>bright, indirect light</strong>.</li>
    <li>In nature, they grow on forest floors and in rock crevices, so they are adapted to filtered light. Direct sunlight will quickly "burn" the heart-shaped leaves, causing them to turn brown and crispy.</li>
    <li>If grown in a terrarium, a medium-intensity LED light is perfect. If the plant is too far from a light source, the stems (petioles) will become excessively long and thin.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> This is the most critical factor. It requires <strong>60-80% humidity</strong>. Without an enclosure or a humidifier, the leaves will curl and die within days.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> The soil should be kept <strong>consistently moist</strong> but never waterlogged. This fern does not have a "storage" system for water and will wilt quickly if the substrate dries out.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Like most ferns, it is sensitive to chemicals. Use <strong>distilled, RO, or rainwater</strong>. Avoid pouring water directly into the center (crown) of the plant, as this can cause rot; water the soil around it instead.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>60-85°F (15-29°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>It thrives in warm, tropical environments. It is not frost-tolerant and should be kept away from cold drafts and air conditioning vents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Use a rich, well-draining, and slightly acidic mix. A blend of <strong>peat moss, leaf mold, and perlite</strong> works best. In terrariums, it does well in ABG mix.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> It is a light feeder. Use a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to <strong>1/4 strength</strong> once a month during the growing season. Do not fertilize if the plant is stressed or the soil is dry.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Fronds:</strong> It produces two types of fronds. The <strong>sterile fronds</strong> are shorter and heart-shaped. The <strong>fertile fronds</strong> (which produce spores) are taller, narrower, and shaped more like arrowheads.</li>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> It is a compact fern, rarely growing taller than 6-8 inches, making it ideal for medium-sized glass containers.</li>
    <li><strong>Texture:</strong> The leaves have a smooth, waxy feel, while the stems are stiff, dark (almost black), and slightly hairy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Spores:</strong> It can be grown from spores found on the underside of fertile fronds, though this is a slow process for beginners.</li>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> Mature plants can be carefully divided at the rhizome during repotting. Ensure each division has several healthy fronds and a good root system.</li>
    <li><strong>Viviparous Growth:</strong> Some species of Hemionitis can grow "baby" plants directly on the leaves where the stem meets the leaf blade, though this is less common in <em>arifolia</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> Snip off any yellow or dead fronds at the base of the stem to encourage new growth and keep the plant looking tidy.</li>
    <li><strong>The "Death" Curl:</strong> If the leaves curl inward, the plant is likely suffering from low humidity or dry soil. Act quickly to mist the plant and increase moisture.</li>
    <li><strong>Terrarium Placement:</strong> Plant it in the midground of your terrarium. It pairs beautifully with <strong>Cushion Moss</strong> or <strong>Fittonia</strong> for a high-contrast tropical look.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 150.00, NULL, 49);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (8, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Cushion Moss (Leucobryum glaucum) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Cushion Moss (Leucobryum glaucum) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Cushion Moss</strong>, scientifically known as <em>Leucobryum glaucum</em>, is also frequently called <strong>Pillow Moss</strong>, <strong>Bun Moss</strong>, or <strong>Pincushion Moss</strong>. It is a slow-growing, acrocarpous moss that naturally forms dense, rounded mounds resembling green velvet pillows. Native to temperate forests in North America and Europe, it is one of the most aesthetically pleasing mosses for terrariums, adding structural "hills" and soft textures to miniature landscapes.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Cushion Moss prefers <strong>bright, indirect light</strong>. Unlike many other mosses that thrive in deep shade, this variety needs decent light to maintain its tight, compact shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Indoors:</strong> A spot near a North-facing window or a few feet away from a bright window is ideal. Artificial LED grow lights work exceptionally well.</li>
    <li><strong>Warning:</strong> Avoid direct, harsh afternoon sun, which can "bleach" the moss, turning it from vibrant green to a pale, silvery-white or straw color.</li>
    <li>If the moss begins to turn yellow, it is often a sign that it is not receiving enough light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> High humidity (70-90%) is the "secret sauce" for Cushion Moss. It is almost exclusively grown in <strong>closed terrariums</strong> or high-humidity vivariums.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> The moss should be kept consistently moist but never sitting in stagnant water. The "clump" acts like a sponge; you can gently press it to feel if it is hydrated.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Use only <strong>distilled, RO (Reverse Osmosis), or rainwater</strong>. Cushion Moss is highly sensitive to the minerals, salts, and chlorine in tap water, which can cause the tips to turn brown or the entire clump to die.</li>
    <li><strong>Airflow:</strong> While it loves humidity, it also appreciates occasional fresh air. Open your terrarium for 15-20 minutes once a week to prevent mold.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: <strong>60-75°F (15-24°C)</strong>.</li>
    <li>It is a temperate moss, meaning it prefers cooler to moderate temperatures. It can survive colder dips (it is frost-hardy in nature) but struggles in extreme, dry heat over 85°F (29°C).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Substrate and Placement</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> It prefers slightly acidic, nutrient-poor substrates. A mix of peat moss, sand, and orchid bark is common.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation:</strong> Simply press the "bun" firmly onto the damp substrate to ensure the bottom makes good contact. It does not have roots, but it uses tiny filaments (rhizoids) to anchor itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Landscape Tip:</strong> Because it grows in mounds, use it to create "elevation" in your terrarium. You can nestle several clumps together to create a rolling, hilly forest floor.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> It is characterized by its silvery-green to lime-green color and its distinct spherical or hemispherical shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Texture:</strong> Very dense and firm to the touch. The tiny leaves are packed so tightly together that the moss feels like a solid object.</li>
    <li><strong>Drought Recovery:</strong> Cushion Moss has a unique ability to survive drying out by entering dormancy. If it becomes dry and "crunchy," a deep soak in distilled water can often revive it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by <strong>division</strong>.</li>
    <li>Gently pull a large cushion apart into smaller "tufts."</li>
    <li>Place these smaller pieces onto moist substrate. Over time, each piece will begin to grow and round itself out into a new cushion shape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Cleanliness:</strong> Because it grows in tight mounds, it can trap dust or debris. A gentle misting now and then will keep the "pores" clean.</li>
    <li><strong>Browning at the Base:</strong> It is normal for the very bottom of the cushion (the part touching the soil) to be brown or dark; this is the older growth. As long as the top surface is green, the moss is healthy.</li>
    <li><strong>Mushroom Growth:</strong> Occasionally, small mushrooms may sprout from the moss in a terrarium. These are usually harmless and add to the natural aesthetic, but can be removed if desired.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 1, 350.00, NULL, 48);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (9, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Fittonia (Nerve Plant) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Fittonia (Nerve Plant) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Fittonia</strong>, commonly known as the <strong>Nerve Plant</strong> or <strong>Mosaic Plant</strong>, is a striking tropical perennial native to the rainforests of South America. It is famous for its deeply veined leaves that create a high-contrast "nerve" pattern. While they are popular houseplants, their intense love for humidity makes them the absolute superstars of the closed terrarium world. They are also known as the "Drama Queens" of plants because they will dramatically wilt the moment they get too dry, only to "resurrect" within hours of being watered.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Fittonia prefers <strong>bright, indirect light</strong>. In their native habitat, they grow on the forest floor under a dense canopy.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct Sun:</strong> Strictly avoid direct sunlight; the thin, delicate leaves will scorch or develop bleached spots almost instantly.</li>
    <li><strong>Low Light:</strong> They can tolerate lower light, but the vibrant colors of the veins (pink, red, or white) may fade, and the plant will become "leggy" as it stretches for light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> Requires <strong>high humidity (60-90%)</strong>. If the air is too dry, leaf edges will turn crisp and brown. This is why they thrive so well in glass enclosures.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the soil <strong>consistently moist</strong> but not waterlogged. They do not like to dry out between waterings.</li>
    <li><strong>The "Faint":</strong> If you see your Fittonia collapsed flat against the soil, it is thirsty. Give it a thorough drink, and it should perk back up quickly.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Sensitive to chlorine. Using room-temperature distilled or rainwater helps prevent leaf tip burn.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 65-80°F (18-27°C).</li>
    <li>As a true tropical, it is very sensitive to cold. Keep it away from drafty windows or air conditioning vents. Temperatures below 55°F (13°C) can be fatal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Use a well-draining potting mix that retains moisture. A blend of peat moss or coco coir with perlite is ideal.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Feed every 4–6 weeks during the growing season (spring/summer) with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to half-strength.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> Low-growing and spreading. It rarely exceeds 6 inches in height but can trail beautifully.</li>
    <li><strong>Varieties:</strong> 
        <ul>
            <li><em>Argyroneura:</em> Silver-white veins on dark green leaves.</li>
            <li><em>Pearcei:</em> Bright pink or red veins on olive-green leaves.</li>
            <li><em>Frankie:</em> Primarily pink leaves with green edges.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is incredibly easy through stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Snip a stem with at least two nodes and place it in a jar of water or directly into moist soil.</li>
    <li>In a high-humidity environment, roots will usually appear within 1–2 weeks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> To keep the plant bushy, pinch off the tips of the stems regularly. This encourages horizontal growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Flowers:</strong> Fittonia can produce small flower spikes. Many growers snip these off so the plant directs its energy toward its foliage.</li>
    <li><strong>Terrarium Companion:</strong> Fittonia pairs perfectly with Mood Moss and Ferns, as they all share identical humidity and light requirements.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 2, 80.00, NULL, 47);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (10, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Sheet Moss (Hypnum curvifolium) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Sheet Moss (Hypnum curvifolium) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Sheet Moss</strong>, often belonging to the genus <em>Hypnum</em>, is the quintessential "carpeting" moss. It is a pleurocarpous moss, meaning it grows horizontally in wide, flat sheets rather than upright clumps. In nature, it blankets logs, rocks, and forest floors, creating a seamless emerald carpet. It is the most versatile moss for terrariums, kokedama, and even temporary floral displays.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Sheet Moss is highly adaptable but prefers <strong>low to medium indirect light</strong>.</li>
    <li>It can tolerate slightly more light than Mood Moss, but direct midday sun will bleach it yellow and make it brittle.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium, place it in a spot that mimics a "shaded forest floor." If using artificial lights, 6–8 hours of moderate LED light is sufficient.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> It thrives in 60-80% humidity. It is very happy in closed terrariums but is also one of the few mosses that can survive in open containers if misted daily.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the "sheet" damp to the touch. It acts like a sponge, holding a significant amount of water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> As with all mosses, <strong>distilled, RO, or rainwater</strong> is best. Tap water minerals will eventually build up in the moss fibers and turn it brown from the bottom up.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Substrate and Placement</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> It isn''t picky about soil but prefers something slightly acidic. It grows well on peat moss, bark, or even directly on rocks if they stay moist.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation:</strong> This is the easiest moss to install. Simply take a "sheet" and press it firmly onto a damp surface. Ensure there are no air pockets underneath so the rhizoids can anchor properly.</li>
    <li><strong>Hardscape:</strong> Because it grows in mats, it is the best choice for covering <strong>terrarium backgrounds</strong> or wrapping around <strong>driftwood</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> It spreads outward in all directions, overlapping itself to create a thick, springy carpet.</li>
    <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> It has a classic "mossy" look with small, densely packed green leaves. It stays relatively flat (usually under 1 inch in height).</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Moderate. It is faster than Mood Moss and will eventually fill in gaps between other plants if given enough moisture.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Maintenance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Division:</strong> If your sheet moss gets too large, you can simply tear it into smaller pieces and move them to other areas; they will continue to grow independently.</li>
    <li><strong>Browning:</strong> If the moss turns brown but feels "crispy," it needs more water. If it turns brown/black and feels "mushy," it is likely overwatered or lacks airflow.</li>
    <li><strong>Dormancy:</strong> Sheet moss is incredibly resilient. If it dries out and turns golden-brown, it usually isn''t dead—it''s dormant. A heavy misting will often "wake it up" within hours.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Sheet Moss is the preferred choice for <strong>reptile and amphibian enclosures</strong> because it can withstand a bit of foot traffic (trampling) better than delicate fern moss.</li>
    <li>It is frequently used in <strong>Bonsai</strong> to cover the soil surface, helping to retain moisture for the tree.</li>
    <li>If you are using "preserved" sheet moss from a craft store, keep in mind that it is no longer living and will not grow; living sheet moss should be vibrant green and slightly damp when purchased.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 200.00, NULL, 46);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (11, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Fern Moss (Thuidium delicatulum) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Fern Moss (Thuidium delicatulum) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Fern Moss</strong>, also known as <em>Thuidium delicatulum</em> or Delicate Fern Moss, is a stunning pleurocarpous (creeping) moss. It earned its name because its intricate, branching structure looks like miniature fern fronds. Unlike the clumpy Mood Moss, Fern Moss spreads horizontally, making it one of the most beautiful "sheet" mosses for carpeting forest floors in terrariums and vivariums.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Fern Moss thrives in <strong>low to medium indirect light</strong>.</li>
    <li>It is naturally found in deeply shaded woodlands and near stream banks. Direct sunlight will quickly dry it out and turn the delicate branches a brittle brown.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium, soft LED lighting is perfect. If the moss starts to look pale or yellowish, it may be receiving too much light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> This moss requires <strong>very high humidity (70-90%)</strong>. It does best in closed containers or frequently misted vivariums.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the moss consistently moist. Because it is so thin and delicate, it lacks the water-storage capacity of thicker mosses and can dry out rapidly.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Always use <strong>distilled, RO, or rainwater</strong>. Fern Moss is highly sensitive to the salts and chlorine found in tap water, which will cause it to die back.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Substrate and Placement</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> It prefers a damp, organic substrate such as peat moss, coco coir, or a specialized ABG mix.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> It is an excellent "shingling" or carpeting moss. Simply press the sheets of moss onto damp soil. You can also drape it over <strong>damp driftwood or porous rocks</strong>, where it will eventually anchor itself with its rhizoids.</li>
    <li>It is particularly effective at covering the "seams" between different hardscape elements in an aquascape or terrarium.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> It is a <strong>pleurocarpous</strong> moss, meaning it grows horizontally and branches out repeatedly, forming a dense, woven mat.</li>
    <li><strong>Texture:</strong> It has a very fine, lace-like texture. When viewed closely, the triangular branches look exactly like tiny ferns.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Moderate. Once established in a high-humidity environment, it spreads faster than Mood Moss but is easier to control than many aquatic mosses.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Maintenance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Trimming:</strong> If it begins to climb over other plants, you can easily trim it with scissors. The clippings can often be used to start new patches.</li>
    <li><strong>Airflow:</strong> While it loves humidity, stagnant "stale" air can encourage mold. Occasional air circulation is beneficial.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaning:</strong> Because of its intricate structure, it can trap falling debris. Gently misting the moss helps wash away dust and keeps the "fronds" looking vibrant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Fern Moss is a favorite for <strong>Dart Frog vivariums</strong> because it creates a soft, naturalistic carpet that stays low to the ground.</li>
    <li>It is often used in <strong>Kokedama</strong> (Japanese moss balls) because of its flexible, mat-like nature.</li>
    <li>If the moss arrives dry, soak it in distilled water for 15–20 minutes; it should "rehydrate" and regain its green color quickly.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 250.00, NULL, 45);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (12, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Mood Moss (Dicranum scoparium) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Mood Moss (Dicranum scoparium) Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Mood Moss</strong>, or <em>Dicranum scoparium</em>, is one of the most popular mosses used in terrariums and floral displays. It is called "Mood Moss" because its appearance changes based on its moisture levels—when dry, it looks somewhat disheveled and yellowish; when hydrated, it becomes a lush, vibrant green cushion. It is a "clumpy" moss that grows in thick, fluffy mounds, making it perfect for creating rolling hills in miniature landscapes.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Mood Moss prefers <strong>low to medium indirect light</strong>.</li>
    <li>In nature, it grows on forest floors under heavy canopies, so it is highly sensitive to direct sunlight. Intense sun will quickly bleach the moss and dry it out.</li>
    <li>For indoor terrariums, standard LED lights or fluorescent tubes work well. Keep the light source at a distance to prevent overheating.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> It thrives in high humidity (above 60%). It is best suited for <strong>closed terrariums</strong> where moisture is recycled.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Use a spray bottle to mist the moss regularly. The goal is to keep it damp but not sitting in a pool of water.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Quality:</strong> Like most mosses, it is sensitive to chemicals. Use <strong>distilled water, RO water, or rainwater</strong>. The chlorine and minerals in tap water can cause the moss to turn brown over time.</li>
    <li>If the moss looks "moody" or shriveled, a thorough misting will usually bring it back to its fluffy state within minutes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Substrate and Placement</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Mood Moss does not have true roots; it has <strong>rhizoids</strong> which are used primarily for anchoring. It prefers a slightly acidic substrate. A mix of peat moss and sand or a specialized terrarium soil works best.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation:</strong> To "plant" it, simply press the clump firmly onto the damp substrate. Ensure there is good contact between the bottom of the moss and the soil so it can wick up moisture.</li>
    <li>It can also be attached to rocks or wood using a tiny amount of aquarium-safe glue or thread, provided the surface stays moist.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> It grows in dense, rounded cushions or tufts. It is a <strong>acrocarpous</strong> moss, meaning it grows upright in clumps rather than spreading out like a carpet.</li>
    <li><strong>Texture:</strong> It has a soft, velvety feel with slightly curved, lance-shaped leaves that all tend to point in the same direction (often called "broom moss").</li>
    <li><strong>Growth Rate:</strong> Very slow. It will not quickly take over your container, which makes it excellent for maintaining a specific design.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Maintenance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Airflow:</strong> In closed terrariums, open the lid for a few minutes once a week to provide fresh air and prevent mold growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaning:</strong> Occasionally, dust or debris may settle on the moss. Gently brush it off or use a light spray of water to rinse it.</li>
    <li><strong>Browning:</strong> If the tips turn brown, it usually indicates either too much light or the use of tap water. Move it to a darker spot and switch to distilled water.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Mood Moss is excellent for <strong>bioactive enclosures</strong> for small amphibians or invertebrates, as it provides a soft, humid micro-climate.</li>
    <li>It is naturally resistant to many pests, but keep an eye out for fungal gnats if the substrate is kept excessively swampy.</li>
    <li>Because it holds a lot of water, it acts as a natural humidity regulator for your terrarium.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 3, 300.00, NULL, 44);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (13, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Selaginella bigelovii "Isabella" (Bushy Spikemoss) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Isabella Bushy Spikemoss (Selaginella bigelovii "Isabella") Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>Isabella Bushy Spikemoss</strong> is a unique and rugged variety of <em>Selaginella bigelovii</em>. Unlike many of the tropical, moisture-addicted spikemosses common in the hobby, this variety originates from the Isabella Lake region in California. It is a North American native that has adapted to more "mediterranean" or semi-arid conditions, making it significantly more drought-tolerant than its tropical cousins like the Peacock Moss or Frosty Fern.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for "Isabella" Bushy Spikemoss:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Isabella</strong> thrives in <strong>full sun to partial shade</strong>.</li>
    <li>Unlike tropical spikemosses that require deep shade, this variety can handle significant amounts of direct sunlight, especially in the morning.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium or indoor setting, place it under bright, full-spectrum LED lighting to maintain its dense, "bushy" structure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Drought Tolerance:</strong> This is one of the few "xerophytic" spikemosses. It is naturally adapted to survive dry spells.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> While it is drought-tolerant, it looks its best when the soil is kept <strong>consistently moist but well-drained</strong>. Water once a week or whenever the top of the soil feels dry.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> It is remarkably adaptable. While it appreciates higher humidity (like most Selaginella), it can tolerate the lower humidity levels found in typical household environments far better than other varieties.</li>
    <li><strong>Dormancy:</strong> If left completely dry for too long, it may enter a dormant state where it turns brown and curls up, similar to a Resurrection Plant, bouncing back once water is reintroduced.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Hardiness:</strong> This is a hardy variety, suitable for <strong>USDA Zones 7b to 9b</strong>. It can handle much colder temperatures than tropical varieties.</li>
    <li>It thrives in temperatures between <strong>50-80°F (10-27°C)</strong> but is surprisingly resilient to outdoor fluctuations in its native range.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Use a gritty, well-draining soil mix. A blend of potting soil with added sand, fine gravel, or perlite is ideal to mimic its rocky, native habitat.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> It is a light feeder. A diluted, balanced liquid fertilizer applied once or twice during the spring growing season is sufficient to enhance its resilience.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> It features upright, branching stems covered in tiny, scale-like evergreen leaves. It has a much stiffer, more "shrub-like" texture compared to the soft, lacy feel of other spikemosses.</li>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> It typically grows to a height of about <strong>6 inches</strong> and spreads slowly to form a dense, textured ground cover.</li>
    <li><strong>Color:</strong> It maintains a rich, olive-to-forest green color year-round.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is best done through <strong>division</strong> or <strong>stem cuttings</strong>.</li>
    <li>Because it produces "rhizophores" (root-like structures that grow from the stems), sections of the plant that touch the soil will often root themselves.</li>
    <li>Simply snip a healthy stem and press it into moist, gritty soil to start a new plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Versatility:</strong> This plant is excellent for <strong>rock gardens, xeriscaping, and open terrariums</strong> where humidity isn''t strictly controlled.</li>
    <li><strong>Indoor Use:</strong> If growing indoors, ensure it has plenty of light; otherwise, the "bushy" stems may become weak and floppy.</li>
    <li><strong>Outdoor Use:</strong> It makes a fantastic native groundcover for West Coast-style gardens or dry creek bed aesthetics.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 0, 250.00, NULL, 43);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (14, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Rotala rotundifolia "Green" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Rotala rotundifolia "Green" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Rotala rotundifolia "Green" is a popular and versatile aquatic stem plant that serves as a staple in the aquascaping hobby. Unlike the standard Rotala rotundifolia, which often turns reddish under high light, the "Green" variety is specifically known for its ability to maintain a vibrant, lime-green coloration regardless of light intensity. Its weeping growth habit when pruned makes it an ideal choice for creating dense, bushy mounds in the midground or background of an aquarium.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Rotala rotundifolia "Green":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Rotala rotundifolia "Green" can grow in low light, but it requires medium to high lighting to achieve its signature bushy and trailing growth habit.</li>
    <li>Under high light, the stems have a tendency to grow horizontally or "weep," which allows aquascapers to create unique flowing shapes.</li>
    <li>In low-light conditions, the plant will grow strictly vertical and may become leggy with larger gaps between the leaves.</li>
    <li>A photoperiod of 8-10 hours is recommended to maintain its lush, healthy appearance.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This plant is relatively hardy and can adapt to a variety of water conditions, making it suitable for beginners.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 64-82°F (18-28°C). {Link: According to www.tropica.com https://tropica.com/en/plants/plantdetails/Rotalarotundifolia''Green''(033A)/4447}, it can thrive in a wide temperature range, though mid-70s is optimal for growth.</li>
    <li>pH range: 5.0-7.5. It generally prefers slightly acidic to neutral water.</li>
    <li>It appreciates soft to moderately hard water, though it is quite flexible compared to more delicate Rotala species.</li>
    <li>Regular water changes are important to keep the water clean, as fine-leaved plants can easily trap debris which leads to algae growth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly required for survival, but it is highly recommended if you want to achieve the dense, creeping growth habit often seen in professional aquascapes.</li>
    <li>Without CO2, the growth will be much slower and the stems will be thinner.</li>
    <li>This plant is a heavy feeder and benefits significantly from regular liquid fertilization (NPK + micronutrients).</li>
    <li>Nitrate levels should be kept stable; while some Rotalas turn redder under low nitrate, Rotala "Green" simply requires consistent nutrients to maintain its bright green vitality.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Rotala "Green" should be planted directly into the substrate in small bundles or individual stems.</li>
    <li>A nutrient-rich aquarium soil (active substrate) is highly recommended to support its fast-growing root system.</li>
    <li>When planting, use tweezers to insert the stems at least 1-2 inches deep into the substrate to prevent them from floating.</li>
    <li>Space the stems roughly 0.5 to 1 inch apart to allow for water flow and light penetration to the lower leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This is a fast-growing stem plant that requires regular maintenance to look its best.</li>
    <li>It features small, narrow, pointed leaves that grow in pairs or whorls along the stem.</li>
    <li>Its most unique characteristic is its growth pattern; instead of growing straight up to the surface, it often curves and grows out to the sides, especially under bright light.</li>
    <li>If left untrimmed, it can quickly reach the surface and begin to trail across the top of the water.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is very easy and is done by taking stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Simply cut the top portion of a healthy stem (about 3-4 inches) and replant it into the substrate.</li>
    <li>The "bottom" part of the stem left in the substrate will soon sprout multiple new side shoots from the nodes near the cut, leading to a much thicker bush.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Frequent pruning is the secret to a beautiful Rotala "Green" bush. The more you trim it, the more it branches out.</li>
    <li>When the bush becomes too thick after several trimmings, it may be necessary to uproot the entire clump, discard the old bottom portions, and replant the healthy tops to prevent the base from rotting due to lack of light.</li>
    <li>It is an excellent plant for "shaping"—you can use sharp scissors to trim the clump into spheres, waves, or sloped mounds.</li>
    <li>Because of its fine leaves, it provides excellent hiding places for shrimp and small fry.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 50.00, NULL, 42);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (15, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Marcgravia umbellata Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Marcgravia umbellata Care Guide</h1>

<p>Marcgravia umbellata, also known as the "Shingle Vine" or "Monkey Paws," is a stunning epiphytic climber native to the Caribbean. It is highly prized in the vivarium and terrarium hobby for its shingling growth habit, where its small, teardrop-shaped leaves overlap tightly against a surface, resembling green shingles. It is the perfect choice for creating a lush, tropical rainforest aesthetic on vertical backgrounds.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Marcgravia umbellata:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Marcgravia umbellata thrives in low to bright indirect light.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate lower light levels, bright indirect light will produce more extraordinary foliage and tighter shingling.</li>
    <li>Avoid direct sunlight at all costs, as the delicate leaves will scorch and turn brown very quickly.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium, standard full-spectrum LED lighting is ideal. The plant will display phototropism, growing toward the light source.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This plant is not an aquatic plant and cannot be grown submerged; it is strictly for the terrestrial or "land" portions of a setup.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 65-80°F (18-27°C). It prefers warm, stable tropical temperatures.</li>
    <li>Humidity: It requires very high humidity, ideally between 60-80%+. It is most successful in enclosed glass containers or vivariums.</li>
    <li>Watering: The substrate and the climbing surface should be kept consistently moist but never waterlogged. It does not like water "standing" on its leaves for long periods.</li>
    <li>If the leaves start to yellow, it is often a sign of overwatering or poor drainage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>As a terrestrial plant, it has access to atmospheric CO2 and does not require supplementation.</li>
    <li>Fertilize sparingly. During the growing season (spring/summer), you can use a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to 1/4 or 1/2 strength.</li>
    <li>In a bioactive vivarium with animals, the waste from the inhabitants often provides enough nutrients for the vine to flourish.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Marcgravia is an epiphyte/hemiepiphyte. While it can start in a well-draining soil mix (like ABG mix or sphagnum moss), its true goal is to climb.</li>
    <li>It must have a permanent, slightly moist vertical support to attach to, such as cork bark, driftwood, or a foam terrarium background.</li>
    <li>Use small floral pins or "U" pins to gently secure the vine against the background until its aerial roots take hold.</li>
    <li>The roots require high humidity and a porous surface to anchor the plant effectively.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is a slow to moderate grower initially, but it speeds up significantly once it begins to climb.</li>
    <li>Juvenile growth is characterized by small, flat, shingling leaves that overlap.</li>
    <li>The foliage is a deep forest green, often featuring beautiful white reticulation or veining. Some variants, like "Red," can show copper or reddish tones under brighter light.</li>
    <li>If the vine loses contact with a surface and grows into the air, the leaves may change shape or the plant may stop shingling.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is easily achieved through stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Cut a section of the vine (ideally 4-6 inches long) using sharp, sterilized scissors.</li>
    <li>Lay the cutting flat on moist sphagnum moss in a high-humidity propagation box.</li>
    <li>Ensure the underside of the stem is in direct contact with the moss; new roots will sprout from the nodes within a few weeks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Marcgravia umbellata is sensitive to sudden changes in environment; expect a short "stall" in growth when first moving it into a new setup.</li>
    <li>Pruning is necessary if the vine begins to outgrow its space or cover slower-growing plants.</li>
    <li>It is an excellent companion for tropical frogs (like Dart Frogs) because the overlapping leaves provide a naturalistic texture and micro-habitats.</li>
    <li>Avoid placing it near heating or cooling vents, as the dry air will cause the vine to shrivel almost overnight.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 0, 300.00, NULL, 41);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (16, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bacopa caroliniana Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bacopa caroliniana Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bacopa caroliniana, commonly known as Lemon Bacopa, is a classic and hardy stem plant native to the wetlands of the Southern United States. It is popular in the aquarium hobby for its fleshy, mint-green leaves and its distinct lemon-like scent when the leaves are crushed or trimmed. It is an excellent choice for beginners due to its slow, manageable growth and overall resilience.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bacopa caroliniana:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bacopa caroliniana thrives in moderate to high lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While it can survive in low light, the lower portions of the stem may lose leaves, and the plant will become leggy as it reaches toward the light source.</li>
    <li>Under high-intensity lighting, the tips of the plant can develop beautiful copper, bronze, or even pinkish hues.</li>
    <li>Aim for a photoperiod of 8-10 hours to encourage compact and colorful growth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This plant is highly adaptable and can tolerate a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 59-82°F (15-28°C). {Link: According to www.tropica.com https://tropica.com/en/plants/plantdetails/Bacopacaroliniana(043)/4461}, it is exceptionally hardy and can even be used in outdoor ponds during warmer months.</li>
    <li>pH range: 5.0-7.5. It prefers slightly acidic to neutral water.</li>
    <li>It can tolerate varying levels of water hardness, though it generally prefers soft to moderately hard water.</li>
    <li>Clean water and regular water changes help prevent algae from forming on the leaves, which are relatively slow-growing for a stem plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly necessary but will result in thicker stems and more robust leaf growth.</li>
    <li>It is a heavy feeder that benefits from both root tabs and liquid fertilizers.</li>
    <li>If iron levels are sufficient and lighting is high, the plant will reward you with much more vibrant reddish-brown colors at the surface.</li>
    <li>In nutrient-poor environments, the leaves may turn pale or yellow, indicating a need for a balanced NPK fertilizer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bacopa caroliniana should be planted directly into the substrate.</li>
    <li>A nutrient-rich aquarium soil is ideal, but it can also grow in plain sand or gravel if supplemented with root tabs.</li>
    <li>When planting, remove the lower leaves from the stem and insert it about 1-2 inches into the substrate to ensure it stays anchored.</li>
    <li>Space the stems about an inch apart to ensure that light reaches the bottom leaves of each plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Compared to many other stem plants, Bacopa caroliniana is a relatively slow grower, making it easier to maintain in a layout.</li>
    <li>The leaves are oval, thick, and grow in pairs along a central, sturdy stem.</li>
    <li>It has a vertical growth habit and can easily grow out of the water (emersed), where it may produce small, beautiful purple flowers.</li>
    <li>It is typically used as a background plant in small tanks or a midground plant in larger aquascapes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is easily done via stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Simply cut the top 3-4 inches of a healthy stem and replant it into the substrate.</li>
    <li>The remaining bottom portion of the stem will soon sprout new side shoots from the nodes where the cut was made, leading to a bushier appearance.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Regular pruning is the key to creating a dense forest look with this plant.</li>
    <li>Because of its sturdy stems, it is often a good choice for tanks with moderately active fish that might uproot more delicate species.</li>
    <li>If the plant reaches the surface, it will continue to grow out of the water; if you prefer the submerged look, trim it before it breaks the surface.</li>
    <li>Bacopa caroliniana is known for its ability to absorb excess nutrients, helping to maintain a biological balance in the aquarium.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 50.00, NULL, 40);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (17, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Pilea nummulariifolia (Creeping Charlie) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Pilea nummulariifolia (Creeping Charlie) Care Guide</h1>

<p>Pilea nummulariifolia, commonly known as "Creeping Charlie," is a lush, perennial evergreen creeper native to the Caribbean and northern South America. It is beloved for its bright green, crinkled leaves that grow along trailing reddish stems, making it a popular choice for hanging baskets, terrariums, and as a ground cover in tropical vivariums.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Pilea nummulariifolia:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Pilea nummulariifolia thrives in bright, indirect light to partial shade.</li>
    <li>Direct sunlight should be avoided as it can easily scorch the delicate, textured leaves, leading to brown patches.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium or vivarium setting, it does well under standard full-spectrum LED lighting. If the light is too low, the plant will become leggy with smaller leaves and longer stems.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This plant loves moisture and should be kept consistently damp but not soggy.</li>
    <li>Ideal humidity: 50-80%. {Link: According to www.glass-box-tropicals.com https://www.glassboxtropicals.com/pilea-nummulariifolia-creeping-charlie/}, it is an excellent candidate for humid enclosures and thrives when the air is moist.</li>
    <li>Water when the surface of the soil just begins to feel dry. It does not tolerate prolonged drought, which causes the leaves to wilt and drop.</li>
    <li>In household environments, regular misting or a pebble tray can help maintain the necessary humidity levels.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 65-80°F (18-27°C).</li>
    <li>As a tropical plant, it is not cold-hardy and should be kept away from drafts or temperatures below 55°F (13°C).</li>
    <li>It enjoys the stable, warm temperatures typically found inside homes or enclosed glass tanks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Use a well-draining potting mix rich in organic matter. A blend of peat moss, perlite, and standard potting soil works well.</li>
    <li>In terrariums, it grows easily in ABG (Atlanta Botanical Gardens) mix or on damp sphagnum moss.</li>
    <li>Feed every 4-6 weeks during the spring and summer growing season with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to half strength.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Pilea nummulariifolia is a fast-growing, trailing vine that can quickly form a dense mat.</li>
    <li>The leaves are round (nummular), deeply veined, and have a puckered or "quilted" texture that adds significant visual interest.</li>
    <li>The stems are slightly fleshy and can root wherever a node touches moist soil, allowing it to "creep" across surfaces effectively.</li>
    <li>It can be used as a cascading plant in hanging pots or as a lush green background/carpet in a vivarium.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is incredibly simple through stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Take a 3-4 inch cutting that includes at least two nodes and place it in water or directly into moist soil or moss.</li>
    <li>Roots usually form within a few days to a week. Once a root system is established, the plant will begin to produce new foliage rapidly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Regular pruning is often necessary to keep the plant contained, especially in smaller terrariums where it can outcompete other species.</li>
    <li>Ensure good air circulation if humidity is very high to prevent fungal issues or leaf rot.</li>
    <li>Pilea nummulariifolia is generally non-toxic to cats and dogs, making it a safe choice for pet-friendly homes.</li>
    <li>If the plant becomes too thin or leggy at the top, pinch back the growing tips to encourage a bushier growth habit.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 30.00, NULL, 39);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (18, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Java Fern Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Java Fern (Microsorum pteropus) Care Guide</h1>

<p>Java Fern is one of the most iconic, beginner-friendly aquarium plants in the hobby. Known for its hardy nature, attractive leaf structure, and ability to thrive in low-tech setups, it is a staple in planted tanks, aquascapes, and low-maintenance aquariums. It naturally grows attached to rocks, wood, and other hard surfaces rather than in substrate, making it incredibly versatile for design.</p>

<p>Here is a complete guide to help you care for Java Fern:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Java Fern grows well in low to medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>High light is not necessary and may cause algae to grow on its leaves.</li>
    <li>Provide 6–10 hours of light daily for steady, healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Under too intense light, leaves may burn or develop black patches.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Very hardy and adaptable to a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li>Temperature: 68–82°F (20–28°C). Ideal range is around 72–78°F.</li>
    <li>pH: 6.0–7.8. Performs well in both slightly acidic and slightly alkaline water.</li>
    <li>GH/KH: Soft to moderately hard water is suitable.</li>
    <li>Tolerates low flow and medium flow, but avoid strong currents directly hitting the leaves long-term.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not required; the plant grows steadily even in non-CO2 tanks.</li>
    <li>Liquid fertilizers improve growth and coloration but are optional.</li>
    <li>Java Fern absorbs nutrients directly from the water column, so root tabs are unnecessary.</li>
    <li>If leaves appear pale or develop transparent spots, consider supplementing potassium and micronutrients.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Java Fern must NOT be buried in substrate. Burying the rhizome will cause it to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach the rhizome to hardscape such as driftwood, lava rock, or stones using super glue gel, cotton thread, or fishing line.</li>
    <li>The roots will naturally anchor over time as the plant attaches itself.</li>
    <li>Can be used as a midground or background plant depending on variety (regular, narrow leaf, trident, windelov, etc.).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Java Fern features long, textured, green leaves emerging from a horizontal rhizome.</li>
    <li>Growth rate is slow to moderate depending on lighting and nutrient availability.</li>
    <li>Dark spots on the underside of leaves are often reproductive spores, not a health issue.</li>
    <li>Leaves may grow up to 12–14 inches depending on the variety.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagates through rhizome division—simply cut the rhizome into sections, each with several leaves.</li>
    <li>Also produces plantlets on older leaves; these can be gently removed and attached to new hardscape.</li>
    <li>Baby Java Ferns grow slowly at first but become hardy once established.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Excellent plant for beginners due to its tolerance of low tech conditions.</li>
    <li>Great for aquariums with fish that uproot plants, such as cichlids or goldfish.</li>
    <li>If algae accumulates on older leaves, trim them off to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Works beautifully in river-style, jungle-style, and nature aquascapes.</li>
    <li>Do not move or disturb the plant often—Java Fern grows best when left undisturbed.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 7, 120.00, NULL, 38);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (19, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Duckweed Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Duckweed (Lemna minor) Care Guide</h1>

<p>Duckweed is one of the smallest and fastest-growing flowering plants in the world. Often appearing as a vibrant green carpet on the surface of ponds and slow-moving rivers, it is a floating aquatic plant that is both loved for its nitrate-sucking abilities and notorious for how quickly it can take over an aquarium. It serves as an excellent natural water purifier and a nutritious snack for many types of fish.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for (or manage) Duckweed:</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is highly adaptable and can grow in almost any lighting condition, from low to high intensity.</li>
    <li>Under high-intensity lighting, the growth rate becomes explosive, and the plant will double its biomass in a matter of days.</li>
    <li>It does best with 8-10 hours of light; however, if the light is too dim, the leaves may become smaller and pale yellow.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is incredibly hardy and can survive in a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 50-86°F (10-30°C). {Link: According to www.aquariumcoop.com https://www.aquariumcoop.com/blogs/aquarium/duckweed}, it can tolerate temperatures near freezing but thrives in warm, tropical water.</li>
    <li>pH range: 5.0-9.0. It is extremely tolerant of various pH levels.</li>
    <li>It prefers still or very slow-moving surface water. Strong surface agitation or heavy bubbles from an air stone can push the plants underwater, causing them to die and rot.</li>
    <li>It is an excellent bioindicator; if the duckweed starts to die, it is often a sign of a significant issue with water quality or nutrient depletion.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not necessary for duckweed because it has direct access to atmospheric CO2 as a floating plant.</li>
    <li>Duckweed is a "nutrient sponge," meaning it is exceptionally efficient at absorbing nitrates, phosphates, and potassium from the water column.</li>
    <li>In tanks with low fish stock, you may need to add a liquid fertilizer to keep it green, as it can quickly strip the water of all available nutrients.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is a strictly floating plant and does not require any substrate.</li>
    <li>To "plant" it, simply place the individual fronds on the surface of the water.</li>
    <li>It is recommended to use a floating "airline tubing ring" if you wish to contain it in one area of the tank and prevent it from blocking light to plants below.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>The plant consists of tiny, oval-shaped green fronds, usually no larger than 5mm in length.</li>
    <li>Each frond has a single, thin root hanging down into the water to stabilize the plant and absorb nutrients.</li>
    <li>It reproduces rapidly through vegetative budding, where new fronds grow out of the sides of the parent plant and then break off.</li>
    <li>It can form a thick mat that blocks out all light to the bottom of the tank, which can be detrimental to submerged plants if not thinned out regularly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation requires no effort from the hobbyist. As long as there are nutrients and light, the plant will propagate itself indefinitely.</li>
    <li>If you wish to move it to another tank, simply scoop a handful out and drop it onto the new water surface.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Duckweed is an excellent way to control algae because it outcompetes algae for nutrients and shades the water.</li>
    <li>Many fish, such as Goldfish, Tilapia, and some Cichlids, love to eat duckweed; it is a great source of protein and fiber.</li>
    <li>Be warned: once introduced, duckweed is very difficult to completely remove from a tank, as a single missed frond can restart the entire colony.</li>
    <li>Regular harvesting is necessary. Use a net to scoop out excess duckweed weekly to ensure that light can still reach your midground and background plants.</li>
    <li>It can be dried and processed into a high-quality homemade fish food flake or pellet.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 10.00, NULL, 37);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (20, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Ficus pumila "Quercifolia" (String of Frogs) Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>String of Frogs (Ficus pumila "Quercifolia") Care Guide</h1>

<p><strong>String of Frogs</strong>, scientifically known as <em>Ficus pumila "Quercifolia"</em> (or Miniature Oakleaf Fig), is a whimsical creeping vine beloved for its tiny, lobed leaves that resemble the silhouette of a frog or a miniature oak leaf. Unlike the larger-leafed Bucephalandra species, this is a terrestrial or semi-aquatic vining plant, making it a perfect addition to the terrestrial portions of a paludarium, terrarium, or as a trailing houseplant.</p>

<blockquote>
    <strong>Note:</strong> Be careful not to confuse this with the succulent <em>Senecio radicans</em>, which is also sometimes called "String of Frogs" but has very different care requirements (low humidity, infrequent watering).
</blockquote>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>String of Frogs</strong> thrives in bright, indirect light.</li>
    <li>It can tolerate lower light levels, but its growth will slow significantly and the internodal spacing (the gap between leaves) will increase, making it look "leggy."</li>
    <li>Avoid direct afternoon sun, which can easily scorch the delicate, thin leaves. Morning sun or filtered light through a sheer curtain is ideal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Watering and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> This plant loves high humidity (60-80%+). It is most successful in enclosed glass containers, vivariums, or near a humidifier. If the air is too dry, the leaf tips will quickly turn brown and crispy.</li>
    <li><strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the substrate consistently moist but not waterlogged. Unlike succulents, it does not like to dry out completely.</li>
    <li>Water when the top 0.5 to 1 inch of soil feels slightly dry to the touch.</li>
    <li>In a terrarium setting, regular misting helps maintain the moisture levels the leaves crave.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 65-80°F (18-27°C).</li>
    <li>It is a tropical plant and is not frost-tolerant. Exposure to temperatures below 55°F (13°C) can cause leaf drop and stunted growth.</li>
    <li>Keep it away from cold drafts, air conditioning vents, or heaters that can dry out the surrounding air.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Substrate:</strong> Use a well-draining, moisture-retaining mix. A blend of potting soil, peat moss or coco coir, and perlite works well.</li>
    <li>In bioactive setups, it grows beautifully on <strong>sphagnum moss</strong> or directly on hardscape features like <strong>hygro-lon</strong> or <strong>cork bark</strong> if kept moist.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertilizer:</strong> Feed once a month during the growing season (spring/summer) with a balanced liquid fertilizer diluted to half strength.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Growth Habit:</strong> It is a vigorous, mat-forming creeper. It uses tiny aerial roots to cling to surfaces like wood, rock, or glass.</li>
    <li><strong>Leaf Detail:</strong> The leaves are typically less than 0.5 inches long and have a distinct three-lobed shape that looks like a frog''s foot or a tiny oak leaf.</li>
    <li><strong>Versatility:</strong> It can be used as a ground cover, a trailing plant in a hanging basket, or a climbing plant on a background wall.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is incredibly easy via stem cuttings.</li>
    <li>Simply snip a 2-3 inch section of the vine and place it on moist sphagnum moss or damp soil.</li>
    <li>Ensure the nodes (where the leaves meet the stem) are in contact with the moist surface; new roots will sprout from these points within 1-2 weeks in high-humidity conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Pruning:</strong> This plant can grow aggressively in a humid environment. Don''t be afraid to trim it back to keep it from smothering slower-growing plants.</li>
    <li><strong>Paludarium Use:</strong> While it loves moisture, the roots should not be submerged in water. Plant it in the "land" section and let it trail down toward the water line.</li>
    <li><strong>Toxicity:</strong> Like most Ficus species, the sap can be a mild irritant to skin and is toxic to pets if ingested. Wear gloves if you have sensitive skin and keep it out of reach of cats and dogs.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 0, 400.00, NULL, 36);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (21, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" is one of the most vibrant and iconic varieties within the Bucephalandra genus. Native to the riverbanks of Borneo, it is famous for its narrow, wavy leaves and its ability to develop a stunning deep red or maroon coloration when grown under optimal conditions. Its fiery appearance makes it a standout choice for adding warm tones to an otherwise green aquascape.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Red Chilli":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" can be grown in low light, but its namesake red coloration is only achieved with medium to high-intensity lighting.</li>
    <li>Full-spectrum lighting is essential for bringing out the deep reds and purples; high-quality RGB LEDs are particularly effective for this variety.</li>
    <li>Maintain a consistent photoperiod of 7-9 hours. Be cautious with high light levels, as the slow growth rate makes the leaves a target for algae like Green Spot Algae (GSA).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like most Bucephalandra, "Red Chilli" thrives in very stable and clean water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 70-78°F (21-26°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/bucephalandra-red-chilli}, keeping the water within this range prevents stress and potential melting of the rhizome.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers slightly acidic water but can adapt to neutral levels if the parameters remain steady.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is vital to prevent debris from accumulating on the small, textured leaves.</li>
    <li>A General Hardness (dGH) between 4-10 is recommended for healthy leaf development.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly recommended to accelerate the slow growth rate and enhance the "Red Chilli" pigmentation.</li>
    <li>Regular liquid fertilization is crucial. It is a water-column feeder that responds well to consistent doses of iron (Fe) and micronutrients.</li>
    <li>A balanced fertilization routine helps the plant maintain its vibrant colors and prevents the older leaves from becoming pale or yellowing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Chilli" is an epiphyte and should never have its rhizome buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>If the rhizome is covered by soil or sand, it will rot, leading to the death of the plant.</li>
    <li>Attach the plant to hardscape elements like lava rock, bogwood, or stones using cyanoacrylate super glue gel or fishing line.</li>
    <li>It is an excellent choice for being placed in the foreground or tucked into the midground crevices of an aquascape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is a slow to medium grower with a compact growth habit.</li>
    <li>The leaves are narrow and lanceolate with significantly wavy (ruffled) margins.</li>
    <li>New leaves typically emerge as a bright red or bronze, eventually maturing into a deep, dark red or purplish-green.</li>
    <li>When submerged and healthy, the leaves display numerous tiny white "galaxy" spots (stomata) that catch the light beautifully.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is straightforward: divide the rhizome using sharp, clean scissors or a scalpel.</li>
    <li>Each piece of divided rhizome should have at least 3-4 leaves and some healthy root growth to ensure survival.</li>
    <li>Secure the new cuttings to hardscape and allow them to establish themselves in a high-flow area.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Red Chilli" is prone to melting if there are sudden shifts in water chemistry or temperature. Avoid introducing it to newly set-up, uncycled tanks.</li>
    <li>If you notice the leaves melting, trim them back but leave the rhizome attached to the hardscape; it will often recover and grow new, adapted leaves.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any decaying matter from the tank to keep the water quality high, which is essential for "Red Chilli."</li>
    <li>Adding Amano shrimp, Otocinclus, or Nerite snails is highly beneficial for keeping the slow-growing leaves free of algae.</li>
    <li>It can also be grown emersed in high-humidity terrariums, though the leaves will generally stay more green and less vibrant than the submerged form.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 300.00, NULL, 35);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (22, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Helena 2014" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Helena 2014" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Helena 2014" is a legendary and highly coveted variety among aquatic plant collectors. Part of the "Helena" family, this specific 2014 variant is famed for its stunning transformation when submerged, evolving from a standard green into deep shades of dark purple, copper, and even reddish-brown. It is often considered a "sister" plant to the famous Brownie Ghost due to its intense coloration and distinct leaf texture.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Helena 2014":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Helena 2014" produces its best colors under medium to high-intensity lighting.</li>
    <li>While it can survive in low light, the plant will likely remain dark green. To achieve the signature deep purple and metallic hues, a high-quality full-spectrum LED light is necessary.</li>
    <li>Be mindful of the photoperiod (6-8 hours); as a slow-growing epiphyte, it is prone to Green Spot Algae (GSA) if the lighting is too intense without adequate CO2 and nutrient balance.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stability is the single most important factor for this rare variety to prevent rhizome melt.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (21-26°C). {Link: According to www.2hraquarist.com https://www.2hraquarist.com/blogs/freshwater-aquarium-plants-guide/how-to-grow-bucephalandra}, keeping the water on the cooler side significantly reduces the risk of "Buce melt."</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It thrives in soft to moderately hard water with a slightly acidic pH.</li>
    <li>Strong water circulation is highly beneficial, as it mimics its natural habitat in Borneo''s fast-flowing streams and prevents organic debris from settling on the textured leaves.</li>
    <li>A GH of 5 or above and a KH of 1-4 will provide the necessary mineral foundation for healthy growth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is strongly recommended for "Helena 2014." It helps the plant grow more robustly and is essential for bringing out the vibrant, dark pigments.</li>
    <li>Regular liquid fertilization (dosing the water column) is required. Focus on micronutrients and iron to support the purple and red coloration.</li>
    <li>In low-tech setups without CO2, the plant will grow significantly slower and may lose its intense metallic sheen.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Helena 2014" is an epiphyte. Do NOT bury the rhizome in the substrate, as it will rot and kill the plant.</li>
    <li>The best practice is to attach the plant to hardscape like lava rock, bogwood, or specialized ceramic mounts using super glue gel (cyanoacrylate) or cotton thread.</li>
    <li>Over time, its roots will anchor themselves firmly to the porous surfaces of your decor.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety has a slow to medium growth rate.</li>
    <li>The leaves are typically oval to elliptical with slightly wavy edges.</li>
    <li>The appearance is characterized by a "velvety" texture and a deep, dark coloration that can range from midnight blue to royal purple under the right conditions.</li>
    <li>Like many premium Bucephalandra, the leaves are covered in tiny silvery dots (stomata) that shimmer like a "galaxy" when submerged.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by dividing the rhizome. Use a sharp, sterilized blade to cut a section of the rhizome that has at least 3-5 healthy leaves and visible roots.</li>
    <li>Once divided, the new cutting can be attached to a new piece of hardscape where it will eventually begin to sprout new lateral buds.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Helena 2014" is a "collector''s plant" and should ideally be placed in a mature, biologically stable aquarium.</li>
    <li>Expect some transition "melt" if the plant was grown emersed; however, do not discard the rhizome. As long as the rhizome is firm, it will sprout new submerged leaves adapted to your tank.</li>
    <li>Keep the aquarium clean with regular water changes to prevent the accumulation of waste, which can trigger algae outbreaks on the slow-growing foliage.</li>
    <li>Shrimp and snails are excellent tank mates as they will gently graze on the leaves, keeping them free of biofilm and dust.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 20, 300.00, NULL, 34);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (23, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Galileo" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Galileo" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Galileo" is a captivating and relatively rare variety of Bucephalandra native to Borneo. It is highly prized for its unique leaf shape and the stunning iridescent colors it displays when submerged. Known for its scientific-sounding name, this variety is a star in high-tech aquascapes, offering a celestial shimmer of blues and purples on its dark foliage.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Galileo":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Galileo" performs best under medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate lower light, higher intensity (within the medium range) is required to trigger the metallic iridescence that defines this variety.</li>
    <li>Full-spectrum LED lighting is recommended to highlight the blue and violet highlights on the leaf surfaces.</li>
    <li>Limit the photoperiod to 6-8 hours to prevent the buildup of algae on its slow-growing leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like most premium Buce varieties, "Galileo" requires very stable water conditions to prevent rhizome melt.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 72-78°F (22-26°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/bucephalandra-galileo}, maintaining a stable, slightly cooler tropical temperature helps ensure the plant''s longevity.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers slightly acidic to neutral water.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is essential to mimic its natural environment in Borneo''s streams and to keep the leaves free of organic waste.</li>
    <li>A dGH of 4-10 and a dKH of 2-5 provide a suitable mineral balance for healthy leaf development.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly recommended for "Galileo" to achieve its maximum color potential and a faster growth rate.</li>
    <li>Without CO2, the plant may remain dark green and grow at an extremely slow pace.</li>
    <li>Regular dosing of a liquid fertilizer containing iron and micronutrients is vital for the health of this water-column feeder.</li>
    <li>Consistent nutrient availability prevents the leaves from developing holes or losing their vibrant sheen.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Galileo" is an epiphytic plant and must not be planted with its rhizome buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>Burying the rhizome will lead to rot and eventually the death of the entire plant.</li>
    <li>Attach the plant to hardscape materials such as lava rock, driftwood, or stones using super glue gel or thread.</li>
    <li>Over time, the roots will anchor themselves firmly to the surfaces of your aquarium décor.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is a slow grower, typical of the Bucephalandra genus.</li>
    <li>The leaves of "Galileo" are generally oval to elliptical with slightly wavy margins.</li>
    <li>Under proper lighting and CO2, the leaves develop a dark, velvety appearance with a distinct metallic blue or purple iridescence.</li>
    <li>Tiny silvery spots (stomata) are often visible on the leaf surface, appearing like a miniature galaxy when viewed closely.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is achieved by dividing the rhizome using sharp, clean scissors or a blade.</li>
    <li>Ensure each divided section has a healthy piece of rhizome, at least 3-4 leaves, and some existing roots.</li>
    <li>Attach the new sections to hardscape to expand your colony or start new growth points in the tank.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Galileo" is sensitive to sudden changes in water chemistry; avoid introducing it to a newly set up, uncycled aquarium.</li>
    <li>If the plant begins to melt upon introduction, do not panic; remove the melting leaves but keep the rhizome, as it often sprouts new, adapted growth.</li>
    <li>Including algae-eating inhabitants like Amano shrimp or Nerite snails will help keep the leaves clean and free of biofilm.</li>
    <li>It is an excellent choice for the midground of an aquarium, especially when tucked into rock crevices where its colors can provide a focal point.</li>
    <li>It can be grown emersed in high-humidity setups, though its metallic colors are best viewed when submerged.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 20, 350.00, NULL, 33);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (24, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" is an elegant and highly distinctive variety of Bucephalandra native to Borneo. It is easily recognized by its exceptionally long, narrow, and needle-like leaves that feature a deep green to dark reddish-brown coloration. This "Super" variant is essentially a more robust or elongated version of the standard Catherineae, making it a perfect choice for creating a flowing, grassy texture on aquarium hardscape.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Super Catherine":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" is quite versatile and can be kept in low to medium lighting.</li>
    <li>Under lower light, the leaves tend to stay a dark, forest green. Under medium light, you may see more bronze or reddish tints on the new growth.</li>
    <li>Because the leaves are very thin and narrow, they are particularly sensitive to hair algae; avoid high-intensity lighting unless the tank is very well-balanced with CO2.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like most Buce species, "Super Catherine" thrives in stable, consistent water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (22-26°C). {Link: According to www.pudr.ca https://www.pudr.ca/product-page/bucephalandra-super-catherine}, maintaining a temperature within the low to mid-70s is ideal for preventing rhizome rot.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers slightly acidic water but can adapt to neutral conditions.</li>
    <li>High water oxygenation and good flow are beneficial, as this variety often grows near waterfalls and fast-moving streams in the wild.</li>
    <li>A GH of 4-8 is recommended to provide the necessary minerals for its unique leaf structure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>While CO2 is not strictly necessary for "Super Catherine," it will help the plant grow a more dense and "bushy" rhizome system.</li>
    <li>Liquid fertilization is highly recommended since it is an epiphyte. A balanced NPK fertilizer with added micronutrients will keep the narrow leaves from becoming brittle.</li>
    <li>Occasional iron supplementation can help enhance the darker, darker-red tones in the foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Super Catherine" is an epiphyte and should never be planted with its rhizome in the substrate.</li>
    <li>The thin rhizome is especially prone to rot if buried. It should be attached to driftwood, rocks, or mesh using super glue gel or thin fishing line.</li>
    <li>Because of its long, flowing leaves, it looks spectacular when attached to higher points of driftwood where the water flow can move the foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Super Catherine" is a slow-growing plant with a creeping growth habit.</li>
    <li>The leaves are its most notable feature: extremely narrow, linear, and slightly wavy along the margins.</li>
    <li>The color is typically a very dark green, often appearing almost black or dark brown in certain lighting.</li>
    <li>It produces a very fine root system that eventually creates a dense mat over whatever surface it is attached to.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is simple: use sharp aquascaping scissors to snip a portion of the rhizome.</li>
    <li>Ensure the cutting has at least 5-6 of the narrow leaves to give it enough energy to establish itself.</li>
    <li>Simply reattach the cutting to a new piece of hardscape and it will eventually begin to sprout new lateral buds.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is excellent for "detail work" in an aquascape, such as filling small gaps between rocks or adding texture to the ends of thin branches.</li>
    <li>"Super Catherine" is known to be relatively hardy once established, but it can melt if moved into a brand-new, uncycled tank.</li>
    <li>Keep the leaves clean of debris; because they are so thin, detritus buildup can quickly lead to localized rot or algae growth.</li>
    <li>Shrimp, especially Amano and Cherry shrimp, love to pick through the narrow leaves of "Super Catherine," keeping them pristine.</li>
    <li>It can be grown emersed in high-humidity setups, where it takes on a slightly more upright growth habit.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 6, 300.00, NULL, 32);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (25, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" is a breathtaking and relatively rare variety of Bucephalandra native to the rainforests of Borneo. It is celebrated by aquascapers for its intense, dark violet tones and narrow, elegant leaves. This variety is particularly prized for its ability to maintain a deep purple hue even under moderate conditions, making it a "splendid" centerpiece for high-end aquascapes.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" thrives in medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate low light, the "purple" brilliance of the leaves is most pronounced under medium to high-intensity full-spectrum lighting.</li>
    <li>Be cautious of algae; because this plant grows slowly, high light without adequate CO2 can lead to Green Spot Algae on the leaf surfaces.</li>
    <li>A photoperiod of 6-8 hours is usually sufficient to maintain color and health.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Consistency is vital; "Splendor Purple" is sensitive to large fluctuations in water chemistry.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (22-26°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/bucephalandra-splendor-purple}, keeping the water on the cooler side of the tropical range helps prevent rhizome rot.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers soft, slightly acidic water which mimics its natural forest stream habitat.</li>
    <li>Strong water movement is recommended to ensure oxygen and nutrients reach the plant and to prevent debris from settling on the foliage.</li>
    <li>Maintain a GH of 5 or above to provide necessary minerals for leaf development.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly recommended to bring out the deepest purple and blue iridescence.</li>
    <li>Without CO2, the plant may lean more toward a dark olive green with only subtle purple highlights.</li>
    <li>Regular liquid fertilization is essential, as this plant primarily feeds from the water column.</li>
    <li>A fertilizer rich in iron (Fe) and micronutrients will help sustain the vibrant, dark pigmentation of the leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Splendor Purple" is an epiphyte and must never be buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>Burying the rhizome prevents the plant from "breathing" and will cause it to rot and die.</li>
    <li>Secure the plant to hardscape like driftwood, lava rock, or stones using super glue gel or dark cotton thread.</li>
    <li>It can be tucked into small cracks in rocks where its roots will eventually grip the surface.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is a slow-growing plant with a compact and bushy growth habit.</li>
    <li>The leaves are typically narrow and elongated with slightly wavy edges and a pointed tip.</li>
    <li>The coloration is its most famous trait: deep, velvety purple and dark indigo hues that shimmer with a metallic sheen under light.</li>
    <li>The leaf surface is often peppered with tiny white spots (stomata) that give it a "starry" appearance when submerged.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is achieved by carefully cutting the rhizome into smaller sections.</li>
    <li>Ensure each cutting has at least 3-4 leaves and a few healthy roots attached.</li>
    <li>Reattach the new sections to your hardscape. Over time, these will grow into new, dense clumps.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Splendor Purple" is prone to melting if introduced to an uncycled or unstable aquarium; wait until your tank is established before adding it.</li>
    <li>If melting occurs, remove the decaying leaves but leave the rhizome in place, as it often produces new shoots once it adapts.</li>
    <li>Using Amano shrimp or Nerite snails is highly effective for keeping the slow-growing leaves free of algae and biofilm.</li>
    <li>Regularly trim dead or yellowing leaves near the base of the rhizome to encourage the plant to put energy into new, colorful growth.</li>
    <li>It can also be grown emersed in high-humidity terrariums, though the purple color is far more intense when grown submerged.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 350.00, NULL, 31);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (26, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Upper Ghost" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Upper Ghost" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Upper Ghost" is a premium and visually captivating variety of Bucephalandra. Similar to the famous "Brownie Ghost," it is highly prized for its iridescent leaves that can display a spectrum of colors including deep purple, metallic blue, and dark green. Its name "Upper" typically refers to the specific region or elevation in Borneo where this variant was discovered, often denoting a slightly different leaf shape or color intensity than standard Ghost varieties.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Upper Ghost":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Upper Ghost" performs best under medium lighting.</li>
    <li>While it can survive in low light, its signature "ghostly" iridescence and purple hues are only fully realized with adequate light intensity.</li>
    <li>Using a full-spectrum RGB LED light is highly recommended, as the red and blue diodes will make the metallic colors "pop" much more than standard white LEDs.</li>
    <li>Target a photoperiod of 6-8 hours; avoid excessive intensity to prevent Green Spot Algae from forming on the slow-growing leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stability is paramount; "Upper Ghost" is a high-end variety that can be sensitive to sudden shifts in water chemistry.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-79°F (21-26°C). It prefers slightly cooler water, which helps prevent melting and rot.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It thrives in soft, slightly acidic water.</li>
    <li>Moderate water flow is essential. In its natural habitat, it grows in fast-moving streams; in an aquarium, flow helps deliver nutrients and keeps the leaves clean of organic waste.</li>
    <li>Maintain a General Hardness (dGH) of 5-10 for essential mineral intake.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly recommended for "Upper Ghost." While not strictly required for survival, it is the key to achieving the vibrant purple and blue coloration this plant is known for.</li>
    <li>It is a heavy feeder via the water column. Regular dosing of liquid fertilizers is necessary.</li>
    <li>Ensure adequate Iron (Fe) levels to support the deep pigmentation, but balance this with consistent water changes to prevent nutrient spikes that lead to algae.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Upper Ghost" is an epiphyte. The rhizome must never be buried in substrate, as this will lead to rot and plant death.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape materials such as lava rock, driftwood, or specialized ceramic rings using super glue gel or fishing line.</li>
    <li>It looks best when planted in small clumps, which allows the new, more colorful leaves to stand out against the older, darker foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is a slow grower, typically producing only a few leaves per month even in high-tech setups.</li>
    <li>The leaves are generally small to medium-sized with slightly wavy margins.</li>
    <li>Submerged growth is where the "Ghost" name comes to life, featuring a distinct metallic sheen and tiny white "galaxy" spots (stomata) across the leaf surface.</li>
    <li>Newly sprouted leaves often appear bright reddish or light purple, eventually darkening into a deep, velvety indigo or black-green as they mature.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by dividing the rhizome once the plant has formed a healthy cluster.</li>
    <li>Using sharp, sterilized scissors, cut the rhizome into pieces that have at least 3-5 leaves and some visible roots.</li>
    <li>Avoid over-pruning, as the slow growth rate means the mother plant needs time to recover and sprout new heads.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Upper Ghost" is best introduced to a mature, biologically stable aquarium. Adding it to a brand-new "unseasoned" tank increases the risk of melting.</li>
    <li>If the plant experiences melt (losing leaves), do not discard it. As long as the rhizome remains firm and hard, it can regrow new leaves that are better adapted to your water.</li>
    <li>A clean tank is vital. Enlist a "cleanup crew" of Amano shrimp or Nerite snails to keep the leaf surfaces free of biofilm and algae.</li>
    <li>Because of its rarity and beauty, it is often used as a high-value focal point in the foreground or midground of an aquascape.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 300.00, NULL, 30);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (27, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" (also frequently known as Bucephalandra Kishii) is one of the most impressive and largest varieties of Bucephalandra. Native to the Kalimantan region of Borneo, it is famous for its dark, leathery leaves and deeply prominent veins that resemble a ribcage, giving it its "Skeleton" name.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Skeleton King":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" is adaptable but prefers low to medium lighting.</li>
    <li>High intensity lighting is not necessary and can lead to issues with Black Brush Algae (BBA) on its slow-growing, textured leaves.</li>
    <li>A PAR level between 30-50 umols is sufficient to maintain its dark, dramatic coloration and healthy leaf structure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Like most large-leaf Bucephalandra, "Skeleton King" requires very stable water conditions and clean environments.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 71-78°F (21-26°C). {Link: According to www.aquasabi.com https://www.aquasabi.com/Bucephalandra-sp-Skeleton-King}, it is particularly sensitive to high temperatures, so keeping the water on the cooler side is recommended.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It thrives in slightly acidic water with low to moderate carbonate hardness.</li>
    <li>Good water circulation is vital to prevent detritus from settling in the deep grooves of the leaf veins.</li>
    <li>It is more sensitive to poor water quality than smaller varieties, so regular water changes are essential.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is highly beneficial for this variety; because it is so slow-growing, CO2 helps the plant recover from any leaf damage or melt.</li>
    <li>It responds well to regular water column fertilization, particularly with a focus on iron and potassium.</li>
    <li>Adequate nutrients help maintain the "black" or dark forest green color of the leaves and prevent the veins from yellowing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Skeleton King" is a robust epiphyte with a thick rhizome that must never be buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>Due to its larger size and weight, it should be firmly attached to large rocks or heavy driftwood using super glue gel or thick fishing line.</li>
    <li>Over time, it develops an extensive and powerful root system that will anchor itself deeply into the crevices of your hardscape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This is a very slow-growing plant, often producing only one leaf every few weeks or even months.</li>
    <li>The leaves are large, oval, and have a distinctively "rugged" or matte texture compared to the shiny leaves of other Buce varieties.</li>
    <li>The most striking feature is the deeply indented venation on the upper surface of the leaf.</li>
    <li>Colors range from a deep olive green to a nearly metallic black-purple, with a pale or reddish underside.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by dividing the thick rhizome, but should only be attempted on mature specimens.</li>
    <li>Use a very sharp, sterilized blade to ensure a clean cut through the woody rhizome.</li>
    <li>Ensure each section has at least two or three healthy leaves and a portion of the root system to support new growth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Skeleton King" is often considered a "collector''s plant" due to its unique look and slower growth rate.</li>
    <li>It is particularly susceptible to melting if moved between vastly different water parameters, so acclimate it slowly.</li>
    <li>Because of the deep ridges on the leaves, keeping a crew of algae-eating shrimp is highly recommended to keep the leaf surfaces clean.</li>
    <li>If the plant produces a flower (a small white spathe), it is a sign that the plant is healthy and well-established.</li>
    <li>It can be grown emersed in very high humidity, where it often grows slightly faster than its submerged counterpart.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 700.00, NULL, 29);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (28, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" is a rare and highly attractive variety of Bucephalandra, cherished for its unique pinkish-green leaves and vibrant pink-tinted stems. This endemic species from the island of Borneo provides a subtle but beautiful pop of color to any aquascape, making it a favorite among collectors and enthusiasts.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Pink Lady":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" thrives under low to moderate lighting levels.</li>
    <li>While the plant can tolerate low light, medium lighting is recommended to intensify the pink and red hues in the leaves and stems.</li>
    <li>As a slow grower, it is susceptible to algae; keep lighting levels balanced (typically below 50 umols) to maintain healthy, clean foliage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stable water conditions are essential for "Pink Lady" to thrive and avoid the dreaded "Buce melt."</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 72-82°F (22-28°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/pink-lady}, this variety adapts well to standard tropical temperatures within this stable range.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0. It prefers slightly acidic to neutral water (pH 6.0-7.0) for optimal health.</li>
    <li>Clean, well-filtered water with good flow is important, as these plants naturally grow in fast-moving streams.</li>
    <li>Moderate water hardness (dGH 4-12) is beneficial for long-term leaf health.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly required but is highly recommended to improve growth rates and coloration.</li>
    <li>Regular dosing of a liquid fertilizer containing iron and micronutrients is helpful for maintaining the distinctive pink tints.</li>
    <li>It primarily draws nutrients from the water column, so maintaining a consistent fertilization schedule will lead to better results.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pink Lady" is an epiphytic plant and must not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, or it will rot.</li>
    <li>Attach the plant to hardscape like driftwood or rocks using super glue gel or thread.</li>
    <li>If you wish to place it in the substrate, ensure only the roots are buried while the rhizome remains completely exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>This variety is very slow-growing, with new leaves sprouting from thin, long rhizomes.</li>
    <li>It is characterized by olive-green leaves with pronounced midribs and stems that display a clear pink or reddish hue.</li>
    <li>Mature leaves can grow up to 1.5 to 3 inches long, making it a larger variant compared to many "mini" types.</li>
    <li>The foliage often features tiny white dots (stomata) and can exhibit an iridescent sheen under the right lighting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by carefully cutting the rhizome into sections with sharp scissors.</li>
    <li>Ensure each section has at least a few leaves and healthy roots before reattaching it to a new surface.</li>
    <li>It is best to allow the plant to establish a healthy clump before attempting to divide the rhizome.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Be patient during the acclimation period; "Pink Lady" may experience some leaf melt when first introduced to a new environment.</li>
    <li>Regular maintenance, such as removing decaying leaves and cleaning organic waste, helps prevent algae issues.</li>
    <li>The plant can be grown emersed in high-humidity setups like paludariums, though its submerged form usually shows the best color.</li>
    <li>Avoid making drastic changes to water parameters, as stability is the key success factor for all Bucephalandra species.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 4, 650.00, NULL, 28);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (29, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Godzilla" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Godzilla" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Godzilla" is a popular and visually striking variety known for its unique leaf texture and robust growth. Originating from Borneo, it is characterized by its long, narrow leaves with highly ruffled or "crinkled" edges, giving it a prehistoric and dramatic appearance that stands out in any aquascape.</p>

<p>Here''s a detailed guide to help you care for Bucephalandra "Godzilla":</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Godzilla" is adaptable and can thrive in low to medium-high lighting.</li>
    <li>Under medium to high light, the plant will develop more intense coloration, often displaying deep greens, blues, and even purple hues.</li>
    <li>To prevent algae from attaching to its textured, slow-growing leaves, a moderate PAR level of 40-60 umols is generally recommended.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stability is crucial for "Godzilla" to prevent the rhizome from melting during acclimation.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature: 70-80°F (21-27°C). {Link: According to www.buceplant.com https://buceplant.com/products/bucephalandra-godzilla}, maintaining a stable tropical temperature is essential for long-term health.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. It prefers slightly acidic water but is tolerant of a wider range if kept stable.</li>
    <li>This variety thrives in areas of the tank with high water flow, which helps deliver nutrients and prevents organic waste from settling on the ruffled leaves.</li>
    <li>A dGH of 5 or higher is beneficial for maintaining the structural integrity of its thick leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>While "Godzilla" can be grown without CO2, supplementation will result in much faster growth and more pronounced "waves" on the leaf margins.</li>
    <li>It is a heavy feeder from the water column; regular dosing of a comprehensive liquid fertilizer is necessary.</li>
    <li>Iron and trace element supplementation is particularly helpful for enhancing the iridescent sheen that makes this variety so attractive.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Godzilla" is an epiphytic plant and must not have its rhizome buried in the substrate.</li>
    <li>Burying the rhizome will lead to anaerobic rot and the eventual death of the plant.</li>
    <li>It should be attached to hardscape, such as lava rocks, dragon stone, or driftwood, using aquarium-safe super glue gel or dark thread.</li>
    <li>Its root system is quite strong once established and will grip tightly to porous surfaces.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Godzilla" is a slow to moderate grower compared to other Buce varieties.</li>
    <li>The leaves are long, narrow, and feature extremely wavy or ruffled edges that give the plant a "spiky" look.</li>
    <li>Mature leaves are typically a dark, forest green with a bluish iridescence, while new growth can appear reddish or bronze.</li>
    <li>The leaves are covered in tiny silver spots (stomata) that are clearly visible under good lighting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is straightforward: simply divide the rhizome using sharp, sterilized scissors or a blade.</li>
    <li>Each divided section should have at least 2-3 leaves and a portion of the rhizome to ensure successful regrowth.</li>
    <li>Once cut, the new sections can be glued or tied to different areas of the hardscape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Because of its ruffled leaves, "Godzilla" is a magnet for detritus; regular turkey basting or high flow helps keep it clean.</li>
    <li>If the plant experiences "melt" upon introduction, remove the decaying leaves but leave the rhizome alone, as it will likely sprout new, adapted growth.</li>
    <li>Algae-eating inhabitants like Amano shrimp or Otocinclus are highly recommended to keep the slow-growing leaves pristine.</li>
    <li>It is a versatile plant that works exceptionally well as a focal point on midground wood or tucked into crevices between rocks.</li>
    <li>Can be grown emersed in high-humidity environments, though the leaf ruffles may be less pronounced than in the submerged form.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 5, 300.00, NULL, 27);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (30, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Black Angel" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Black Angel" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Black Angel" is a striking and relatively rare variety of Bucephalandra native to the streams of Borneo. As the name suggests, it is prized for its exceptionally dark foliage, which can appear almost black or deep indigo under aquarium lighting, providing a dramatic contrast against lighter green plants.</p>

