    <li>The underside of the leaves usually features a lighter, silvery or reddish tint.</li>
    <li>It stays relatively compact, making it excellent for midground placement on hardscape.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagation is done by rhizome division.</li>
    <li>Use sharp scissors or a scalpel to cut the rhizome into pieces, ensuring each piece has at least 3-4 leaves and a few healthy roots.</li>
    <li>Glue or tie the new cuttings onto new pieces of hardscape or rock rubble.</li>
    <li>Do not propagate too frequently, as the plant grows slowly and needs time to recover.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Deep Purple" is prone to "Buce Melt" if water parameters change too drastically (e.g., moving from one tank to another).</li>
    <li>If the leaves melt, do not throw the plant away immediately; keep the rhizome in the tank, as it often regenerates new, adapted leaves after a few weeks.</li>
    <li>Algae eaters like Amano Shrimp, Otocinclus, or Nerite Snails are excellent tank mates to keep the leaves clean without damaging the plant.</li>
    <li>It can be grown emersed in terrariums, but the leaves will likely be green rather than purple until submerged again.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>
```', 20, 300.00, NULL, 25);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (32, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Christmas Moss Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Christmas Moss Care Guide</h1>

<p>Christmas Moss (Vesicularia montagnei) is a popular aquatic moss native to Southeast Asia, commonly found in tropical regions of India, Thailand, and the Philippines. This attractive moss gets its name from its distinctive triangular, Christmas tree-like growth pattern. It''s highly valued in aquascaping for creating natural-looking underwater landscapes and provides excellent shelter for small fish and invertebrates. Christmas moss is considered relatively easy to grow and maintain, making it suitable for both beginners and experienced aquarists.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss can thrive in low to medium lighting conditions, making it very versatile.</li>
    <li>Low light (1-2 watts per gallon) is sufficient for healthy growth and maintenance.</li>
    <li>Medium lighting enhances growth rate and creates denser, more vibrant moss coverage.</li>
    <li>High lighting can promote algae growth on the moss, which can be difficult to remove.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss is adaptable to a wide range of water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 68-78°F (20-25°C) for optimal growth.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5, with slightly acidic to neutral water preferred.</li>
    <li>Water hardness: 5-15 dGH, tolerant of both soft and moderately hard water.</li>
    <li>Good water circulation helps prevent debris accumulation and promotes healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Regular water changes help maintain water quality and prevent algae issues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not required but will significantly enhance growth rate and density.</li>
    <li>Can grow well without CO2 supplementation in low-tech aquarium setups.</li>
    <li>Liquid fertilizers containing micronutrients benefit overall health and coloration.</li>
    <li>Iron supplementation helps maintain the rich green coloration of the moss.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss does not require substrate as it attaches to surfaces naturally.</li>
    <li>Attach to driftwood, rocks, or aquarium decorations using fishing line or cotton thread.</li>
    <li>Can be secured to mesh or plastic grids to create moss walls or carpets.</li>
    <li>Allow several weeks for the moss to naturally attach before removing tying materials.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss is a slow to moderate-growing aquatic moss.</li>
    <li>Features distinctive triangular, Christmas tree-like branching patterns.</li>
    <li>Individual fronds can grow 2-4 inches long with proper conditions.</li>
    <li>Creates dense, carpet-like coverage when allowed to spread naturally.</li>
    <li>Rich green coloration that can vary from bright to dark green depending on conditions.</li>
    <li>Forms thick mats over time, providing excellent hiding places for fish and invertebrates.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by dividing existing moss colonies into smaller portions.</li>
    <li>Simply cut or tear healthy sections and attach to new locations.</li>
    <li>Each small piece can develop into a full colony given time and proper conditions.</li>
    <li>Propagation is most successful during periods of active growth in spring and summer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Christmas moss is hardy and forgiving, making it excellent for beginners.</li>
    <li>Regular pruning helps maintain shape and prevents excessive thickness that blocks light.</li>
    <li>Remove any dead or brown sections promptly to prevent decay and water quality issues.</li>
    <li>Gentle water flow helps keep the moss clean and free from debris accumulation.</li>
    <li>Compatible with most community fish and is especially beneficial for breeding tanks.</li>
    <li>Provides natural filtration by absorbing nutrients and producing oxygen during photosynthesis.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 4, 100.00, NULL, 24);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (33, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Peperomia Raisinet Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Peperomia Raisinet Care Guide</h1>

<p>Peperomia Raisinet (Peperomia clusiifolia ''Red Edge'') is a striking succulent-like houseplant native to the Caribbean and South America. This compact variety is prized for its thick, waxy leaves that feature beautiful cream and green variegation with distinctive red or pink edges. The leaves are spoon-shaped and arranged in a rosette pattern, creating an attractive, sculptural appearance that makes it popular among collectors.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia Raisinet thrives in bright, indirect light conditions.</li>
    <li>Bright light enhances the red edge coloration and maintains compact growth.</li>
    <li>Can tolerate medium light but red edges may fade and growth may slow.</li>
    <li>Avoid direct sunlight as it can scorch the thick leaves and cause burning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water when the top 2 inches of soil are completely dry, typically every 10-14 days.</li>
    <li>Peperomia Raisinet stores water in its thick, succulent-like leaves making it very drought-tolerant.</li>
    <li>Water thoroughly but allow excess water to drain completely to prevent root rot.</li>
    <li>Reduce watering significantly in winter when growth slows down.</li>
    <li>Use room temperature water and avoid getting water on the leaves.</li>
    <li>Overwatering is more harmful than underwatering for this species.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 supplementation is not necessary for this houseplant.</li>
    <li>Feed monthly during spring and summer with diluted liquid fertilizer.</li>
    <li>Use balanced fertilizer (10-10-10) diluted to quarter strength to prevent burning.</li>
    <li>Reduce feeding to every 2-3 months during fall and winter dormancy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requires very well-draining, lightweight potting mix similar to cactus soil.</li>
    <li>Ideal soil blend: 40% potting soil, 30% perlite, 20% coarse sand, 10% orchid bark.</li>
    <li>Choose pots with multiple drainage holes; terracotta pots are excellent for moisture control.</li>
    <li>Shallow, wide pots work well as it has a compact, spreading growth habit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia Raisinet is a slow-growing, compact succulent-type plant.</li>
    <li>Features thick, waxy spoon-shaped leaves with cream and green variegation.</li>
    <li>Distinctive red or pink edges on leaves that intensify with proper lighting.</li>
    <li>Grows in a rosette pattern reaching 6-8 inches in height and spread.</li>
    <li>Produces small, inconspicuous flower spikes on mature, healthy plants.</li>
    <li>Leaves may develop more intense red coloration under slight stress conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Leaf cuttings are the most reliable propagation method.</li>
    <li>Cut healthy leaves at the base including a small portion of stem.</li>
    <li>Allow cut surfaces to callus for 24-48 hours before planting.</li>
    <li>Root in well-draining cactus mix under bright, indirect light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia Raisinet is very easy to care for and extremely drought-tolerant.</li>
    <li>Sensitive to overwatering, so water sparingly and ensure good drainage.</li>
    <li>Remove any soft, mushy, or yellowing leaves immediately to prevent rot.</li>
    <li>Clean leaves occasionally with a damp cloth to maintain their waxy appearance.</li>
    <li>The plant is non-toxic to pets and children, making it household safe.</li>
    <li>Temperature range of 65-80°F (18-27°C) is ideal for optimal growth.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 120.00, NULL, 23);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (34, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Syngonium Tiffany Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Syngonium Tiffany Care Guide</h1>

<p>Syngonium Tiffany (Syngonium podophyllum ''Tiffany'') is a stunning variegated climbing vine native to Central and South America. This captivating variety is prized for its arrow-shaped leaves that display beautiful cream, white, and green variegation with subtle pink undertones. As the plant matures, it transitions from compact juvenile leaves to larger, lobed adult foliage when provided with climbing support. The high variegation makes this cultivar particularly sought after among houseplant enthusiasts.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Tiffany requires bright, indirect light to maintain its striking variegation.</li>
    <li>Higher light levels enhance the cream and white markings while preventing reversion to green.</li>
    <li>Can tolerate medium light but variegation may fade and growth may slow significantly.</li>
    <li>Avoid direct sunlight as it can scorch the delicate variegated leaves and cause browning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water when the top 1-2 inches of soil feel dry, typically every 7-10 days in growing season.</li>
    <li>Maintain consistent moisture but avoid waterlogged conditions that can cause root rot.</li>
    <li>Use filtered or distilled water when possible as variegated plants can be sensitive to chemicals.</li>
    <li>Reduce watering frequency in winter when growth naturally slows down.</li>
    <li>Water thoroughly until excess drains from drainage holes, then empty saucers.</li>
    <li>Yellow leaves typically indicate overwatering while crispy edges suggest underwatering.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 supplementation is not necessary for this houseplant.</li>
    <li>Feed every 2-4 weeks during spring and summer with balanced liquid fertilizer.</li>
    <li>Use diluted fertilizer (half strength) to prevent burning the sensitive variegated foliage.</li>
    <li>Reduce feeding to monthly or stop completely during fall and winter dormancy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requires well-draining, airy potting mix to prevent root rot and promote healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Ideal soil blend: 40% potting soil, 20% perlite, 20% orchid bark, 20% coconut coir.</li>
    <li>Choose pots with adequate drainage holes to prevent water accumulation.</li>
    <li>Repot annually in spring, increasing pot size by 1-2 inches in diameter.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Tiffany is a fast-growing climbing vine when provided with support.</li>
    <li>Features arrow-shaped juvenile leaves with cream, white, and green variegation.</li>
    <li>Mature leaves become larger and more lobed when the plant is allowed to climb.</li>
    <li>Can reach 3-6 feet indoors with proper support and care.</li>
    <li>High variegation makes this cultivar more light-sensitive than solid green varieties.</li>
    <li>Pink undertones may appear in optimal lighting conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stem cuttings root easily in water or moist potting mix.</li>
    <li>Cut 4-6 inch stems with at least 2-3 nodes for best success rates.</li>
    <li>Remove lower leaves and place cuttings in water or directly into moist soil.</li>
    <li>Water propagation typically shows roots in 2-4 weeks under bright, indirect light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Tiffany requires more attention than solid green varieties due to high variegation.</li>
    <li>Provide moss poles or trellises to encourage climbing and larger leaf development.</li>
    <li>Maintain humidity levels of 50-60% to prevent brown leaf edges and promote health.</li>
    <li>Pinch growing tips regularly to encourage bushier, more compact growth.</li>
    <li>The plant is toxic to pets and children due to calcium oxalate crystals.</li>
    <li>Temperature range of 65-80°F (18-27°C) is ideal for optimal growth and variegation.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 50.00, NULL, 22);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (35, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Peperomia caperata Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Peperomia caperata Care Guide</h1>

<p>Peperomia caperata, commonly known as Emerald Ripple Peperomia or Radiator Plant, is a charming compact houseplant native to Brazil. This popular species is prized for its distinctive heart-shaped leaves with deeply ridged, quilted texture that creates beautiful ripple patterns. The leaves are typically dark green with silvery veining, though many cultivars exist with variations in color including red, burgundy, and variegated forms.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia caperata thrives in bright, indirect light conditions.</li>
    <li>Can tolerate medium light but may lose some vibrant coloration and compact growth.</li>
    <li>Avoid direct sunlight as it can scorch the delicate leaves and fade their patterns.</li>
    <li>Insufficient light causes elongated stems and loss of the characteristic compact form.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water when the top 1-2 inches of soil are dry, typically every 7-10 days.</li>
    <li>Peperomia caperata stores water in its thick leaves making it drought-tolerant.</li>
    <li>Water at soil level to avoid getting water on the textured leaves which can cause fungal issues.</li>
    <li>Ensure thorough drainage as waterlogged soil quickly leads to root rot.</li>
    <li>Reduce watering frequency in winter when growth naturally slows down.</li>
    <li>Overwatering is the most common cause of plant failure with this species.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 supplementation is not necessary for this houseplant.</li>
    <li>Feed monthly during spring and summer with diluted liquid fertilizer.</li>
    <li>Use balanced fertilizer (10-10-10) diluted to half strength to prevent burning.</li>
    <li>Reduce feeding to every 2-3 months during fall and winter dormancy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requires extremely well-draining, lightweight potting mix to prevent root rot.</li>
    <li>Ideal soil blend: 50% quality potting soil, 30% perlite, 20% orchid bark.</li>
    <li>Choose pots with drainage holes; terracotta pots help prevent overwatering.</li>
    <li>Shallow pots are preferred as it has a small, fibrous root system.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia caperata is a slow-growing, compact plant.</li>
    <li>Features heart-shaped leaves with deeply ridged, quilted texture.</li>
    <li>Leaves are typically 2-4 inches long with distinctive ripple patterns.</li>
    <li>Can reach a height and spread of 8-10 inches when mature.</li>
    <li>Produces small, cream-colored flower spikes on healthy plants.</li>
    <li>Many cultivars available with varying leaf colors from green to burgundy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Leaf cuttings are the most successful propagation method.</li>
    <li>Cut healthy leaves with petioles attached for best results.</li>
    <li>Allow cut surfaces to dry for 24 hours before planting.</li>
    <li>Root in well-draining mix under bright, indirect light.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Peperomia caperata is relatively easy to care for and beginner-friendly.</li>
    <li>Sensitive to overwatering, so err on the side of underwatering.</li>
    <li>Remove yellowing or damaged leaves promptly to prevent disease spread.</li>
    <li>Clean leaves gently with a soft brush to remove dust from ridged surfaces.</li>
    <li>The plant is non-toxic to pets and children, making it household safe.</li>
    <li>Temperature range of 65-75°F (18-24°C) is ideal for growth.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 100.00, NULL, 21);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (36, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Syngonium Pink Arrowhead Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Syngonium Pink Arrowhead Care Guide</h1>

<p>The Syngonium Pink Arrowhead (Syngonium podophyllum ''Pink'') is a stunning tropical houseplant prized for its heart-shaped leaves that display beautiful pink, green, and cream variegation. Also known as Pink Splash or Pink Allusion, this climbing vine starts with compact arrow-shaped juvenile leaves that gradually develop into larger, lobed mature leaves as the plant climbs. Native to Central and South America, this plant is beloved for its vibrant coloration and relatively easy care requirements.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bright, indirect light is ideal for maintaining the pink coloration and healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Place near an east or north-facing window, or 2-3 feet from a south/west window with a sheer curtain.</li>
    <li>Direct sunlight will scorch the delicate pink leaves and cause them to fade or turn brown.</li>
    <li>Insufficient light causes the pink coloration to fade, leaves become more green, and growth slows significantly.</li>
    <li>LED grow lights can supplement natural light, especially in winter. Keep 12-18 inches away for 10-12 hours daily.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Requirements</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water when the top 1-2 inches of soil feel dry, typically every 7-10 days in growing season.</li>
    <li>Water thoroughly until excess drains from drainage holes, then empty saucers after 30 minutes.</li>
    <li>Use filtered, distilled, or rainwater when possible for best results.</li>
    <li>Tap water should sit 24 hours to allow chlorine to evaporate before use.</li>
    <li>Yellow leaves indicate overwatering, while droopy leaves with crispy edges suggest underwatering.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Temperature and Humidity</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 65-80°F (18-27°C) with day temperatures of 70-80°F (21-27°C).</li>
    <li>Night temperatures should remain between 65-70°F (18-21°C).</li>
    <li>Avoid temperatures below 60°F (15°C) as this can damage the plant.</li>
    <li>Prefers 50-60% humidity for optimal pink coloration and leaf health.</li>
    <li>Increase humidity using humidifiers, pebble trays, grouping plants together, or misting around the plant.</li>
    <li>Ensure gentle air movement to prevent fungal issues, but avoid cold drafts from AC/heating vents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Soil and Potting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Requires well-draining, airy potting mix to prevent root rot.</li>
    <li>Ideal soil blend: 40% quality potting soil, 20% perlite, 20% orchid bark, 20% coconut coir.</li>
    <li>Choose pots with drainage holes; terracotta is excellent for moisture regulation.</li>
    <li>Start with 4-6 inch pots for young plants, repotting annually in spring.</li>
    <li>Repot when roots grow from drainage holes or water drains too quickly.</li>
    <li>Slightly acidic to neutral soil (pH 5.5-7.0) is ideal for optimal nutrient uptake.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Feed every 2-4 weeks during spring and summer with balanced liquid fertilizer (10-10-10 or 20-20-20).</li>
    <li>Dilute fertilizer to half strength to prevent burning the roots.</li>
    <li>Reduce feeding to monthly or stop completely in fall and winter when growth slows.</li>
    <li>Resume regular fertilization in spring when new growth appears.</li>
    <li>Over-fertilizing signs include brown leaf tips, excessive green growth, or white salt buildup on soil.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pruning and Support</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Regular pruning maintains compact growth and encourages pink juvenile leaves.</li>
    <li>Best pruning time is spring/early summer for optimal recovery.</li>
    <li>Remove yellowing leaves, leggy vines, and trim for desired shape using clean, sharp tools.</li>
    <li>Provide moss poles, trellises, or stakes to support climbing growth habit.</li>
    <li>Supporting the vine encourages larger, more mature leaf development.</li>
    <li>Sterilize pruning tools with rubbing alcohol between cuts to prevent disease spread.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Water propagation is the easiest method for beginners.</li>
    <li>Cut 4-6 inch stems with at least 2-3 nodes (small bumps where roots emerge).</li>
    <li>Remove lower leaves, keeping 2-3 leaves at the top of the cutting.</li>
    <li>Place cutting in clean water with nodes submerged, changing water every 3-5 days.</li>
    <li>Roots typically develop in 2-4 weeks in bright, indirect light.</li>
    <li>Transplant to soil once roots are 2+ inches long for best success rate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common Problems</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Pink color fading: Usually caused by insufficient light, overwatering, or excessive fertilization.</li>
    <li>Yellow leaves: Typically indicates overwatering, root rot, or natural leaf aging.</li>
    <li>Brown leaf edges: Results from low humidity, fluoride in water, or over-fertilizing.</li>
    <li>Drooping leaves: Usually caused by underwatering, being root bound, or temperature stress.</li>
    <li>Leggy growth: Indicates insufficient light or natural climbing behavior requiring support.</li>
    <li>Spider mites, aphids, and mealybugs can be treated with increased humidity and insecticidal soap.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Syngonium Pink Arrowhead is relatively easy to care for and suitable for beginners.</li>
    <li>Rotate the plant weekly for even growth and coloration throughout the foliage.</li>
    <li>Clean leaves monthly with a damp cloth to improve light absorption and plant health.</li>
    <li>Group with other tropical plants to create a humid microclimate naturally.</li>
    <li>Pinch growing tips regularly to encourage bushier, more compact growth habit.</li>
    <li>Watch for seasonal changes as growth naturally slows during winter months.</li>
    <li>Keep away from pets and children as the plant contains toxic calcium oxalate crystals.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 10, 50.00, NULL, 20);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (37, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Boyan" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Boyan" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Boyan" is a slow-growing variety of Bucephalandra that is relatively easy to care for, making it suitable for both beginner and experienced aquascapers. Native to Borneo, these plants are known for their ability to thrive in flowing water and attach themselves to hardscape.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Boyan" can grow well in low to moderate lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While low light is sufficient for survival, moderate lighting can promote faster growth and enhance coloration.</li>
    <li>If using higher light levels, be mindful of potential algae growth, as Bucephalandra grows slowly.</li>
    <li>Some aquascapers place Bucephalandra in shaded areas to help prevent algae issues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Maintain stable water parameters for optimal health.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 71-79°F (21-26°C), although they can tolerate a wider range of 70-82°F (21-28°C).</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, mimicking its natural habitat in fast-moving streams.</li>
    <li>Clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes is essential.</li>
    <li>Some water hardness (GH) is helpful, preferably 5 dGH or above.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly required but can promote faster growth and more vibrant coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer can be beneficial.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer is recommended, particularly if using CO2 injection.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Boyan" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can cause it to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape, such as rocks or driftwood, using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If placing it in the substrate, ensure the roots are covered but the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Boyan" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It typically grows up to 3-6 cm (1.5-2.5 inches) tall.</li>
    <li>Some varieties, like "Mini Narrow," are known for their compact size.</li>
    <li>The leaves can have a distinctive shape, with round or elongated forms and potentially wavy edges.</li>
    <li> Its final size, coloration, and growth rate depend on the aquarium conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by cutting the rhizome into sections using clean, sharp scissors.</li>
    <li>Ensure each section has healthy roots and a few leaves or a growing tip for best results.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape or plant them (with the rhizome exposed) in the substrate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Boyan" is considered easy to moderate in care level.</li>
    <li>It is sensitive to sudden changes in water parameters, so aim for stability.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when the plant adjusts to a new environment, but it will usually recover and produce new growth from the rhizome if it remains healthy.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any dead, yellowing, or decaying leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra can be grown emersed (out of water) in humid environments like paludariums or terrariums.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 8, 300.00, NULL, 15);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (38, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" is a beautiful and popular variety of Bucephalandra, known for its small, elongated leaves with crinkled edges and colors ranging from deep green to reddish-brown or maroon. It adds a vibrant touch to any aquascape, particularly in nano tanks or as a midground plant. Like all Bucephalandra, it is native to Borneo and is an epiphyte, meaning it naturally grows on rocks and wood rather than being fully planted in substrate.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" can thrive in low to medium lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While it can tolerate low light, moderate lighting is often recommended to bring out its best colors and encourage faster growth.</li>
    <li>It''s important to be cautious with higher lighting levels, as Bucephalandra is a slow-growing plant and can be prone to algae buildup on its leaves.  Target a PAR (photosynthetically active radiation) level below 50 umols for easy management, though higher levels are tolerable in very clean and healthy tanks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" prefers stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 71-79°F (21-26°C), though it can handle a wider range of 70-82°F (21-28°C).</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-7.5. Some sources suggest it can tolerate a wider range of 5.0-8.0.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, mimicking its natural habitat in fast-moving streams.</li>
    <li>Clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes will help prevent melting and ensure healthy growth.</li>
    <li>Some hardness (GH/KH) is beneficial; a dGH of 5 or above is helpful.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly necessary but will significantly enhance growth rate and coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer like Easy Green can be beneficial.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer will ensure the plant receives the necessary nutrients.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot.</li>
    <li>You can attach it to hardscape elements like rocks or driftwood using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If you choose to plant it in the substrate, ensure the roots are covered but the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It features small, elongated leaves with crinkled edges.</li>
    <li>The coloration can range from deep green to reddish-brown or maroon.</li>
    <li>Leaves are generally 4-6 cm long and 2-4 cm wide, often with wavy edges.</li>
    <li>Many Bucephalandra varieties display tiny white dots on their submerged leaves, and sometimes a metallic blue color may appear.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Red Cherry" can be propagated by cutting the rhizome into sections.</li>
    <li>Each section should have healthy roots and a few leaves or a growing tip for best results.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape as you would with a new plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra is generally considered easy to care for, making "Red Cherry" a good choice for beginners and experienced aquarists.</li>
    <li>However, they are sensitive to drastic changes in water parameters, so aim for stability.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when introduced to a new environment, especially in uncycled tanks, but the plant usually recovers and produces new growth from the rhizome if the rhizome remains healthy.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, decaying, or melting leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial for distributing nutrients and preventing detritus buildup.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 15, 300.00, NULL, 14);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (39, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
