INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (40, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Bucephalandra "Catherine" Care Guide</title>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Bucephalandra "Catherine" Care Guide</h1>

<p>Bucephalandra "Catherine" (also known as Catherinae) is a diverse group of Bucephalandra varieties, including "Red" and "Green" forms, originating from Borneo. These plants are highly regarded in the aquarium hobby for their unique appearance, which often includes varying coloration and ruffled leaves. Like other Bucephalandra, "Catherine" varieties are slow-growing epiphytes that are relatively easy to care for.</p>

<h2>Lighting</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Catherine" varieties thrive in low to moderate lighting conditions.</li>
    <li>While low light is sufficient, providing moderate light can help bring out more vibrant coloration and encourage faster growth.</li>
    <li>Be mindful of high lighting levels, as Bucephalandra are slow growers and can be prone to algae buildup on their leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Water Parameters</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" prefers stable water conditions.</li>
    <li>Ideal temperature range: 70-82°F (21-28°C). Some sources suggest slightly cooler water (71-79°F / 21-26°C) for optimal health and to reduce stress and melting.</li>
    <li>pH range: 6.0-8.0.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial, as it mimics their natural habitat in streams with moderate to high flow.</li>
    <li>Maintain clean, well-filtered water with regular water changes to prevent the buildup of waste products.</li>
    <li>Some hardness (GH/KH) is helpful, with a dGH of 5 or above being recommended.</li>
</ul>

<h2>CO2 and Fertilization</h2>
<ul>
    <li>CO2 injection is not strictly necessary for survival, but it will significantly enhance growth and coloration.</li>
    <li>Bucephalandra primarily absorbs nutrients from the water column, so a liquid fertilizer is beneficial.</li>
    <li>Regular fertilization with a comprehensive liquid fertilizer will support healthy growth and vibrant colors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planting and Substrate</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" is an epiphytic plant and should not have its rhizome buried in the substrate, as this can lead to rot.</li>
    <li>Attach it to hardscape elements like rocks or driftwood using super glue (gel type recommended) or thread.</li>
    <li>If planting in the substrate, ensure only the roots are covered and the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>The leaves can vary in size and shape depending on the specific variety (e.g., "Mini", "Red", "Green").</li>
    <li>"Catherine Red" is known for its deep green to reddish leaves, often with a ruffled appearance, and may show tiny white dots and sometimes a faint metallic blue color on submerged leaves. </li>
    <li>"Catherine Green" features narrow, wavy, emerald green leaves.</li>
    <li>"Mini Catherine" has wavy leaves and short stalks, with a dark, olive green coloration when submerged.</li>
    <li>The final size, coloration, and growth rate are dependent on the aquarium conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" can be propagated by cutting the rhizome into sections.</li>
    <li>Each section should have healthy roots and a few leaves or a growing tip for best results.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape or plant them (with the rhizome exposed) in the substrate. Bare rhizomes can also produce new shoots if healthy and provided with good conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Catherine" is generally considered easy to care for, making it suitable for beginners.</li>
    <li>However, it is sensitive to drastic changes in water parameters, so maintaining stability is key.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when introduced to a new environment or with unstable parameters, but new growth will typically emerge from a healthy rhizome.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, decaying, or melting leaves to encourage new growth.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is crucial for distributing nutrients and preventing detritus buildup.</li>
    <li>Algae growth on the leaves can be a problem due to the slow growth rate and large surface area of the leaves, especially in tanks with strong lighting and unbalanced parameters.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>```', 0, 300.00, NULL, 17);
INSERT INTO public.core_productvariant (id, variant_type, description, stock, original_price, offer_price, product_id) VALUES (41, 'medium', '<!DOCTYPE html>
