    <li>If you choose to place it in the substrate, ensure only the roots are covered and the rhizome remains exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Growth and Appearance</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra "Pygmea" is a slow-growing plant.</li>
    <li>It typically reaches a size of 2-4 inches (5-10 cm) tall.</li>
    <li>Features wavy, green leaves that grow in a rosette formation.</li>
    <li>Can exhibit rich, dark green hues, and some varieties may have subtle red or brown undertones.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Propagation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Propagate by dividing the rhizome into sections.</li>
    <li>Each section should have healthy roots and at least one growing tip.</li>
    <li>Reattach these sections to hardscape as you would with a new plant.</li>
</ul>

<h2>General Tips</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Bucephalandra is generally considered easy to care for.</li>
    <li>Maintain stable water parameters to prevent melting.</li>
    <li>Melting can occur when introduced to a new environment, especially in uncycled tanks, but the plant usually recovers and produces new growth from the rhizome if the rhizome remains healthy.</li>
    <li>Regularly remove any yellowing, decaying, or melting leaves.</li>
    <li>Good water flow is beneficial.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>', 2, 300.00, NULL, 12);
